      <Placemark>
        <name>Arcus Augusti (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzoJjrpPSQYeP6srU1QrfKzENPMxXLm8zikmfvq-OG1q06U2dulA0p9pTrcNt_TLMCMNeE2TfIApYr6OuvoOdVLn_JiHBp5vZCftLhxWTcX4f3mEQZv4nc4S2O-_qtZNUTeMrS9r9Y5Keu_PAUWcQPlb1oJSD9TAdjqPsXK46dweTEAPF0uv1RC8WrVsi1L0ZesNQec-6Zrq-k?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Arch of Augustus, Rome.<br><br>This monumental arch carried the three canals of the Aqua Iulia, Aqua Marcia and Aqua Tepula over the Via Tiburtina in its high attics. The inscription on the topmost attic (which covered the specus of the Iulia) dates the arch to the Augustan restoration of the aqueducts between 11 and 5/4 BC. It was a single travertine arch that still survives intact, incorporated into the Porta Tiburtina of the Aurelian Wall; this gate later came to be known as porta Taurina on account of the bucranium on the Augustan arch. The arch is oriented along the axis of the “Via Collatina”, though the Via Tiburtina also passed through the arch. This concern to align the arch on axis with the “Via Collatina” resulted in a pronounced kink in the course of the aqueducts at this point.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1599-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
