      <Placemark>
        <name>Arcus Dolabellae et Silani (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzlSfxZ768RJfQZFay7mQPocTqXa0DGr0O3xvijvSJMCS3vtJWoLKif3zau9qEHp0z8ioeHPquzGqMZQcLPFEL_a7k3imgD1V8mct3sQNhptw6-5TjYp2OfRLvEbsSbtRwH6xabo-nFYSMDsrgN5ZK0d2joQdXSXURQY3Q0-CmnwTdyzyWRqFNT0m7ycOLVViRNvdYtA-R9KA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Arch of Dolabella & Silanus, Rome.<br><br>The Arch of Dolabella and Silanus, a single bay travertine arch (W. 4 m, H. 6.56 m), stands on the summit of the *Caelian at the upper end of the “Clivus Scauri” close to the present-day Church of S. Maria in Domnica. The arch is decorated with two plain cornices, and on its E attic façade an inscription dates it to 10 AD and indicates that it was built by the consuls P. Cornelius Dolabella and C. Iunius Silanus: P. CORNELIVS P.F. DOLABELLA C. IVNIVS C.F. SILANVS FLAMEN MARTIAL(IS) CO(N)S(VLES) EX S.C. FACIVNDVM CVRAVERVNT IDEMQVE PROBAVERVNT.<br><br>Formerly the arch was thought to have been part of the Aqua Marcia. Now, Colini’s hypothesis that the arch replaced the porta Caelimontana, one of the two known Caelian gates in the Servian Wall, is widely accepted. Colini observed that blocks of Grotta Oscura tufa, typical of the Servian Wall, were connected to the N pier of the Arch of Dolabella and Silanus, and suggested that in 10 AD this arch replaced the porta Caelimontana, which, following Livy, stood on the summit of the Caelian. Thus, the “Via Caelimontana” would have passed through the Servian Wall here, to continue within the city as the “Clivus Scauri”.<br><br>An inscription of 2 AD with the same formula and content as that of the Arch of Dolabella and Silanus marked the “Arcus Lentuli et Crispini”. The similarity of these two monuments may indicate an Augustan-era renovation of the Servian Gates from the Tiber to the Caelian. Under Nero, the Arch of Dolabella and Silanus was incorporated into the aqua Claudia.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1599-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
