      <Placemark>
        <name>Arcus Septimii Severi (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v52L7u93PfTzIJfW7kwx7sC46qGrPVwyTTpk8xkEoKO9mSpfQHDiWLoXLwc_x8ahrSS38S_-QpK5YkrB4dthvQiC33-3Bw_-ygnlev3jJHfttYoxKOTjNKTWoOcw4ZUyZftWrPyZXCdTf4ElCNxu34SefzL2x6Glm7ky9MCD9MANhh8e-aLATqHzNigsWDL5cRl8CH1wG97foBQv7iMmj8uW8yrYUeQLXU3nC6AihD1dFobNdSXybhcBl3YplKHmYZgQ9IkXR6MCwCFUEvr3JoH2BNPUG4nrx18SwsBFtfKr69EgZJ0Md2jgKgw5umh3pDZWmkuIlSN4aUARL61MYNZoM6WAnLyxAMoXEcsGUvrytwTI8QB6_H5yfFnfPUSHTMNPZpzlQnU9cUIC7qMxm5QPK9T6m70DZNUM3iw-yuWYyp0y5iifLHx-GMzIcfmFeOgLDKnENsJ_1VPFJegwS6w32L_l4BSwVZeMgKOt3pXMP5VTGgL9HGRsoKpVjt20gAh7lHn5MsBMEGUlmPv-3-X6uUSye-IM3anlNoerdUrRuI5l8H8o7dyvtTj6EByjKSei2YiRGa2NXSGsrPVri7-ExdHv3QPRPgnh8MvgY4AeUqUqFOyBUXzOvlzKJzriZgtEk6iJeuTXcK76fPPLS5ejbr5nSVuBhy7HP1AXylw27YMWOrvd6kFictXuQ2APpepgH_1H5lUeg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Arch of Septimius Severus, Rome, 3rd c. AD.<br><br>At the northwest end of the Roman Forum is a white marble triumphal arch dedicated in 203 AD to commemorate the Parthian victories of Emperor Septimius Severus and his two sons, Caracalla and Geta, in the two campaigns against the Parthians of 194/195 and 197–199.<br><br>The arch was raised on a travertine base originally approached by steps from the Forum's ancient level. The central archway, spanned by a richly coffered semicircular vault, has lateral openings to each side archway, a feature copied in many Early Modern triumphal arches. The Arch is about 23 meters in height, 25 meters in width. The arch bears two sets of reliefs. The first set includes four large panels on each face of the attic and the second set consists of eight panels that are set into the inner face of the four archways.<br><br>The three archways rest on piers, in front of which are detached composite columns on pedestals. Winged Victories are carved in relief in the spandrels. A staircase in the south pier leads to the top of the monument, on which were statues of the emperor and his two sons in a four-horse chariot (quadriga), accompanied by soldiers.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7hiB5Nqd3noATIzNFytLK0d4Kr3f2kYJHtZ8CbsuUqd1h1sAjs_LXtkr_4LG-XoA822GDh_x3Tpw7ie7e2he3ZigxExR_Lt2pKylJFel1WXkrbBk_zmPedcuWKElIDgOrzCItf7FW3-p3WmZpsiUClEjLvOIysZaDwnjEg5XXSGzUeDlRb3kvCbT8flVul7JhyVViiTSU7Rceh2IotiwO7ABcRGqXdM0YcA6tB-l_Rjawk_qRR2bNRaWhFf5EXnv_0zOlCSxOtDfuBWuRc7TLpU60FdCx9OKBMONx0K-AouEuA_sKRomJ1xvGeyRBs8wTvwdXgFSHDr-rmMJ5ezpuM_xAlqcusvD8SZhhy_FoEgQgBTfwUv2GwqIvLQny19QBLxW-p9kxTsjJ1YCGNCy-0RU2Ib-FvFgL9lyP8lHLdSxoCv281NHpZoZrw3N8dR4URo6ppRBd-ip84XZELCIS2829ezrreWvtVkzk-inbOtxGo6CES3MAAQ5YL1XTVrNo25MjIw9Zx_fRHofI4LyCL5Bbtw9wxO4ec8G_1EBKdyMSSMSjXZViYcyoQzDtDcoqTg9rsWei43Kncv96Y16XG7n4krd4eIWSC3ksI_R3BtAxowKmcoTzd1ol-8BQi1x3_4QZCDHQ-wGfkxu2fACeI78_cbxKFS9EA3ttTn8KkGinqoZJBDnt6ETQF0n0uZdfki-hlmpH-MA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxqDpNKF0VQ1AlSD85JgluVat3iI9iHTD4veM2vsl-Tjd2vRjCfnbwEOdwkQj4kzbmVgxvpChQ90Dl0fDDhSuEtPu6_412eirAJXI31eNLJl4Cov7I1xU387ZpEwM7jR4piRT7H_DUKEnEKmrog_ucKe6WkPW_gQZB87ZRR_6-8fQp8VX1RkwYMU99HwdYKHipm7JLQWf2Nrg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
