      <Placemark>
        <name>Asylum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxV3Yc6_MOKryOz1SilUC867KsmisHeMKB_Wfa0ENu43KPEhs2X63LVtmk6LAqj5i9ZHzkcGoN0_xbxmS0Mh_RvWpv7ZvoddkLJgB7-35xc4bWmf17s5-46pFGVXlw5eelzp5DIOqbbKU6viwD3X4Dd-m26Ft8raD9eH9E6w9fW0yEJUB_JYcS6I63-yrSxPD9_B15u8X0aCg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Rome.<br><br>The area remembered as the site of the sanctuary established by Romulus to attract new settlers stood on the saddle between the N and S peaks of the Capitol, inter Arcem et Capitolium, ‘between the Arx and the Capitolium’, in the area ‘between the two groves’, inter duos lucos. It was thought that the summits of the Capitol were once wooded. The Asylum is traditionally placed in Piazza del Campidoglio. More recent scholarship has noted that the expressions, ‘between the two groves’ and ‘between the Arx and the Capitolium’, are also used to describe the location of the Temple of Veiovis, which was nestled in the SW corner of the Tabularium. The Asylum was originally surrounded by a high stone wall, and in the Augustan period it was still marked off as a locus saeptus, ‘an enclosed space’. This delineated sacred area which stood between the two groves was itself a grove, lucus Asyli. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1834-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxV3Yc6_MOKryOz1SilUC867KsmisHeMKB_Wfa0ENu43KPEhs2X63LVtmk6LAqj5i9ZHzkcGoN0_xbxmS0Mh_RvWpv7ZvoddkLJgB7-35xc4bWmf17s5-46pFGVXlw5eelzp5DIOqbbKU6viwD3X4Dd-m26Ft8raD9eH9E6w9fW0yEJUB_JYcS6I63-yrSxPD9_B15u8X0aCg?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
