      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Balneum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Private Baths, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a private Republican bath-house, consisting of a circular room with two semicircular niches, are preserved in the cellar of no. 111 Via Sistina, on the S slope of the Collis Hortulorum. The mosaic decoration, wall-paintings and opus incertum construction date the building to c. 70 BC; its shape, size, fixtures and decorative motifs suggest it served as part of a private balneum, possibly associated with a residential villa.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1701-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.48688,41.904735,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Basilica Aemilia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5wxLdSYFl45TyJPJVtBS3PvB3Wt0HgSSOsfb0kAQgyLFVF_-Hr3jA4Ym4oBRjbHQ0j1HKuYDN34prwLrnt_8pDtjZcnAQFnNPfEEBqZllN65U_FnqJuRavHmT1jnfTSl3FpLUZZTvauWV-85A7jVglyyUHp0m3HGBoX1YIS3MvM_9oRTjx6Lm8BCSAgE2wCdZtaMwlfdrqyZbwV92jCsPvNbsHYEQ4PRgl3_Nr-4So7RQ-IWYQJK-X-Geexh7zk1rKFXmLteRgIU4IhV77tnSNxcTKkv5KmWUoyq5s5UqqUktiKfdn4SadwcxfLBckwcP9wot5EtEdyyY5g-QTh21GnIQcH9oofonpp4Ip8wjG4wFaQ6UZU6VERZV4FN_4y-ufMlQf-nQX5E5y4GGTQjJr-rL9cup2xhDsxY4VVJ7p9dtcU6ITCOA8bIQyRUDAkd7xba1LL9QYUifzMIFPzXOXFfq8R9DwqM6N7UqDPl7VQoQdbjnyg0aqGcCNvCqsYIH-OYE__PCEjZPA8eQ3dPyFfEfnassFzxzUZLoLIoQhuIlbkYOf1Kup3j5SIMBHPqDC8erQnzTKmo0zNUdz-hflUrSILlf5tdTuJxQdwjX3VK8aa_XzOCUqRbjKBIFucIyh4LYPasGT-S--HFeXywAQ5xyOINBuoFKFS5SLThzgOGMyePcY588AF2f-dw1b52ZbAT-lwlMzdg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Rome.<br><br>The basilica along the N side of the Forum, opposite the Basilica Iulia was rebuilt c. 54 BC by L. Aemilius Paullus to replace the Basilica Fulvia of 179 BC, and referred to after 54 BC as Basilica Paulli. The rebuilt basilica of 54 BC, which maintained the axis of the Basilica Fulvia and its dimensions to the S toward the Forum and to the N toward the Macellum, while shortening its extension along its E–W axis, was dedicated in 34 BC.by Paullus’ son, L. Aemilius Paullus Lepidus. This basilica burned in 14 BC and was restored by Augustus and the friends of Paullus; it was restored again c. 22 AD by M. Aemilius Lepidus.<br><br>In front of the basilica ran the Porticus Gaii et Luci, a series of 15 shops (tabernae) and a two-storey arcaded portico which effectively shielded the Basilica Paulli from the Forum. Excavations have revealed most of the ground-plan of the two structures, segments of which are represented on the Severan Marble Plan.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4tJvnPCaEsZCLZoXUmPFeEELKSYrHJr4rIXU_hEbykrKVRmrcyk95111hz-gG5-OUeG2IwFdrb8QLJxDzhM4c30CCI1HB7dqXX1xr66rqC3QGN7ihmTsuS1RjapLswyY9fLVjxyOfW_0xDXijxY2u40RBsyi-POWAn5R-hnHUAdNbgedkW44ZF-TMASg3nE3olU1rEnNoikOPkviV2HPil_Kf7EUpAF0ivgrxXmfQBCnF-2GesmyK0wW1BOdDZ7eRWZ69OTmFZRdhezB2JPcvc25QRoiOtLSRagxf6gW1v3VEIb9b7bDcGEel3pslE2h0JeOxMm6tgaC_GzGpu0PKR6V6ozoJ5oNaUEbctf3RbjzIl1RgTDYbV9ZcEeSAM8EiBlVJO78mZlHmYPWWjMf3khAw-T8kjRTJVVz3wq7OKW1SzJZVHAfZbqbAzLiW9-MNJiafooiwSUOaosCzSHQDt1gik2JAcQ-b-cKWexI4NfhcQsSxigLyqeB2bq99Ffd771Hv8bGkd61XgGVL0d1FLv1xeuLuryIWHkeqMLM8--_JTXX7_9lsmtXp1mT6pUudidx5LI-wkkMY6_V30t7vzq1HHPa6ua-gOJOyiAMDbbYQpxELr5btt0ymPyzfO0MURyAmc0llKQJgQlVLUQ3J0KsqTJn7NVAWfydtGJkeUb4WoDjvFTeqH__gTAdTQTi82EX8z4hM9ZQ" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
