      <Placemark>
        <name>Cloaca Circi Maximi (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwR6a-eoPBaWb7RygfV7vEw606ElLVt0hYTsjDOsr6AiWXhtObL7zn8Ok9Tr1fxH9HJPICKcPNXLeStgUfwjCaJ8y-SJNXP0DrZ23Q6pCve7ibT36xBcH2BKNBdZPGXzaQYM888Ppv27g0cN7zj7dnVRrFrKwhp9Ksh7f3c7sf8KsOu-CRkWLH1BuEMYEV1Vu7QzzdgkJgj4w?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Sewer, Rome. <br><br>One of Rome’s major drains, the “Cloaca Circi Maximi” ran through the Circus Maximus and the Forum Bovarium, to discharge into the Tiber; it collected waters from the valleys of the Circus Maximus, Via Appia and the Colosseum. The drain must have been installed at an early date, since it was necessary to make the swampy lowlands of the Circus Maximus valley suitable for racing; most believe the sewer followed the path of an ancient watercourse. <br><br>Archaeological evidence for the drain is scarce. Brief traces of an opus quadratum drain were recovered at the base of the Caelian, near the foot of the “Clivus Scauri”; these, as well as sewers running below the Via di S. Gregorio and the intra-urban continuation of the Via Appia, probably connected to a central drain that ran beneath the Circus proper. Though recent excavations in the Circus Maximus sought to locate and reactivate the drain (in hopes of lowering the restrictively high water-table in the region), their efforts have not yet borne fruit. <br><br> In the Augustan period the Circus’ euripus (water channel) flowed along the edge of the stands and almost certainly utilized the cloaca as a drain, and perhaps even as a source of fresh water. The best preserved segment of the “Cloaca Circi Maximi” lies next to S. Maria in Cosmedin. Partially explored in the late 19th c., the remains suggest that the “Cloaca Circi Maximi” was engineered at a scale similar to the Cloaca Maxima prior to the reorganization and raising of the Forum Bovarium in the late 3rd to early 2nd c. BC.<br><br> The discharge of the “Cloaca Circi Maximi” is preserved in the 19th-c. Tiber embankments just S of the mouth of the Cloaca Maxima. Thus, the course of the drain is sufficiently understood to map it from the Tiber through the Forum Bovarium, but its path through the Circus is too hypothetical to render. As the drain’s ancient name, if indeed it had one, is lost, the modern label “Cloaca Circi Maximi” has been employed here.]]></description>
