      <Placemark>
        <name>Fornix Fabiani (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzM4zJuDX83YvVZiDTCzQwOlB4CVs-FJ0dFaT0uYHiBHj5fWWehiMCff3mXR-EsMBGDwn2uxIX7seOYEm1599SxuG4fcPjgo-FMEax1Fqp80TvRcopmtv9HDxY4Nvzhx47EcqQWMQVy3eMjdLnvdT3b2Y3x3v2p2fAkuc4eJGHajkjzSz9vtpij_44MR_l0FHN9uJG33PFi8A?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Arch of Fabianus, Rome.<br><br>An arch at the E end of the Forum erected by Q. Fabius Maximus Allobrogicus in 121 BC to commemorate a triumph over the Allobroges, then restored by Fabius’ grandson in 57 BC.<br><br>The arch was located at the entrance to the Forum on the Sacra Via, but the precise location is much debated. Sources describe the Fornix Fabianus as ‘next to the Regia’, ‘next to the stone wellhead of Scribonius Libo which is in the Porticus Iulia’, and ‘before the Sacra Via between the Temple of Faustina and the Temple of Vesta’. Scholars have located the arch both to the S and to the N of the Regia, but an emerging consensus favours a location to the N, mainly on the basis of recent excavations and Coarelli’s persuasive analysis of the literary evidence.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GypXvMAUxAPTanTdCbs4nHxPX519dFAeYoitsXcdOHKQ1xoJNqTg5P0vdvmvPCzmys5HhxdwufGf7GP5a_ZntALovVJAkmijYFrujxb5335qK2brzF8HoTxOZpaDQ1kHwuH66PmhdiXTMwATjFggt_e0c6hHuXP3ic6SkCeX21_bP50leBkmqj6rhWsqAzlVfeovTLPhEbRCQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
