      <Placemark>
        <name>Forum Augusti (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6NhYTwuh6m4vyhbzVJLmrW5SBIPyenKDKHh42R3rHhD93Hg5XRtJyWzm-ySw2N4NWLV_4I2j4qhEt2cCYpZsKzCp-lo_LaZlU_fcwzS8QK40xwHqq77dhhgwCZPTP_vZM04A72BeM176Y6eGj5qL1moph_T5EACIcj9Oax_iWEDaY-0lmrvZKEjCyTtgpjFP31SnTLjzgGxouBXCIlokZwSFdU2juwPrm4Bx3ac8aGYw3SxihL4o4rH8xKFLd6I-ZK4T0rT6Ja34z6H1qR2bKdE9jEKl8f3V5AnnYNiGeTCxZiWfSLGVG_vcwo-16WYNYCVHGxphfpIi-h_8EpiAqzGN0UHToMo44_5Eu8o99EwSODQDjE4o71KglhuLfd_b16x_NSfJYmqHrPQqq4fJDZMCIhQYqG07_MUb95aEMrWYKvCBhzt1WiX0KWPPcu_SdVdkY5PmWZ0IxrWxx4Ygh0WP_Q19Xq2yVFjTobykDBLR0CmzWE0CNEiv3hyaOlf4RgegEQVpTB2c1227ki3wFJchbwYbRAp9ftlWCh_JZX2kD7asSSReTeXilt7nfRNuXRC9MjjlRoDwkmevPhPpas5_3Qgz0pIYeB6GLMCy8fERX0orKt5iqJAT3BM8n7HGPOwKqOHezuGdDV_PxGVwl85gxnuXPkICPTs_vSyQQI8hc3jan7DnO-Yq-Ui-Kdhfy1HR4kNQa4GA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Forum of Augustus, Rome.<br><br>The Forum of Augustus and Temple of Mars Ultor, dedicated in 2 BC, comprised one of the most magnificent of Augustus’ building projects in Rome, and is noted for its programmatic sculpture and refined architectural design; for Pliny, the Forum Augusti was among the most beautiful of the city’s architectural wonders. The architectural form of the forum derived in large part from the Forum Iulium and the Forum Romanum: a paved, open square (125 x 90 m), flanked by marble colonnades and dominated on a short side by a temple, here the gleaming Temple of Mars Ultor whose Luna marble Corinthian columns rose from a high podium at the forum’s N end. Long porticoes with columns of colored marble defined the E and W sides of the forum, and at their N ends the rear walls bowed out to form two broad hemicycles, housing a series of portrait statues emphasizing the lineage of Augustus: one featured men of the Julian clan, the other the summi viri of Rome. At the far N end of the W portico is the “Sala del Colosso”; this room was lavishly decorated with colored marbles and featured a colossal statue, perhaps of Augustus. Prevailing opinion places the main entrance of the Forum Augusti on its short S side, which would require the forum to be accessed through the Forum Iulium, and a recent hypothesis restores a third portico along this unexcavated border (La Rocca 171, with caution); these hypothetical features have not been depicted on our rendering. Though adjacent to, and clearly influenced by, the Forum of Julius Caesar, the physical and programmatic relationships between Augustus’ forum and that of Caesar await further clarification.<br><br>Numerous activities were held in the Forum Augusti including senate votes, religious rituals and, especially, decisions involving foreign policy. Portions of the forum may also have been used by the praefectus urbi for criminal trials.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5rDCn3TeCbOyksjyZfG2h-NjsHfGn9dDExICZtMAhCaOu7IQoSo7bj5uIQV_YbHYsGx8oKS40nV3dxNP_X25RRNMTnuTmcrPyJ4mqh4jIcjkOCGbdFShk5nzvU0kU66QotBNG_SqafQzC21mVkko_NEW1wKIJ1_35JATd05CfySSGZnkrIQ1VzDDSnbLbFpiM7wxSGHrG4wNXH4PKP1MRRGZpQS7pn4grA0BhmC_7GXXrA9wNc9GYNfWSMjJdhH4oRERIOpHtEgUAaaRb6loqoBJrRRYkbDmpY1xSDERqXrJF_C6TN7XcRJYzXzTdJh2LZyWUXbEQBPMnDULOiCnsxcnaeWN3EVWSCj84vcLP6Ag87R2VLd9otvLNbLMycnMY2huzYk3zbt9bjUVVZ32KG-TMtWKKMLOH4MyCJ4BPhIb092S4PxB_iMa7ND4OSEAputshGtJpFJ61GKaUck21T8wHRLABs2zjTb3yGTpsMOGaSRiDViI_vQLVlfaG28Gdr3tI1Nd6J084ITRfugHZLxRBU9OGkmvYOqk6BpS9Hw-ydtR-XFSiOtCUcX3zwu-T6HeopgrekH7stLPU0N7EnCFW12dl_cqAi19M9nMkpjAl-3YMvU9Li9MWegsvDTj3tJZbIfa5Zh8kXhR60C-n2zZwNByCli0Rnnr0S6l27yreh_14YE3DD8CqPqBJf57jDgtKSU-jlCA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5qIqT8eLorNe2oawBb8WWV2PnGEa96Fijd3CA3ZEtNBV8I3sF9o0UbKMmVcrYpXrZpmDr1MNxcYCSMp-2hvuWLaRD03Q5Fq33uwyyXm4U70jYW88G5JPkaTz8Y6MZI-_fIKiY3GmTMLVWZUhTtlz9nN3t3D_R__XLPjHveQXULFHrnu2d0AGWa7ccHevokRKuLjmZ1IiUHpcmcMOvzlRHWFVL-ExkyeLCoWhrn8Vxu3TnygG8YhHWGDj5bu5m6UEW2yvVN37rZGQLQIHmF2OpKzplNyy5zOJxBxim-iHxAbojHND9whB93EhNenDKuqhMg2IQnFRDvzh_ohnh63svSOB4oTVYTw7eXtfYaakneSA-