      <Placemark>
        <name>Pons Cestius (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5LXnfE1ZlXMCIvQ02GPf5zpT74LlBJyw30_TP7_UMnWskLfecl6kAFSxu3K4vROsP6pDi5pbdu4a_iDY8f6vsjg9ZLAQDQPyNyTUr9bYP2K6yfurIlPPOQV2GMotQxZL_Avr6NS2RW3UKrQVLul0JttZHB9rQpaM42anX1o6HIoQR2SpQDABrBWJGG3dGs_b-4Zi4sXQA_8Kmh3KfThDD97do-x0kOh7EOGBCYAqyr7VWiasLUjL2iu8fMsUsVdivpIMWZob8TAMJ0df39S0_8AtxWiW_qzhacIZEjaMh9B0yEbDNSwPfQmXvzOhH-V2IlHm1UlM-dJ55MSU57EtcD0Rop-eUF32yKfeYu3RjbTTAsl9ws6mf1mNSJPy7_ltGql11F0xgVzb2eRFihI65UK9a1JIfRELgv_bLCEewDn1VBbb1n3hyZGyi4uXHBQlSrAuNYt9b6gpQr1s0IuAmf9u1BttjTA5uv1QtI9oUd0ed2Kp11YDCRyeVzY_qf35hoLnVPGyHNkOyaSE-PZ5_A1t8a2Gq3c-sWzxJdcuKSM4YRDKy-eQtoMUib6PfTzl0SJzypQ4KmurfK-hvrwR9mg_Ot5yEUU-2l57LkP-inx1V3vRLO8jkz8nMZgZMY3KB_MBpY2p68ksjrgz8U_tm5rJEneP8Wo-fRvXdueQMkqfsQFIYLVD9t3mYUfmF_1U3vdldKCIa_UQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Bridge of Cestius , Rome.<br><br>The stone bridge that connected the Tiber island to Trans Tiberim. <br><br>General scholarly opinion attributes its construction to members of the late-Republican Cestius clan, most possibly C. Cestius, builder of the Pyramid of Cestius, praetor in 44 BC, or L. Cestius, praetor of the following year, suggesting a construction date between 49 and 43 BC. Even though it is not known whether Cestius’ construction was an original building or a restoration, the stone bridge should be considered within the historical context of the wide-scale building activity on the island at that period.<br><br> The modern bridge, Ponte S. Bartolomeo, that stands at its site today is a late 19th-c. rebuilding of the Pons Cestius. The ancient structure, which was partly incorporated in the central arch of the modern bridge, must belong to the 4th-c. AD rebuilding of the pons, which then was dedicated as the PONS GRATIANI. The ancient bridge was much shorter than the modern one (L. 48.50 m, W. 8 m) and rested on a single arch.]]></description>
