      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Capena (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwOx_UdmrhGreHlYLXGp0Y_xdphKD39gQZD0ubFji0CukVy1OHmwSjhVAswDkSCBowLUmTj7sIUJtPdxOvCv6hQJ3L5T6Cj0jDt3ib8RJFAHw_4I_0SPpHprEUn2h2d8n7szvRNXc2Hr3h4ZB-WtEug4P22tI8MFJ-hTy7MhumRZ1VuU5JHuY6i7_ALd-Bf03sZz7F93O-77w?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>Gate in the Servian Wall from which the Via Appia departed, situated by a natural depression between the Caelian and the Aventine. <br><br>The gate is mentioned in connection with events as early as the years 484 BC and 459 BC. Parker and Gori’s excavations in 1867 identified the gate, which probably consisted of a single arch. Architectural fragments in tufa and travertine indicate the last century of the Republic as their terminus post quem.<br><br>The name of the gate may have derived from either the city of Capena or Capua, or from the sanctuary of the Camenae which was located in close proximity to the gate. Coarelli refutes all three alternatives to propose the city of Cabum on the mons Albanus, the original destination of the Via Latina, as the etymological origin of Capena.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwOx_UdmrhGreHlYLXGp0Y_xdphKD39gQZD0ubFji0CukVy1OHmwSjhVAswDkSCBowLUmTj7sIUJtPdxOvCv6hQJ3L5T6Cj0jDt3ib8RJFAHw_4I_0SPpHprEUn2h2d8n7szvRNXc2Hr3h4ZB-WtEug4P22tI8MFJ-hTy7MhumRZ1VuU5JHuY6i7_ALd-Bf03sZz7F93O-77w?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
