      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Catularia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>Gate in the Servian Wall on the W tip of the S Capitol. Its only mention in a canonical ancient source is in Festus. <br><br>Coarelli places the Porta Catularia on the W tip of the Area Capitolina. The remains of a marble arch and steps leading up to the summit were observed in 1524 by Marliani behind the now-destroyed Church of S. Andrea in Vincis, c. 100 m NE of the Theatrum Marcelli. Festus states that the Porta Catularia, ‘The Gate of the Little Puppies’, was associated with the sacrifice of dogs. Puppies were sacrificed because of their purity. It is a coincidence that the Lutatii Catuli bore the cognomen ‘puppy’ (catulus).]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.480642,41.892431,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Collina (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzJp12BC1OyVj77RcChrBEGvZqyzZf3tunjQF7GktVc323_3iu38EJDeonNz3Hm91JQE3ALXRDfFT_skUFIZK6TCk_GiSioPYDU4i49wUalmA41aNcOYLnKtCIqetR3Sob89vhK5ceNjw79XvgmIUZaZMX5UjFRVjwYNQelc9nqly4UTfSg_4HfTNGzr0KHS44cj-1XEbUGkw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>Northernmost gate in the Servian Wall, located on the Quirinal. Leading up to the gate is the Alta Semita, which branches into the Via Salaria and Via Nomentana just outside the wall. <br><br>Remains of the gate were first unearthed in 1872 and re-explored in 1996. The gate’s two long walls flanked the street and converged toward the city. They were reinforced by two bastions. The Agger extended on both sides of the gate, though significantly reduced in size to the N. The origin of the name ‘Porta Collina’ is debated; Coarelli derives it from the collis Quirinalis, while Richardson evolves it from Regio Collina, one of the four divisions of the Republican city. The gate guarded the N approach to the city and was often under attack.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzJp12BC1OyVj77RcChrBEGvZqyzZf3tunjQF7GktVc323_3iu38EJDeonNz3Hm91JQE3ALXRDfFT_skUFIZK6TCk_GiSioPYDU4i49wUalmA41aNcOYLnKtCIqetR3Sob89vhK5ceNjw79XvgmIUZaZMX5UjFRVjwYNQelc9nqly4UTfSg_4HfTNGzr0KHS44cj-1XEbUGkw?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
