      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Fontinalis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gy44JkVpVv5iuupexLjnXcK35mrR_BoipVFNkIf7GXad5wyJh8JC5HaqtyvwqDpnB9iCSZCj6iLcz6Byn0t7_9CWnV6tpfRz1np40UiCS9HR0buzAudsIFMngfTh8hberzcCEEr182hrdt58uYKtiDRGsNLc35Us1ah9zJlaRXdQMh1PcbP93xHYRxZPMKM6GraKzP1leFyvA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>A gate in the Servian Wall, which common agreement now places on the saddle between the Arx and the Quirinal. This was the terminus of the Via Flaminia, which became the “Clivus Argentarius” inside the city walls. Its position is defined by Livy, who mentions the Porticus Aemilia (Campus Martius) that was built in 193 BC to connect this gate with the ara of Mars in the Campus Martius. It follows that the gate stood at some point where the ground immediately outside the wall was level enough to accommodate this portico. A spring from which the porta may have taken its name rises nearby under the Carcer.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gy44JkVpVv5iuupexLjnXcK35mrR_BoipVFNkIf7GXad5wyJh8JC5HaqtyvwqDpnB9iCSZCj6iLcz6Byn0t7_9CWnV6tpfRz1np40UiCS9HR0buzAudsIFMngfTh8hberzcCEEr182hrdt58uYKtiDRGsNLc35Us1ah9zJlaRXdQMh1PcbP93xHYRxZPMKM6GraKzP1leFyvA?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
