      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Mugionia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzcKoEmbNRcTU4rq9vxKXB9H0putmV1PUY4D364xIN5j5zLj9D2pkW9_R68AJRSu_WFVmbZ0reb1NyRuh9YCFRUdcJKU31cbd45yb_clxk4ri9-gya6NXhbH2Z7BdM57EaGFf7cXKf3dmTQcTUIrADk8Np2CRPevPxBkSr51nyXvnYrRqNCwL9qnjco_Yib2kmUhtpkiTguKw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Romulean Walls. Rome.<br><br>Gate in the Romulean city wall on the N slope of the Palatine, considered ancient even in antiquity. <br><br>Literary sources associate the gate with the Temple of Iuppiter Stator and the summa Nova via, ‘heights of the Nova Via, so it has traditionally been located near the Arch of Titus. <br><br>Recent excavations by Carandini have unearthed remains identified with the Romulean city wall and the Porta Mugionia S of the Sacra Via between the Domus Publica and Domus of M. Aemilius Scaurus; this identification, however, is far from secure. Further, the physical presence of this gate in the Augustan period is unknown; though Terrenato suggests that it stood, in some form, through the Republican period, the Romulean wall itself had been demolished by the late 6th c. BC. Thus it remains a strong possibility that under Augustus ‘Porta Mugionia’ was only a toponym. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzcKoEmbNRcTU4rq9vxKXB9H0putmV1PUY4D364xIN5j5zLj9D2pkW9_R68AJRSu_WFVmbZ0reb1NyRuh9YCFRUdcJKU31cbd45yb_clxk4ri9-gya6NXhbH2Z7BdM57EaGFf7cXKf3dmTQcTUIrADk8Np2CRPevPxBkSr51nyXvnYrRqNCwL9qnjco_Yib2kmUhtpkiTguKw?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
