      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Naevia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwWYhgvS_L3Xsua64Im0QA87ZSPw9DVGAmSgx1Wysx4TvB08MT00dnrUpvtH2agRG90jLHAOp5N4Fkxwl3Xu7U38rOnLafKuYMgApkxvRMZBJc7kTjWEJqgStvUpBFVJGWL3JPU1EpnAaG4JTwHlfFsokVzfc9QG0V47yyqOVHvV5llrd93O6oMHDQrj_MgcyQBT2iMmKbH4A?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>An unidentified and extremely old gate in the Servian Wall on the Aventine, known only from textual sources, especially Varro’s catalogue of gates; its name was taken from the ‘Naevian woods’ which ranged somewhere in that area. The general location of the gate is indicated by an eponymous neighborhood, the vicus porta Naevia, listed in Regio XII on the Capitoline base.<br><br>With the additional testimony of Varro’s list, in which the gate comes before the Porta Rauduscula and the Porta Lavernalis, the Porta Naevia may be placed on the SE summit of the Aventine at the point where the Via Ardeatina is, traditionally, supposed to have issued from the city.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwWYhgvS_L3Xsua64Im0QA87ZSPw9DVGAmSgx1Wysx4TvB08MT00dnrUpvtH2agRG90jLHAOp5N4Fkxwl3Xu7U38rOnLafKuYMgApkxvRMZBJc7kTjWEJqgStvUpBFVJGWL3JPU1EpnAaG4JTwHlfFsokVzfc9QG0V47yyqOVHvV5llrd93O6oMHDQrj_MgcyQBT2iMmKbH4A?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
