      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Querquetulana (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GznA4Q8LVyt442G2a7rw_dYwXnLvCutS3R7p5qjEWLg5n_sltDgiSWS329kr3n8-v_-vozUMEg5vZKDkFyHz4mhmjEfbecWH4ReC-LKPqjlNyC4XrnzzfMwkZHAaHBqoDGWakSBNOfzIhxSWL93jrySDIRIx41vbE0wsxAl04rejveSVmCOkzM2gb-0srO8ZpGnqDWtTjJ0Kg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>The Porta Querquetulana was one of two known gates in the Servian Wall on the Caelian. It stood between the Porta Caelimontana and Porta Esquilina. The name ‘Porta Querquetulana’ is derived from a grove or wood of oaks that stood just inside the gate. <br><br>In this grove was a shrine, the Larum Querquetulanum sacellum, which stood on the Esquiline. Some place the gate in the valley between the Caelian and the Oppian, but there is no evidence of a Republican street here. The gate must have stood on the crest of the Caelian on the “Via Tusculana”, the road which led SE from the Colosseum valley to the “Via Caelimontana”. As for its lateral E–W position, most scholars place it near the Church of SS. Quattro Coronati. Some place it on the site of the mediaeval Arco di Basile, a monumental aqueduct arch which served as the entrance to the Lateran precinct until demolished in 1604. However, the discovery of an extra-mural Republican cemetery zone just to the W of the Arco di Basile makes it almost certain that the wall and the gate are both to be found in the area of SS. Quattro Coronati.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GznA4Q8LVyt442G2a7rw_dYwXnLvCutS3R7p5qjEWLg5n_sltDgiSWS329kr3n8-v_-vozUMEg5vZKDkFyHz4mhmjEfbecWH4ReC-LKPqjlNyC4XrnzzfMwkZHAaHBqoDGWakSBNOfzIhxSWL93jrySDIRIx41vbE0wsxAl04rejveSVmCOkzM2gb-0srO8ZpGnqDWtTjJ0Kg?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
