      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Rauduscula (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzRhK_0lZbXl2d7uIadN6cfzSPaedOVoyKXizAmOf9a0uQ-mdYCr6Pmf6Qax3D6_TGmCqpA2scmhfVaCnYT3Fup2rQgooKxGzOxFMyGmE91VXYIYJV0FAVmywgnQKNFwvoNbfMpRUfqBytXf8sgxp6TpwKQl6WHWuqJz8Mp3Oj55aQ6nnt7g2gDhlButMfVfEmCj_AzoQxEng?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>Gate in the Servian Wall on the Aventine without identified remains but known from textual sources, especially Varro’s catalogue of gates; in various ways, these sources derive the gate’s name from raudus, ‘copper or bronze’. Since the Capitoline base situates the neighborhood of the vicus portae R(a)udusculanae in Regio XII and, in addition, Varro’s catalogue lists the gate between the Porta Naevia and the Porta Lavernalis, there is general agreement that the Porta Rauduscula marked the intersection of the Servian Wall with a Republican street likely to have followed the central depression of the Aventine. While the existence of a gate is plausible, its identification remains entirely hypothetical.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzRhK_0lZbXl2d7uIadN6cfzSPaedOVoyKXizAmOf9a0uQ-mdYCr6Pmf6Qax3D6_TGmCqpA2scmhfVaCnYT3Fup2rQgooKxGzOxFMyGmE91VXYIYJV0FAVmywgnQKNFwvoNbfMpRUfqBytXf8sgxp6TpwKQl6WHWuqJz8Mp3Oj55aQ6nnt7g2gDhlButMfVfEmCj_AzoQxEng?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
