      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Salutaris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwiCokAqapV5W42TrAATk0xfRLf3Z_o2-3DLOhdfuEwI41vogWnKEXfJXjRogLPtJmw6BVxM3sgZwoVIynreRx3g0qnirrz-6QimxKgOFqyt1J603bi4weJTx7UIC0JMvGu4ccel_mBxeATpGV7YgcCWYLEv0j9WTj3UxHhMw6gyc9sgEiwSnLg9DnOECIy1h7O40niJkebjQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>Gate in the Servian Wall located along the W edge of the Quirinal, at the top of a natural depression near Via della Dataria; no remains are extant. <br><br>The name of this gate derives from the Temple of Salus, and the likelihood of this site is reinforced by the nearby Compitum: Vicus Salutis. The road passing through the gate, alternatively called the Vicus Salutaris or considered an extension of the Alta Semita, linked the Campus Martius and the Quirinal.]]></description>
