      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Porta Triumphalis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4SjYGGNGvfD9OrnmZaeQBZJc0bYrV2WyKy4AWGeO0XSTn5IH8Yk_FjxIBS4YYmgpRKlNDXsCdwlsnDvjl_p_h20eqnETNEU43fINbCLWmhd9BYkORXqmYpRxK_CNzOqEhy23ZR2ydzj9U1SSHhRAvvqsyVitvHPZBZO3Ez-SNaLop4UqNSn1beNlcZBzLJfpaE32Yhwv_H2mE23RT84o4eau1KjjGv_9EBE9CRxLNnpo_tqwRoKmib8E07wVN3E43Ok8zZXKsSDQ3XOF7nzdYF0u0M3ySB8j3uO6sa4HYIiZkO-RAKjQcgFTEWeMWv3f0MgY2zoqVFA_BuZTNKrQjG1W3LlKC5u79CGSKt2RixDC20SyJWB5B0kFA6uGR8YWHIqEhdmhjIm72HZk-bKn_-gy2VSC0ur2YIIHNx5SYKbHL4Z01WlNzoaUWwu0kxgcoydS6Y907SXP2Rxa0Vqt2bLU9OKv87yhvNPMnBVRP0A4McUNOBjm_Tg9sX8jYTh3V1vIziqaZsD2eEDR1wf4S_NdR1hL5A15xi5dqLpmGRckffIssg_inYSYzJ2PNGSNN07xDFXLsa1U_shInJhp1ZTKlKD9aiJPCpKVun_TzbJAy_BTE81qn94jxXRlzVl8Y1VQ60_vB5Gt_hvZi7JH7_FwBCeTbtdIbWfF1hnn2CjN8rsT73G-QmnrWtYJV53jocGXGbwR_S" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Servian Walls. Rome.<br><br>The ‘Triumphal Gate’ of Rome, reserved for the spectacular ceremony of a victorious general and his troops entering the Urbs ‘in triumph’. In 14 AD, as an unprecedented honor, the gate was proposed for the passage of Augustus’ funeral procession to the Campus Martius. <br><br>Since Cicero is quite explicit that the prestige this gate bestowed upon entry into the city was far beyond that offered by any other gate in the Servian Wall, it seems safe to conclude, with Coarelli, that the Porta Triumphalis was situated along the course of the city walls. After a long history of scholarly dispute, the Porta Triumphalis is now placed NW of the twin temples of Fortuna and Mater Matuta and identified with one of the double gates of the Porta Carmentalis. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
