      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Portico, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a Republican portico found near S. Alessio on the NW Aventine, close to the ancient street under Via di S. Sabina; two walls of “yellow tufa” blocks, perpendicular to each other (one L. c. 19 m), were uncovered by Lanciani in the garden just NE of the church. At the intersection of these two walls stood a marble column atop a travertine slab. These remains have been plausibly addressed as a portico, and attributed to the late-Republican period. Recently, Vendittelli proposed that this portico be identified with the colonnades in the precinct of the Temple of Diana Aventina; this, however, is based on the inconclusive placement of Diana’s temple near S. Alessio. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1677-A52714</styleUrl>
