      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Apollinis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxPpQrNvfu36g90JORPwOJZqT5bQx9Og9CLLhMGMA4HdjTHde83pygMyPLPQp4OVVWG_JOCaQBXVhv5oUp26dfHUA8IdGRuUYKnM10PVtwCbCfIKNC4HCde6jAKhWbqrrXJpmPvKF6gh5v1Xhy6bJqhkvlmN1B60Y2YqyW8PGna1dVwb-LQ5G-mfJyKeDwGUM3mwOz693lmAQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Portico of Apollo, Rome.<br><br>Portico, or porticoes, on the Palatine built by Augustus in close connection with his Temple of Apollo. Erected, perhaps, by 23 BC, these colonnades are known primarily through literary evidence.<br><br> Propertius refers to ‘the golden portico of Phoebus [Apollo]’, due to the overall effect created by its giallo antico columns. The portico was adorned with statues of the 50 daughters of Danaus, which were probably rendered as female herms in a variety of colored marbles; three of these sculptures, datable to the Augustan period and rendered in black ‘nero antico’ marble, were discovered in 1869 by Rosa directly N of the Temple of Apollo. Fragments of similar female herms in red ‘rosso antico’ marble have also been reported. There may also have been statues of the sons of Aegyptus, but this is debated. With or without the additional figures, the use of polychrome marble imported from remote regions of the empire must have created a powerful visual effect. The accounts of Propertius and Ovid are generally thought to suggest that the statues of the Danaids were placed in the intercolumniations of the portico. However, study of the herms leads Balensiefen to propose a two-storeyed porticus with the Danaids placed along the upper storey, though such use of free-standing herms as caryatids would be unique in contemporary architecture.<br><br>Modern scholarship often equates the porticus of the Danaids described by Propertius and Ovid with the porticus connected to the Temple of Apollo and the Greek and Latin Library. However, literary sources addressing the Augustan Palatine use the noun ‘porticus’ mostly in the plural, thus the porticus of the Danaids may be just one of the several porticoes in the area. The close physical relation between the Portico of the Danaids and the Temple of Apollo has been placed within the late Republican tradition of building temples on terraces enclosed by porticoes.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxvpP3Kitmk-VmWV6u-aUD7wCrFzRWYiX8vgD3s4PvXm5gAZl4rm7BoI9ojH6HQHcDk6dYvxxkujim9Xx0OW4VEfBO0qivCT42b9Yep3i_exz-l6-LMNS1sKTvni30149q4n23QadxwFZlgmg7KocqiVLMGjvfvLncd3cVq_944ocAV6owYIL9A4p1RFtRs4guyqGktn_Ewkg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
