      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Fori Holitorium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v62LDbb0anybXJuMQvmovvgLXftqpWxfNy5KnG7FX45Sbg59ny5S_aNQMCKv5KwGodxzIuFMsVKcmvtiGGo4ESOPnbTndpRG2JVxs-vPt920t-TD4m1-do7QRwMATp8mZc6CCbBbwx_aBfMnsV_VojWuo_-Ng2puJsmmcLm-QK6jNH0NLYXSzEWSOpD_DZqh8-SCU8MUfbDanX8ARGue2lP0zR_I9__WYxjIQN5UI2rKLDj1Shsisg-L6nWuir8mDxTTuX6k3kovuRuIdn1wDZupeNMPQ5351Eyp6WG2d2hRXHfKEzwgM3djmqlZzLKrTMHyVBPBSzSk5MQQoY2f3yFjQWDL73bKc3YF4ohV737LaXoQyFzpuInqEMkpoWK9JJHEDZCBemVT4hg6_2HK3R9FM6tAmSb8sFA2EXACUpz4mU2r3p7dynenQMW9q4CrRyUMuffloMALgSoePglENKdxIwh_6G10nHP5sO5BRTyujHTFB5CGiaTpSwmGWEAnqKVySgeU7NI1aWyl-RRTiN4e2bI3IgIUlYdvcv1n6PsCQ9IXgoqSYNe9MaBOEsoSfD997McVi0Dp9oAU88-SEvV2ZdbbbDQz6AVwpL2pDGXqzMIx8J71C-ytTj7-maquvJpLgH5b101HbfpVVQ9KWlDHzGsBTGmCkun-PNjZ8EpcF6duudqlKwIbMQFgnz0MzgBVKI7i63upw" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Portico of the Forum Holitorium, Rome.<br><br>Late-Republican “gateway” portico at the S end of the Forum Holitorium, just outside the Servian Wall, where it covered the street issuing from a 4th-c. BC gate. <br><br>With parallel arcades, 5 m distant from each other, and exterior semi-columns on both sides, the portico is only partially preserved, without its ends, and consists of two sections which differ slightly in construction and date. The portion closer to the gate, extant to its entablature, is constructed of peperino on travertine footings; its orientation respects that of the other, pre-existing section — a more monumental construction entirely in travertine, though now reduced to mere remnants. Their successive phases or renewals notwithstanding, both sections can be attributed to the 1st c. BC based on materials and style. In its entirety, the portico formed a monumental entranceway to the city — paralleled perhaps only by the Porticus Aemilia (Campus Martius) outside the Porta Fontinalis. Further, the portico articulated the connection between the Circus Flaminius and the Forum Bovarium, both major stations on the triumphal route.]]></description>
