      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Philippi (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7ZOJUYQdh_I1720Pa-Tm1Ylsqqn5aeTBEVyNXF4PNalGdXMI04_0wNE2M35tw0HCY4dd7UqsFx6_LOgBh3pFmhfw6Aidkkn0Fbs8S_nK2Rxttd3VjOLYoBODa8FgFsvwfE6BtNlWb_Bh1JjkxnVPtAJXhY0WNBLMmV90eOjCA81Ts8d_7-G4tDDJyI12-cv8_4gDmEv5T1SJKDz0QNv8Ry0CqU_A-XBN4SrXgpJMLj893z8QjFcvD61UVA_KQVdVsOiNDVvLkEDPNtXLS67xv0WZf3uWxDkNh7hiYYLgoC4o5IZpYZ1z5SPzJUnqWGDzC3tc6i7AGMlGON14g8uoTBKq7O3D6atigbrTm5-Ks7fzqnOSVCFPHMFO_J8_3SYbSJugV6sXwOMUQlA8c2DoAa8OLh39RrauD4OM6d4-xHmAyQetvz8QWaumeutm8EBy17r3CN6vdTLn3e2IUJuSaXc8TZNmnVWDolltgp6z1yb8n8g-3_g_kkjW-iBN2persDmSIRLVJCfXmiNvUFt3iJmVxip_0HxEYUxphRsHWwixrTPUW80RKrpt1TWXE1xmdTCH6aHuplDkOzE7rGqiefwQjVgBkN53_HZx6jKpd8Us1vix-JsQ7KFV0cSjWfIKHHrPjCwXm5RLCuyO6kpfsDgTtHZPPtQIsSSbrX14rts2K5dH8tKWLCHtsHTVeEqq1qlW0sdeop" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Portico of Philippus, Rome.<br><br>This quadriportico, located along the NE side of the Circus Flaminius between the Porticus Octaviae and the Petronia Amnis, enclosed the Temple of Hercules Musarum. M. Fulvius Nobilior may have built it between 187 and 179 BC, yet according to Ovid and Suetonius, L. Marcius Philippus, who triumphed in 34 or 33 BC, restored the Temple of Hercules Musarum and built the portico around it. Near the temple was the formal gathering place of the collegium poetarum, which may have been established in Rome during the period in which the temple was built.<br><br>A fragment of the Severan Marble Plan depicts part of the enclosure; its S façade is a direct continuation of the S wall of the Porticus Octaviae. The outer perimeter of the porticus may have consisted of a wall with only one row of columns. It seems also that, unlike in the Porticus Octaviae, the temple was completely enclosed by the colonnade and lacked a monumental entrance.]]></description>
