      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Vici Aesculatorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Portico of the Vicus Aesculati, Rome.<br><br>Remnants of a monumental, double-sided Republican portico were found in the late 19th-c. between the Circus Flaminius and the Tiber in the vicinity of the Navalia, some 7 m beneath Via Arenula. <br><br>The portico has a central longitudinal wall (at least 15 m long, 1 m thick) of opus incertum faced by tufa blocks and topped with profiled travertine blocks; the building techniques suggest a 2nd-c. BC date. On either side of this wall stood a row of columns, six of which are preserved up to a height of c. 3 m. The portico’s total depth, c.18 m, rivals that of Rome’s most notable colonnades, such as those in the Forum Iulium or Forum Augusti. While the total length of this monumental portico remains unknown, it clearly extended for a considerable distance in an E–W direction parallel to an ancient thoroughfare, which has plausibly been addressed as the main avenue of the Vicus Aesc(u)leti; across the street from the portico was the Augustan-era compital shrine of the vicus (Compitum: Vicus Aesc(u)leti).<br><br>The portico has been tentatively connected with the Navalia, though a commercial function for this street-side portico in the Vicus Aesc(u)leti seems more likely.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1677-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.475504,41.892277,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Vispania (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Portico of Vipsania, Rome.<br><br>A famous portico built by Agrippa’s sister Vipsania Polla in the Campus Agrippae. It contained Agrippa’s annotated map of the world, which was erected there after his death by Augustus.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1677-A52714</styleUrl>
