      <Placemark>
        <name>Saepta Julia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5lJ3if3VhcmZzYFtP-RAyNeSAAiJcDCJIgdnz6WChOE05JycgFM5Izpe-DkUK2qEkWvmCX9ECDtSqAfrnijfV9AHxKmi9jWKTb3aTqVZyNr5U-QbwbLiz-1iOaz0-k-3vSUnttgJbaqB8YKxIXvJuFTHZu_5Sc29QA6J6LKK5fQg2iQ-Vu-dDzCO6YsZsWwADQ3UfN4E15hjE8RA5LI0oCaMnMnA-RoHJkU4IWOSHFrZ8TfvmNuLqwwYiUry426VKMkQudRA0P3-2a-GoxdQbhDPk2Dwe0rtSzQORjVF23NQU4gbZvqcg-HBTml8cj8dyZpskDuEwLyDCx_PUtHmy-NJNhpqrhLPLAB3n8a2KkWjlUcnedV7Zu77tng3AGqrF3HVd-3-4o3lVcdN9m3JKV2XQjXmmqssxoVeNQ7DpJVINWzgKtH1OithHFA8vwoCqRXCdW39xRMHo5Dx7OM7Wio1bMid3_xlXocwe0mCw_FXZf94JE6s5mCp6ysFTgBf-wIRpUud9uMlCwLMaukiGBOVBSAeeX4FKZvqLWUxCf446iFAMia_IvJK_7QkNSKn7CkoJiCRFOk4grvy_CIzEi_5sCp40JxsRuvpEKYkuqAfyHjJVDRH4gKZPEUeGrkrg3pfyVKoj_n8RuPZuxdR0LZ6aKqgQeoF-wBlEj7nU0DZqYcXteULBum1V7YkakDQMdHoavRwE2dQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Voting Hall, Rome<br><br>An open rectangular piazza of extraordinary dimensions (c. 310 x 120 m), enclosed by porticoes in the Campus Martius and intended for the casting of votes by the comitia tributa (assembly of tribes). <br><br>Planned by Julius Caesar in 54 BC, the Saepta was continued by M. Aemilius Lepidus after Caesar’s death and dedicated as the Saepta Iulia in 26 BC by Agrippa to honour Augustus.<br><br> Located E of the Pantheum, Thermae of Agrippa, Stagnum and Hecatostylum, N of the Diribitorium to which it was joined, W of the Isis Campensis temple, and S of the Aqua Virgo, the piazza was framed by two porticoes: the Porticus Meleagri on the , and Portico of Agrippa on the W.<br><br>In the Imperial period, the function of the Saepta evolved by necessity to become a center for popular entertainment and relaxation. Agrippa decorated the Saepta lavishly with marble and paintings; two celebrated sculpture groups, one of Olympus and Pan and another depicting Achilles with Chiron, were displayed there in a public picture and sculpture gallery. When not serving the idle crowds of early-Imperial Rome, the immense enclosure could house gladiatorial combats, including those of Agrippa’s funerary games. After the Saepta burned in the conflagration of 80 AD, it was rebuilt, presumably without a change in plan, by Domitian and frequently served as a market for luxury goods.]]></description>
