      <Placemark>
        <name>Stagnum Agrippae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6fnykNXHeWK-zp00dc0fsTDf1NNUFb85POh4i2vmSEHYxAtKtcTbZyteARsDKlXaKBqOglUpJPL60_hqvjaD20fNepKs8V8E8_3Uf7XxedzYXJgPA4I1Zg4lNZ5bdtwnLGjZEbOaZ8CrlN_uXY1gO5fyjTnAiY8qfyrcJJutVsIOd9W-TDqJnIbwmApz8cqg4TeWG45H6Ls_nAh09edWFFRloxwSlCp7K43UIMRjhXwYw0_8SvOTdWEC2D97OM0bXQWop8xJmdiWtQhqBUWjXSpSXJCUupTlkMcFQqp8hIRyxLjJJxoyuXufTz7AIC4ofQ4stY578ToVO0bnkUydovMeyJRdVB63rP_NSgHfgQgeVoSLBsq8ervUi-VVHkem5N8S3MYR-ZVCjCGisnH913CC0PSCAQ8yfGBiX3MZCfhRodX8js_wT8yC27oI8VZr3Cb_v4WQ_EgNk3rH7BGHmyi4l58dlXBIvBTflWP0br8zA5ItLDort-gW-JD6ES8DPba9gr_sbtF53ZxMnqrOMnmM297IoaAJAjYWUOydIkURBxIZH03szIoq9Zh85TguOKn7ZQ0YvMijKjLCMZVkDf04anH6zRkevQkka6ttBNLzhq_vr0HP9wutrMLh9Z-rbEyfGIsfNimvwymLAbY3wvCTuiFecsze7FnAypHhRgRVBpYdYxQzV3FpJ4nsTj0-QNamY_EcRM-A" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Lake of Agrippa, Rome.<br><br>A large artificial basin filled with still water created by M. Agrippa in a natural, marshy depression (the palus Caprae) just W of his Thermae in the central Campus Martius.<br><br>Most believe the Stagnum received its water from the Aqua Virgo and drained into the Euripus. If fed by the Virgo, the water would have been quite clear and cool, so perhaps the Stagnum functioned as a natatio (swimming pool) for the Thermae of Agrippa. The lake was also broad and deep enough to be navigable; Tacitus describes a lavish banquet held aboard a pleasure barge which was steered about the Stagnum by tugs. Apparently, there was also sufficient room on the Stagnum quays (crepidines) for brothels and parading harlots. Further, as darkness fell, the adjacent Nemus of Agrippa was filled with song and twinkling lights; by the mid-1st c. AD the surrounding area had become a residential neighborhood.]]></description>
