      <Placemark>
        <name>Thermae Agrippae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6avUbzuYfkm2uslcjERDroTjj1guc0XQF5QBAlvnwQwRR90IUBSNEAMvNwaKl5PFqzhG-tY8Elwh1SItJPWVOqdcHGDtOiYq_SPKZnAPcaM7BO9XNNlcDn4X0djuzQp_W9ohW6TUpKtHFZYD0fWuYk4XnTWkU4wggZd3kq_8XTwS6GCXx-Foj4yl_mqK1p_uNqTaGqihsC1z6ehq_IC_ZCPv3GzmopCqNJ8p3IdMU_0Ou_4W8RjHJRdUboWSdZ6_9Ww3VeWx_UPTa5ISsVwuPFZd8FwtsfXXhEza5OROjDprxucveHCJQ1MRHfVevWCYFweWhBLStHXw-3fgoZ_2LHPYmrU8f2JDnOGXgRk26F-u-fFTIwJyyEKD4tCGQbD-eRUddgHkOrzehDXQib_rIsyIWrqol8dnbp0Nt2BoAT8pQKxkkAArDDg46nrJ-x4pWQSGg7AmSyBfjkP8jZTvaznclPmEFO4b8Xy3ADPm2xkTWvn2V6VymDCyo9kvf1MP18s_zmXGpyRVVDjdffVXRE63l5_Uy5MJ2ELMZ5FtVmMK_wf3Q3r6kmXzUujCWhyKkuA9nans25ONFCalGN59qxBC6o7MPA3q5aYF-eEKIFJV5eLN8RjmDOuweMf-UF7iGSJ8Q215kpYzfIAMWX0qaqd9qrJxfLYGimBc6JYgDp8sv9BqSKgI-p4L6wzMCMCcRkG9ZabRli" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Baths of Agrippa, Rome.<br><br>In 25 BC Rome’s first public baths were built by Agrippa in the Campus Martius, just S of the Pantheum, W of the Saepta Iulia and the Porticus Agrippae and directly E of the Stagnum Agrippae.<br><br> The baths included a gymnasium and a sweat-bath which Agrippa called the Laconian Pyriaterion. Perhaps when first initiated the complex was a Greek-style gymnasium with a palaestra, sporting grounds and gardens. The baths were only fully realized in 19 BC with the completion of Agrippa’s Aqua Virgo which supplied the Campus Martius and Trans Tiberim with water. <br><br>The baths had elaborate decorations: Lysippus’ Apoxyomenos stood before the entrance, paintings were hung throughout, stuccoes adorned the walls and vaults, and encaustic decoration was applied to the terracotta surfaces. During Agrippa’s lifetime the baths may have been private, since Pliny notes the seeming contradiction between Agrippa’s call to make all paintings public property and the existence of paintings in the hottest rooms of his thermae. At his death in 12 BC, Agrippa bequeathed his Baths to the people of Rome and made provisions so that they might bathe free of charge.<br><br>The plan of the bath complex survives only in part. The Severan Marble Plan depicts a large circular chamber surrounded by blocks of rooms to the W and S; due to the circumstances of the fragment’s preservation, the areas N and E of the rotunda do not survive. A large section of this circular, domed space is preserved along Via dell’Arco della Ciambella. <br><br>The structure was restored on numerous occasions and substantially rebuilt after the fire of 80 AD; as a result, only a small portion of the original opus quadratum and reticulatum masonry survives. Preserved throughout these restorations were asymmetries of the bath’s original layout and its N–S orientation, an axis derived from the pre-existing buildings and street pattern. For these reasons, the 3rd c. AD and later plans are thought to reflect the Agrippan layout of the baths, if not their specific details.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6Y0vFV9Mx7_KMOkoikNa8zYfwKkZPre1W2LU9p0KZlmRRvocXZJnvDicM4sR27ZZip5uZ2MqEom_8ZjtOO1ImVGes8pBVlIegDTuQjJMfAymuaYOdYoo8CJwFdizZ0e3VC3f_Py2VNdP_-2l1CXQ9u_NJtIGrTCRWblaHUFF1K7l1L6PmM1Ct-FWHwwyj20pAeq1VxHywPIo_LuiAVMZHR2Zgrds6vCqCWRyuqApqao-IC7OKKK0qbtNhAxQINkIXoN14DrAgEI4yvI4UO91Dm71woliM5CdnDR2LAsvrGqb5hp843ufb6LA8Rp9FzdkGBJ_xi0jMYkR_07Oszp6JTa9rxl5uVPkNHwcP44hVmTk8Ye6umAn9Crd-UxzkP35wwu0-7aQG3zH40Yp2VuiSFoKoVVl3kLGXyXvwY7Jiyho2urDXygMa-asI_rYCxby_bxhyXACCw7beVP1dCKkgUsBCycMmLGGKHby8QxGFPJulv6Bzemp6hvyNG2-LYmXyBc5ogamvVol-bqoX6d9yfzLQ-GwPGtcE_8R5nXSzrCglsFjbl39v0i5MjSHNgrCgM-UwgAjqmNwQ9n4P2G2YkSTMCMkZWyKvFa_lspOLNEcH_5nvlp90lxgIqYQIMiocrmKTOh9E5qXFNc3crXER37bwT_0Uwzyn2tePmHGabhqZOmn_iIFsxdrqqTwnIcM0gmK13xIhloA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7W_5K9klR4YJf3EKPijw1-MuEommwZ1_ZmnAHXVwwbQyY1p8285YOBhp-vAbevEzxb1LTs4xpuzOm88m8B1yfVu1Mh_X0GYZwoUOHMMVi2Y1FRRIn_4VBHPu1k8sXZMoulzCD3_63NfSWAxDrL3XvnwmAf-OSagSgj7I4n0QzrzlapNAIifEG_ij5xoF9RYwLDQiJS8vNPa8OPoatG7m