      <Placemark>
        <name>Thermae Caracallae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gy0Cv8PcH3oolkwto0_qEXq9BHtWgIO_oz6ylBKPt9xILoEAv9qN-lRYNKTD-IKlE1grnQy0L1ZuZWoUn6D8v9Kfixv9Utt8_U43BDaME_VxfPszN2FSjS-0kXqNqg7lnSATop7d8j943bZ1Oo641im3V1Svw3jHJr9dVlkPa4Urx1tMaatsGDIZJbZdSYzYZ9ZywGmKGtXXGA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Baths of Caracalla, Rome.<br><br>Construction of the baths was probably initiated by emperor Septimius Severus and completed during the reign of his son, Caracalla. They were inaugurated in 216 AD. The baths were located in the southern area of the city, Regio XII, where members of the Severan family commissioned other construction works: the via nova leading to the baths and the Septizodium on nearby Palatine Hill.<br><br> The site chosen for the baths formerly belonged to a vast garden estate known as the horti Asiniani, developed by Gaius Asinius Pollio during the reign of Augustus. The Farnese Bull sculptural group which was later moved into the Baths of Caracalla was already present at the site in the time of Pollio, who imported it to display in his gardens.<br><br>The estate of Pollio was appropriated by Caracalla to build his baths and the existing structures were demolished down to their ground floors, filled in with earth and incorporated into the foundations of the new bath complex. The remains of one of these structures, a noble domus (house), were excavated by Francesco Ficoroni in the mid 18th century, and again in 1860-67 by G.B. Guidi.<br><br>For work to have been mostly completed in the time of Caracalla, workers would have had to install over 2,000 metric tons (2,200 short tons) of material every day for six years in order to complete it between 211 (when Severus died) and 216 AD.<br><br>Work on additional decorations continued under Caracalla's successors Elagabalus and Severus Alexander. The baths were likely mostly finished by 235 AD. Later renovations were conducted under Aurelian (after a fire) and by Diocletian. Under Constantine the Great the caldarium was modified.<br><br>The building was heated by a hypocaust, a system of burning coal and wood underneath the ground to heat water provided by a dedicated aqueduct. The baths were free and open to the public.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyIKCHakLFIozPDfC55XQXN4tnjAr9avrS-W9LIkZlgtb0CgcwmyCNfQ1ncKrJUerYQKr6LS92qOaNLGajp7Yk-aLirAXAOjkaNIQ7iFEwZc_491-wbvVHDHp_kiFSdjjqRHIW6JsOI_X49WWOb5_z5MebDo8GMM25fDIH9hwbfXfgvKrguCOPoq2SiidjWuGjB7wdmdDaP4A?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gw1EGXhDqQDf8X7ZdonPb7xO6EmBbJuAu6rV2k-tD1UxDw5G1RZHBL59WD0-O2x70Fchl-kx3Zu95VqhL7-MsAj_XAS3mhkcwZGqRDiVlAkCcJaDJifbTWIyDuhBmlvb6xBWTebuAkForwkOg-PfSWQDQjfgdtLqfadMwBciq-Mm7gCD2J1TRNolTDbAw7YqthksVmVDYLxJg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gw38QdtjV09sd-h2xxGHiniJ1ry_SuOmdAXfTxIAb_jhk0r0rZhYlw4mjH6c5E7iIa5lAfEB91UkCHuG5Qn0MoflmH9A_IYLQgH3N5DFMBpA1u2t9FOPn88ybj9rt8kbMh1BzmbSJ69tW1X6to7hZuOEWFa5i7RyQOGF77S8vhTYjhwZLHMfAQvqQX9wBXQCpUnuEPWCXEucg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
