      <Placemark>
        <name>Tigillum Sororium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Rome.<br><br>One of Rome’s most ancient monuments, the Tigillum Sororium was a beam supported by two vertical posts that spanned a road, probably the Sacra Via.<br><br> Dionysius of Halicarnassus relates that the beam served in the expiation of Horatius and that located nearby were two altars, one to Iuno Sororia and the other to Ianus Curiatus. The Arval calendar reports that the Tigillum Sororium was located AD COMPITVM ACILI; thus its location is fairly secure, despite the absence of archaeological remains.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1599-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.491122,41.891704,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Turris Murorum Aureliani (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxwPuobnbDsanC1pe-vymHHSEQSgQpKeduwi5kGiLaHAJIiBbrhwKOqzGLfI9tdCVLBHjEJTH6awAuzI50BDTgp2qPv7m0TjESqghmr1QtOS5cJFkmwLw6LZp78UkymQ0x5GLicuAumUa9sgdA1vB6QgQXbmJWY3Lddxxuf35m2ytjtznibRFMsXjY9c-rQuj9CTI7VkKk-ElA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Aurelian Wall Tower, Rome.<br><br>The Aurelian Walls were constructed between 270 and 275 AD at the behest of the Emperor Aurelian, as a defence for the city from the threatened invasion of barbarians from the North of Europe. The new enclosing wall had a perimeter of about 19 km and was constructed of sections of wall about 6.5m in hieght, with a parapet walkway along the top, punctuated every thirty metres by higher towers, covered by terraces.]]></description>
