      <Placemark>
        <name>Circus Maximus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4qU5Ow6wIgTf7BMXxyHWQ1EMpUdcPhHYABjifvD0OVQPG7JjY7CGScJOMyGzPREihejOCNEvx0XXMOPGmBhXumEfcpWL2h6Qi1AbSGdkpn2fNKNA5QirOJ4fg9z_QQC6eeEWG3GNAzD7HDBl3Djr7gn5Esz5C5SqzcfHZP_Vw-g-FtU0Rj4p0aIzHtPM4E3ptwJLpv_MgZDKt2VLszTX9LH7kkoqRh4ZK0WbkNlb0BbqoMaIxgkqlvckGWJ7Acm5y9nZubLihOEURbgWgWd3jrIEeu-Dwn5kJ1h5lw2GSEeVqeVADZo6RxynlHPp3iFFQbwgWDfnx96atw-QWNPHGy9OT7albjYyshYP0TneD1FJI4uwsDIVJ4aKBf1dYKfuE8Am484Rhxi6D7qedJzQX1mhXFxswTElZfOexDhfa3UAgSGVsXOKPTUCLFq2GuBjdQ7PLa2zdmERyZ6deftU7H36BJqbG0gR7Q34hgC0i5BUe3tqi8e1cs6pppfGMYZDgJdBVKiq46h5417DJ25r73iHQ3Cf-qwUmvQDx7HsKKtT9BG5iQqiC6R52F9RJxd80fltVlpr6b-60JrEsEgupcz9LpeR9p0jGTXO-9yiOyUXYhURONpg7BBN75AlrGNJcV1XtR7Bud8a-SndvclQbr_sFmI8wSfEQoI4o4ZWXUEvO3au_3gDYu_qCcgtdq7FbJD7bvIv6q" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Race track, Rome.<br><br>Rome’s oldest and largest circus, a hairpin-track designed for chariot racing, situated in the valley between the Palatine and Aventine near the Forum Bovarium.  <br><br>The Circus Maximus was an important venue for public entertainments, which included wild-beast hunts (venationes), mock battles, and athletic contests (i.e., running, boxing, wrestling) in addition to equestrian events. <br><br>Roman tradition credits the Tarquins with founding the Circus, but the structure, which had been modestly built up in the 2nd c. BC, only achieved its definitive form under Julius Caesar and Augustus. Though little archaeological evidence remains from the Augustan Circus, detailed literary accounts, especially that of Dionysius of Halicarnassus, allow a relatively reliable reconstruction of the monument.<br><br>Caesar inherited a circus with an already well-defined race course: 12 starting gates (carceres) stood at the Forum Bovarium end, two turning posts (metae) were fixed within the track and their axis was adorned with statues set on columns; in the Republican period, there was no continuous central barrier wall. At an early point, the “Cloaca Circi Maximi” had been installed to drain the valley floor and thus provide an area suitable for racing. The starting gates themselves were unroofed, wooden stalls with an arcaded, tufa façade facing the track. Each gate had a pair of wooden doors, which were simultaneously opened ‘by a single rope’ at the start of the race. A set of ova (eggs) used to count laps stood in the track near the carceres where they were readily visible to the charioteers. Before Caesar’s modifications, most spectators sat in wooden stands erected along portions of the long sides of the Circus; limited stone seating was reserved for the élite. An arch erected by L. Stertinius in 196 BC is often placed at the SE end of the Circus on the spot that would later mark the center of the semicircular stands; however, this monument is known only to have stood ‘in the vicinity of the Circus’, in Maximo circo ; given the vagueness of Livy’s description, the arch cannot be indicated on our rendering.<br><br>Several ancient cults native to the valley were honoured within the circus. Near the first turning post (primae metae, at the SE end of the track) was an underground altar belonging to the archaic deity Consus. The shrine of Murcia stood within the track near the primae metae on the Aventine side. The Temple of Sol rose within the seating area on the Aventine side of the Circus, just opposite the finish line; it was to Sol that the Circus as a whole was consecrated.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7NZn7xntG_yaqmDyfDvmsfI8plRz4tR9Am_75y6ztlOkkHc36ppK5qMn4CHUQv1-a4iDPqWzJEXpPoEAQpXMbbZDoGrno563BTlL5B2uOj6Zf-0euAFEKxl3MsWxsoNXPCnIMgTRfa0UmXYN7dYX9XEqhvZQ_HMenmrAO7RfHdq_xinaqj4gYz8bpFD5A8Sge5P6tlxV_yhCh1SkqRoSaxhKNjzRDEjFBY3m_WgEc8oGfYOhyWfzdF0Ongbg59dYUnkEUZdEKA5OgYqUALXKYXwcVFoB62Bn_GBW89qFH_1IaYY0piqNULtXb5knOlrYd-Ut4w_EVW15d_yQUSMbHOgsYy15OysI0CrQN_a3n4g5fEFfwue07ghDJRT3ZJOZRycCgM9Cum1OKeg9F6862sM9bA5HofFX95JFHx6dIFGjzO4PmmoLua6PNln-0k6o3mGnm1PQV-dsf