      <Placemark>
        <name>Naumachia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzH3Vhu4fRINV0LGJXnSvajUjaAev0G26lybQHLAWBdngR9RvNmt0X_KJvAVK536q-ZT6TfiALBhiSOj8NyjspjX22A5WU4Abbm5AYaEQsdGHjH7R6m0F1zV4yc8ILsRVZ7UIM53nOUhn_5DM5S--rMLWSn1fzzQiDfCEU72uko8NRcbtCCsilCPPjvgytG-a3FEdyctT_EJw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Naval battle amphitheatre, Rome.<br><br>Extensive water basin built by Augustus in Trans Tiberim and dedicated in 2 BC to stage mock naval battles and aquatic displays for the Roman people.<br><br> In the Res Gestae, Augustus gives the dimensions of this large basin as 1800 by 1200 Roman feet (533 x 355 m), with a possible average depth of 1.5 m. There was ‘an island in the middle’ of the basin, probably for distinguished spectators, and the island was connected to the bank by a bridge of notable span. The useful life of the basin reached into the time of Titus; literary sources (including Augustus himself) imply that it was soon surrounded by the nemus Caesarum, the Grove of the Caesars.<br><br>According to Frontinus, the main reason for the construction of the Aqua Alsietina was to supply water for the Naumachia and the adjacent gardens in Trans Tiberim; its conduit ended behind the Naumachia.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxEZYWg87o5yLahAPhC38LYGt2bONRlhLkZ38nlnHSMgs74vqM3kBam0_4FemZh0tpj0LFHKiooBnOQ1scKVPyE_nRrplmxclcQZmkddGA_E-Zb021_ZONz68fqNghTgE8InZtQ7YuUlJiWIW_G7BYs9IpMpSr0f2wAKFC0BszIBXDQ7ExJ5f11wmL9-dxeHdOeADZnmvM2Pw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1788-A52714</styleUrl>
