      <Placemark>
        <name>Theatum Marcelli (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5iztI8sPXBLOZJFrmlouszjed9lsIJ4vMErQ4k-3xGmhd1cvmXadGqVjOr3n0TTqoMlLzaiUNN9bWEnWEZ2bYcoSs_78wYXkqiUDoesdu1KT2uJ1DsSfGz4WyhyYzLHGuUXTMSEoirchVt916brHWKUhSuCXdBgbyWVnC7sFONPRmIuFgp3HjXkn4K8UiZhq5gXwikTzIpP8r_pk5jWrw2LprXq2hogCmh8rkkMjKQ9vf3ZBJVSmbfQKZya6FTD53tZG8_ONvSP0RqeKA4nkTeBfHm_zkRjbb5XIohPslVm2TuUhKJlgpeHT-oN-_djwtbcu38Xa-y5vJW_-YxNTgy6fp2mosIE_AIq6OgEtROHhC20z6IEcEElQ0SHX3ZB9VJ51f-4rvqrtOqlnuNEWq8N0SR1eZh_dXVMBykK84z8RXQGXSnslrbERAE9NRyO7A8I6z8OXLImEciA_D_bBybJj3rPuCW2gkL42SMOA605UszyZM6Bq50iIAlfSEpT9RjL4JybPYpDm6q7MnxHly91REYinOt2aciXV669Pf_tnWSqDDQcsmK57-kU5ExpJbbPtfM7YpLR5bIWwtH8QIKpUPN1e33MIp8tuj22HrjZcxvOaOY6PdgZLy7_dSf37uT7fpz5pRLG2JljTT-0KLLEDu2grXFm9Mvg6YDUMhhJGCBtAc9Rvks9MudQqZmNTmLZ04d--0jPA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Theatre of Marcellus, Rome.<br><br>The Augustan successor of the theatrum ad aedem Apollinis and the second stone theatr built in Rome, located at the E end of the Circus Flaminius in front of the Temple of Apollo Medicus.<br><br> Dedicated in either 13 or 11 BC by Augustus, the theatre was named after his nephew M. Claudius Marcellus. <br><br>When Julius Caesar initiated the building project in 44 BC, he had to remove temples and houses from the area, including the Temple of Pietas on the NW end of Forum Holitorium, to prepare the site for the theatre. The construction took place under Augustus, who bought more land near the Temple of Apollo to extend the site. Even though the construction was not complete, part of the celebrations of the ludi Saeculares in 17 BC. took place in the theatre. Possibly a larger theatre was intended at the time of Caesar; while the 4th c. AD Regionary Catalogues list its capacity at 20,500 loca, others estimate it held c. 13,500 spectators.<br><br>Space for only a narrow thoroughfare remained between the rear of the theatre and the terrace walls of the Apollo and Bellona temples. If the widespread scholarly opinion that this passage served as part of the triumphal procession route is correct, Augustus’ architects must have had difficulty fitting the large theatre building on the site. However, the triumphal parade, which originated in the Circus Flaminius, may not have followed this paved street, but rather have passed through the orchestra of the theater itself, with the cavea conveniently open to seat spectators. Since two circular monuments, the Perirrhanterion and the columna Bellica, may have obstructed the passage behind the theatre, a route through the orchestra seems likely.<br><br>Most of the theater’s plan is preserved on the Severan Marble Plan. The foundations of the cavea survive to our day, having served as the infrastructure for Palazzo Savelli-Orsini. The lower vaulted arcades of travertine, located beneath the cavea and facing the Temple of Apollo Medicus, were uncovered when more than 4 m of débris was removed during the Italian government’s ‘liberation’ of the monument between 1926 and 1932. Two symmetrically-placed halls of basilical plan were located on either side of the stage building. The function of the semicircular walled space behind the stage building that appears on the Marble Plan (on a now-lost fragment) with a pair of centrally-placed, small square features is a matter of debate. <br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6czPHJd2IrZD3ffqCP24ss-qDmAD5lkCo6VNQN_ae8P-b4pQXEZ6wWlFYt-KYJ0z5sIoMNbmbCDPD0dJlwVybQ3N-WukMDS11Gf45hcxgzvBAtb5t_WgHC4_EJ5kXTRjkv-D8eZX_GcSUT9VQuUMumWWURcu1NYZ-GPAAyw2opO2Z4Do_bF0AICaiS2xcofccYIhBnkM5n4eyI9py8cxp8f5SqAxy6ieG3Ze6CzCbARu_zGQqIvdNJK7zv_zkeYTzsKlP_jF40WC_RLdyeNP3tMvST-7gGP8FgWmVs4FXdp9YBxbBwXs_gVoAfHWJWhhjA5jd80ix2HlRN1UtOaIYRNuWhk50M18v5YbbS_uEM3-42TGO5n-jKR7quXTNdQmiAvTJtB7EB6VERt4fRbB9INtE8O1KvHGnW4TPahZYCWjYVPNyiQ4yxqoWWSZz7y4rDLIn4ZhTMjhNv-sLpnmyfogFj-1_QHYxn7yLkRrzmA9CIjeGPBs59pkRuyTotaEJ_VHV9XLYPbspGEWqCK08Rqz9ztEunzT6hrs3Ong69P3LaNiRvGTJ7B7nB8CIQBhKdUufPWV-8lpICJmVOp-cMcw2dhbKL-kyiCL