      <Placemark>
        <name>Theatrum Pompeium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7CojnHi4iJK2HvUKEtoLA_9VJoekWa-a3w_6HAsAxc1b80Odv1zPThUdPaCHNM73fAIU0oSNtZc7lHAsvhXHnfK7vdIcJfUFIWrcIxqb3XuKH8L4Jyydk-lgLwQbXSTXljQKAF0IUTdgINlAj6r4N-sld60iluEoaAgJe22SNHveC5BGqtpPRV_NxUctS0Wp-mnuJeFt8EQGWSjPRCcEdKuf6fK_XID4iV5WvppDjU4MRd7zy3tV1MXWEHnYE7F7WGWoezEoVCHWPkBQAewgbPL1WTCiZOnGvXPls6oLCiN9QVCdMdJp4QYl21tZ3ncV2FJaswPyUohNjbcHQG_m2vIriEp1KjyoGXeaqENr1zMTO-jdiYNdvN9JCpr_v11hbN93hSx8OV57mr2Hni-pHp-97niPAIwFKs9ijcD_AcDd86db_j2lgVjT-bwTFW5SSv23UsK5u5rjiVH7-C44wKNNoHQqReDXeXqQagIC17VHJ-ooVntq1X6o9TrokAJbTRrGgnXgEEpKjwVGpwXsQ9KMfur86u9tmBwkR6QXi-LlK0qfBsZIWSywNqVLLOQo6a6JKL15Imqji1jjIrhXm_9dsl60FDCxfCBsDuLS2SvWE_X0pooEDCTzM2OG-WsnqOkpQUm24x4y7qjIbBWuyNQ-qIKakCZFHWg4650YwHQ19me4hAA83mBXPF9WihFWSP_7x0NXmQIA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Theatre of Pompey, Rome.<br><br>Pompey’s Theatre, dedicated in September 55 BC, was the first permanent stone theatre in Rome, and it remained the largest, and arguably the most important, of the three permanent theaters in the Campus Martius that existed by the end of the Augustan period.<br><br> It was known specifically as Pompeium theatrum or Pompeianum theatrum, and also under the more general terms theatrum Marmoreum, theatrum Magnum, or simply Theatrum. <br><br>Adjoining the theater on the E was a quadriportico enclosing a sculpture garden, the Porticus Pompeianae. Pompey’s Theatere was perhaps restored by Agrippa as aedile in 33 BC along with all other public buildings. It was also restored by Augustus but it is not known when. Subsequent restorations are attested in abundance, and this makes it difficult to reconstruct the appearance of Pompey’s Theater under Augustus with absolute certainty.<br><br>The curved seating area, or cavea, of Pompey’s Theatre has a continuing presence in the urban fabric of the modern city: it is visible in the distinct curvature of certain streets (Via di Grottapinta for the internal curve, Via del Biscione and Piazza Pollarola for the external curve), and in the ground-plan of houses built on the theatre’s radial foundations, partly extant in cellars. Modern maps thus allow an accurate reconstruction of the cavea; this reconstructed cavea belonged to the original construction phase of Pompey’s Theatre. The orchestra, the open area at ground level within the cavea, was a perfect semicircle. The outline of the cavea is also known from drawings of a lost fragment of the Severan Marble Plan, one in the Codex Ursinianus. The cavea faced E onto the stage building, or scaenae frons. Comparative evidence from other theatres of the 1st c. BC lets us reconstruct a stone, rectilinear scaenae frons for Pompey’s Theatre in the Augustan period. The scaenae frons shown on the Marble Plan with its central rectangular exedra and two lateral curved exedrae dates only to its Domitianic rebuilding after the fire of 80 AD. The Marble Plan shows two widely-spaced, parallel rows of 5 squares, adjoining the scaenae frons to the N and S, and extending beyond the outer limits of the cavea. The absence of an outer wall on the N and S suggests that this may have been a thoroughfare that passed between the theater and portico. <br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7x6_HgQrVaFLTcrVvL74n-bNf2Yi91SmChKalxhjTOtM9Fyx-BgHbB_4DLVtTx6zwl12acHYbZaY-oyUXYvyVhRj32GRk5xfn5KOScC6HAk2CEF9E_YTt-ExdCMsOoSIIc9CfFCZEYmX8t6GnWgCap0LTTJwaoePmdATAlfyOJn1iZOXozjnhRX5FJw0wgbZ80W4o_sqrL_lluuIfivVFJjRg8I4uleFaIbCs3fvviBRMorJOuHZU2RnUGtaOtpcSHi2nBr6-uoFrRqHnv8OGtDOz7JtNYqKcL2g_-4J57Lyp3ap5D52J_YSCoVkh7zOhk5xu51mP2WpF40JCQQf8-f6nudwgaQQF0Zy10QLTA_hsyCwBH5Cj0uV3irEs_-GxDl6mBPJqTn3FOBrHokOk5qBrk9zluh72P3uagVu8EPdK8-OA8Nb_rxvpFVUOBLEmyk_GTT6Pp2nHUlCll9JBpKXEFsmHf7UhY1MUph8DZP9xDJVxeeIOeXL3XMtxrNKHtOut-kz8FOkNtZHIEIdDDXjM9ArA_HQ610iqG8ilrkge1Ru1WMEne7Um7FetZALuGE9vzTaHpfNTnQIMZWuI-vli0fQzG4_cK5pkIPFQ5SRMNLXLtLVpaSBBhpUivV_tMsh51WrlDhCMrwCbR6YtlB6Ret6Q4Qj1cGJvTy-8nnUiQ-JZic7JlHhVztVXCSDwf5SlN3QiH-g" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5RiUJSdKPRNJXRrT7QO9HFeDgm3WJFX4bgsY1jWN0Kb1CtLhU7FYqgNCSFlbDEUUzK1ezap04R8fX6GIVNSUTMDj74xAOwGuRXqKwJX3pKAM6nV_6D-Q3SwNpY1gaUganAy2fMzHaErgj-dEt_fHhbLhUXvVQamGwLoYbOrEq_bFCl4DTUNN5oDShV6Z2BI2zgUBD44EK8ESMQsltgHjAM79CAv9WMkH6Hgk6UQGLuBlXOouYbja88Pln8iWFQKRXn2tmU-Wi7GH91hIv43tb0QFTt0JS7-XEEWdAul-sfXLpCBsvxpA_2ARehTieVLGOAZepR6CXNcx9l62jJnrqnoRbhD1tYW5N_xQuO40Y4Nrwy3eTNrw0yGMJFl4LtjxdI9nAWAj_b7b3XZiQcMRPT-J5-FVuktV4Q74t7EiLFQiJuXX3GJ-pVCf0G2Dx5LpIS-Ilv3R09ZO4xJ5cy0sCXBXV5O1wyTGZbUVyqmJ_5No2JWoZmqjkY2beoGdGoat-7bzZT5OTUL5-0e4xMKM6pj6XAr82fBGm1h4SMrCAwjTpq7KZ5dFoEs1JZux82pB8c90g9qVdUti8KVbdkrcYCIlwQ46NkdXfJy9tuwLTvreYDGBo62SdtxkzM_ygeC6cUS41IPhqrUjCHo34RxxtVzYjTuA0iJfTYEyKqys3HBJQmOxuVdJYSSE_YN6QyH7a9cTxs33Pgrg" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
