      <Placemark>
        <name>Ara Martis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Altar of Mars, Rome.<br><br>This altar, the primary cult site of Mars in Rome, was one of the most important and ancient religious monuments in the Campus Martius. <br><br>Though the dedication of the plain itself to Mars was linked with the expulsion of the Tarquinii, the altar was thought to date from the time of king Numa. During the Republic, the altar was closely associated with the activities of the censors, and thus was linked with the Villa Publica, in which it probably stood.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.481026,41.896387,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Ara Maxima Herculis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwTy8S2Y8hT4IQy8cIG7pUxwOM2hrnzamyq6KU8X61ULbxDXVLhPu08nNIYCb9sTTKmv8Ug-yEPmC-weNrpxuZaRxvrJPC9ek-MNV26W5Gv8g9OYTT9SwAf89sTo4JeCtGgE4bf5AdR2BdTFl-36Snx1ipWMnQ2nOUrPd9FrY3JqnDtb4Psb-a6PpRDE3TUPj-Edy9eE0XnLA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Great Altar of Hercules, Rome.<br><br>A monumental altar to Hercules at the Forum Boarium, the oldest and most revered centre of Hercules’ cult in Rome and also the alleged starting point for Romulus’ delimitation of the pomerium.<br><br> The altar’s approximate location and grand size can be deduced from Servius, who mentions it as ‘behind the gates of the Circus Maximus’ and explains the epithet Maxima by citing ‘the magnitude of the structure’. Dionysius of Halicarnassus describes it as ‘near’ the Forum Boarium and, while judging the construction inferior, stresses the altar’s extremely high reputation; it was the place where ‘oaths are taken and agreements made for sealing business transactions’.<br><br> The remains of a large, solidly built podium, exceeding 20 x 30 m, of Anio tufa blocks with travertine facing under the E part of S. Maria in Cosmedin have convincingly been attributed to the altar. The objection by Richardson that this place is “poorly located to be a turning point in the pomerium of Romulus” hardly forms a valid argument, but his conclusion that the platform “is clearly not temple foundations but a solid mass” <br><br>The altar must have formed the focal point of an agglomeration of sanctuaries to Hercules in this area. Its connection with the Invictus epithet of Hercules is not explicitly attested and rests on the attested cult of Hercules Invictus ad circum Maximum as well as a postulated temple of Hercules E of the altar, combined with a series of mid- to late-Imperial Hercules Invictus inscriptions some 50 m N of it.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
