      <Placemark>
        <name>Auguraculum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Augury, Rome.<br><br>Site for augurs’ observations on the Quirinal.<br><br>It stood on the collis Latiaris, the lowest and most southerly lobe of the Quirinal (near SS. Domenico e Sisto), but its exact location within this region is unknown. On analogy to the auguraculum in Cosa, it was probably a relatively small (5 x 5 m) open space demarcated with cippi (Castagnoli 13). While there may have been a thatched hut at the Auguraculum on the Arx, there was no building at this site.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.486786,41.895753,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Capitolium Vetus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, Rome.<br><br>The most ancient shrine in Rome dedicated to Jupiter, Juno and Minerva, the Capitolium Vetus was located on the Quirinal at the top of a steep street leading from the Temple of Flora.<br><br> Varro explicitly notes that the Capitolium Vetus was older than the Temple of Iuppiter Optimus Maximus Capitolinus. No archaeological remains of the structure survive, but Varro’s use of ‘sacellum’ implies that the Capitolium Vetus was of modest dimensions, perhaps no more than an open area with altars.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-9C27B0</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487389,41.900462,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Romuli (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5o-L6Iri1Jy91QuWQJeGX61zDflm3OhnUIrJHqVa6YMd7utJTCnOnaOhVC6QbtaKtaTOVscgQosu3wYpBSsFVg_mp6Av7rhUkW_FhlSCAbWk9DFQQ1UUv20_l1NumYzbJHzlIRuJN4-RN5zhXR3cKFX7aw-DwPzqXnw7z8c5TxPcZHVIHk5I35C_PndOatFTwEvvVgy-FW-0eLfMxvD7CjkUbp0HCxuQCPP16K9Rgo5xlN3Anjmxd_FQazyE2FRpZ6IHbJ0w5C0_Js8qZpXMpRCw8efaMrFJDy0fXWU9-nH-VXxRN6cZKPUlJVeXLInwB2TfhU2X17hc_uD2pm53iur0v89UAKqJVlArHI8liopur2in-485oGhyzqkfoBrm4oNN976PcWtpU25z5r367jhUpE58ic04A683YStl5AvTp8fhh_wUXaAj0pp41mUDFGsj9F80VdtpGukiv4Vy2qTiA8ZE799j2dsDlyD5SnoB3uM9u945AZkDOi-ZHUnN_IyG5QxeYocGpwDRB7r9u5P6tgTybkfFtxWIeiypdKVTeKVb3cuPqKRBiKwbCbxWn0KT6AMffg35XCUqAnQPj_FjoIEgAr0dHWPq6LGfRWUEoPlnNybCMm5yRCKWdjUrvOREH-YHq3tPoly9NrIB-6tkGhqs-xYfMLnar3ibs-Q2wKVZxmuhvH3EjYJUP1ckW-h3clo8pc" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>House of Romulus, Rome.<br><br>An archaic hut built of ‘sticks and reeds’ was located on the S Palatine slope near the Domus: Augustus and the Scalae Caci by literary sources.<br><br> In antiquity, unusual care was taken to preserve the exact appearance of the archaic dwelling, which was maintained as a sacred site.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4azCGl1bVkquOPY94Y0ivFqi-gNrimjv0H4oS4QYiJIyGJYjK2JOK-qwNcy695rfaZY_33j9-AMYp5Ej8ne3BfemPAJE4bPbo4vPaara1IWgnXRjKA5VI1dAndOxNb6rziVs-QHeysxzrPoQVNIpBXuYHChPZzAccEdw6dJwdNKHKsra1oa_JvVRK2wRH3CpK_LsZBO3y9TeWkXandegSOsGQvPr7aTIEuHljgNtbEukBHVDnDteBdsYsJQrKUeXvlTfLM-WlfyLW1AlKSHXlmN_e0rxc6kQ8Sz4_CQxXmZNQV-95Dvibiz0w9-Bzwu8p8u47PizWcxwiq794m9tUo-kIk5znn-i2QvJnGjaRyTH-ei8DRoE1YpOPQN6tOs_QuQwp4MW6rHk93Mb6-UlzXH6CyoxwSfCN9Q7feQD0edk4qOnoSs6w9Ei9RMc1whLqJ-TQs6TA3sZxjrewMNrHngHjrWKBXtuJ0vwWv-L3kDbSXxQJ-0v6R53zIceWeD6kGCHUkBTGbK6CBiXPC7SxPTw9tZwUquHQXv4t6AWdVRc-QYkogiDvhK94mk9dxEWcreRdNwMpRb598sDMmKHOYOR5-sHqfz1pYi9VWpqwABuM8s29-JgJOlbhI7NCaPoge_B4VWEYexN3zUVfVK-cGRA9vI_nEc9Ix977zOI_UCBeXwNeSXWC9g1Ah5swVeLeJzKpDIwOHKA" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
