      <Placemark>
        <name>Pantheon (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7HFDJQZg_wQW7buQ8lkbFwrOrMimGxKTHEQVNl65hugggG48xno18ZBuCBMMv-9cMmNz01QjdjBeChiY7o1JHmRRzyH3VB6MMVDZNcx8t7eW9EXQb_ZlaIqrqXa9683Zn4kDlhKqD3CbTAAW0RZnBU2IxtgkEF9RxZ6WZnU0pBwWQWnZqChWN-ns6JLkrx9qr6W6H4NEjRcDb79tpX3kaBhYs4Yb-GGAKaWGSfARjBfW85vXobmoGZtSwNL96PRMmYUoK6Me4pUzMcn-q75amqHIBSbJirCVXAAagw_qmvYAvroPsHoPgyfo5V4s6ys9vFPU-LVlVftWvnYAaeVDNZAmMOlJVjR1JBxRveFn8-RBZLSKWm0xwcsQevhZXqsTr80YUXCU_hIvbeXy8Gv3CRQNQC9ZpsqTsOXpRE9gV4Kh3CkuoFMFuGI_XC9UrGiveyAFf28zLWRTgOWfOxwo6MiN8ZyrB8kr84T4mTauR9FF670e-diB-r1YV0yDDzdkKE4SQBT3pykaHJrjhpqZ_r6dorEPmg_Ioll4RPmSxtmSXSn55tp2k-UZTfQ7wqhGU2oFcRIT6erWaqvprgRfnjbTqTJENvnAPIGs0NUOc4kPath4jgxpddhD6zVdBAWvIm7Ym1W0RXMSY4SRj3PKOwKU_Pw7ECpYYWXLB4Dyp5ydMByZNOJu50UWP3TS4l6Lx_Tn34yXZdrg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple to all the gods, Rome.<br><br>Famed temple erected by Agrippa in the Campus Martius, c. 27 BC, with his other building projects there: the Saepta Iulia, Diribitorium, Thermae of Agrippa, Nemus: Agrippa, and Stagnum Agrippae.<br><br> Evidence suggests that Agrippa’s Pantheon had a rectangular pronaos entered from the N by way of two flights of stairs, which was linked, perhaps via a S projection, to a marble-paved, enclosed circular component, which was almost certainly hypaethral.<br><br>Under the façade of the Hadrianic Pantheon, 19th-c. Italian excavations uncovered a rectangular foundation c. 44 x 20 m; its N wall, composed of 10 travertine piers and rubble infill, lies beneath the 8 front columns of the Hadrianic pronaos, while centered on its S wall is a projection 21.25 m wide (the depth cannot be determined due to the massive foundations for the Hadrianic rotunda). Traces of two levels of marble paving slabs were located below the rotunda’s floor, one plausibly attributed to the Agrippan monument, the other to its Domitianic restoration. Further, fragments of a circular wall (L. 10 m, H. 1.65 m, W. 0.61 m) were revealed along the SE edge of the Hadrianic rotunda’s foundations; it has the same degree of curvature as the Hadrianic Pantheon.<br><br>The Augustan Pantheon was destroyed along with other buildings in a huge fire in the year 80 AD. Domitian rebuilt the Pantheon, which was burnt again in 110 AD.<br><br>The degree to which the decorative scheme should be credited to Hadrian's architects is uncertain. Finished by Hadrian but not claimed as one of his works, it used the text of the original inscription on the new façade (a common practice in Hadrian's rebuilding projects all over Rome; the only building on which Hadrian put his own name was the Temple to the Deified Trajan).<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6Pol8ZKXsyiXXagT6Xfmxp1uN8WtAgeLSqRe1nWnAtYBi5IMOJa8N7o6-TwQIq-mlhhEiGGGAoDxEn9pN5CxCgpfy234tZoi-IsPvVlld_xgSMWE-frBD1BcYMnf2aoovN0nzaKqmpP-GDvAFpA23rCwQKniUUFKm9YFIqWtPn7t0Y6ReRaYwgTMHMsQoMFF138eXXYwHXawsY5-JKuSrx9T0l3daWi0cRXzE3UO7SojqMkpW9X7SoVZP6nkZ2pvJUuE3rs7KOncW14QbGDu1HXL7-P11dRWbvwAZavrypyBLW_eW0oKx1Vff7VZEIQ6MiBGgy7Wz5pWgz7wwdQaeNzoNDLRiL5ZSA1obA7k7zRFyC-wR4EKHa5fpwGGHLDCCsvr0NU4ZWksjmAEMP5xpYzN10vztFS_rOumdisC3CiEGanU4a0TgespkaoFw3qQbQ_a0JteqtviTiDyIYfgls12fWuOZOKpteAxjuOS6AcWS0KPU-V1xCP-Yba1QHv-LrWUt4HshHZP1xw2HrKyM9VqUyRz6OAMspeLMPCkuWmFlWpuBH44MnbHzbE73o2Pa3J-djthvJLW3vNYCQ9reJSqP1qayhE0Bavu20iBuSFCpIUIPyOpYQ_COdi2mDZU9bW5CLfWP93IFr-eTE6ZKKxXij0_tSj4JfQwjB3AMu-6E3h_m4WjEDOZIDpEsFtcTeapGR4cmJwA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6-HSXfmtNzA0PQ35-gFe37qqlU9gGpCtvpxN4SH4MVu__fVdeZXt7rniEOPBLVnPsiSSXX784DMcPGf-VH2UCGLU18FI6DOTj5H7Jux1OEnKTFUV8Hp23OuDCYAH5O18mB8Y6sUuuT94xL_d7Btp5ZRyKs9WZK4iZbOa53hibuSV3G8fEuDbdQCuTfmMH8tdAvbIddptWf0k1ABwIGQWCHLyGNfqZn95_IWQPyEgS56vMgRbbwXUg8sEUTHZ5xYiVTZDpJJlcHSTR8TeIczqoF4k0MVJ12rssOPWpyW_qrfi2-roLvIqWFkpS28roxxBZVtOoxB7wY1rSWqYUGxz6RzWqq1uF39YWVwMhgCjgF_HW1J6aditYpJLGQzgjSvvQ43ZoioctE-vlWboTvt63ZxeRZX2GOjcx9SBv5ZQ8fvKVT3ciWfztT04bxUjnoPY71cLdRWiAskM54tNT