      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Acilium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to the Acilii, Rome.<br><br>An Augustan compital shrine marking the intersection of the Sacra Via with a street running N–S on the S slope of the Carinae. <br><br>Its well-preserved remains were excavated by Colini in 1932 during the opening of Via dell’Impero. The compitum consisted of a small distyle shrine atop a square podium (c. 2.75 x 3 m) reached by a short flight of 4 steps. Inscriptions date the monument to 5 BC, identify it definitively, as well as indicate that the surrounding neighborhood was known as the Vicus Compiti Acili. This shrine was a notable landmark in the Augustan era; the calendar of the Arvals describes the Tigillum Sororium as ad compitum Acili.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.490554,41.891205,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Concordiae Augustae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gz98267hyo6nYZq1KzfgqxP-zrzwN9HU2TAQeGogQTGlD0LHseXWOoMoNS9qqQGX33qrXWuRzF0Y49zGI4ybEZX5ogK1JgqwHfkdzVBwXdhHxUl4bC4XxcWN1IsBjMW6WcbMGE9jRBiIz13w6-oFy8ASBvfPpfuMnqfaIneTVkABpGxC2qysC1erm2yL-oMqZGgqfOCooFrRQU?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Shrine to Concordia Augusta, Rome.<br><br>A travertine altar inscribed CONC]ORD[IAE] AVG SACR and a massive travertine base (1.58 x 1.62 m), most likely for a statue, were found in situ at the Forum Boarium in 1939; the findspot is close by the Cloaca Maxima and just E of the major N–S traffic artery along the Forum Bovarium. <br><br>The altar and base have been convincingly interpreted as the remains of a compital shrine and attributed to the Augustan period. As a compitum, the shrine is an important testimony for the existence of residential quarters in this area, which must have demarcated the W border of the forum.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gz98267hyo6nYZq1KzfgqxP-zrzwN9HU2TAQeGogQTGlD0LHseXWOoMoNS9qqQGX33qrXWuRzF0Y49zGI4ybEZX5ogK1JgqwHfkdzVBwXdhHxUl4bC4XxcWN1IsBjMW6WcbMGE9jRBiIz13w6-oFy8ASBvfPpfuMnqfaIneTVkABpGxC2qysC1erm2yL-oMqZGgqfOCooFrRQU?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.481991,41.888743,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Fontis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to Fons, Rome.<br><br>A shrine to Fons built from Corsican spoils was dedicated by Papirius Maso, probably in 221 BC.<br><br> There are no known remains, but on the basis of a fragment of the fasti viae Ardeatinae it is agreed to have stood outside the Porta Fontinalis, probably on the saddle between the Arx and the Quirinal next to the Via Flaminia.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.484102,41.894574,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Fortunae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine of Fortuna, Rome.<br><br>Shrine of Fortuna, on the Vicus Longus, believed to be founded by Servius Tullius. Plutarch describes the cult site both as an altar and as a shrine; it probably comprised an altar surrounded by an enclosure wall. The exact site of the shrine along the Vicus Longus is unknown, as is the Latin translation of the Greek epithet ‘Εὔελπις’ (bonae spei, bene sperans, bona, felix, and tutela have all been proposed).]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-E65100</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.490695,41.899567,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Iani Quirini (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gybc8ZE4cvd1Sk_gRGDJiWOYUPpKpegdyUWcppYdHSJElPKw9bulNdpQlbvcf7gvfsnr5XNzix7nEz3DJwhP-bFBt8kaDTUyt80__FRE2SRi5IpD05bpeZDHSSmjrnxch-cEa9usKEdbSwreq2x7rPmYCiw0UWzAeuT0t5uJHTrdJVn1kol1Ugp4wnnUI67wSK3WQWBNqzbaw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Shrine to Janus Quirinus, Rome.<br><br>The shrine of Ianus Quirinus, was one of the oldest monuments in the Forum; perhaps it served originally as a bridge (ianus) carrying the Sacra Via over the Cloaca Maxima. <br><br>Its precise location in the Forum in the Augustan period is unclear. Ancient sources locate the Ianus sanctuary ‘at/near the bottom of the Argiletum’ and ‘in front of the doors’ of the Curia Iulia. It is probably to be identified with the small structure in brick and travertine still visible at the corner of the Basilica Paulli facing the Curia, an identification reinforced by Cozza’s location of fragment 212c of the Severan Marble Plan. <br><br>According to tradition, the doors of the Ianus Quirinus shrine were closed when Rome was at peace. Augustus boasts in his Res Gestae that these doors had been closed only twice before his time, but three times while he was princeps. The shrine is depicted on coins of Nero as a small rectangular building in ashlar masonry with double doors and no visible roof.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
