      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Victoriae Virginis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to Victoria Virgo, Rome.<br><br>Small shrine to Victoria Virgo which stood on the SW Palatine between the temples of Magna Mater and Victoria. Dedicated on August 1, 193 BC by M. Porcius Cato, the shrine shares its dies natalis with the adjacent aedes of Victoria.<br><br>The brick ruins visible today date from the Hadrianic period; below these are incomplete late-Republican foundations for a rectangular room which has been identified as the aedicula of Victoria Virgo based upon their date and literary sources that place Cato’s shrine in close proximity to the Palatine Temple of Victory. While the Republican shrine was certainly much smaller than the Hadrianic building, its full dimensions are not preserved.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.48518,41.889632,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Vici (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyFRWywJN-smJIedXYPK9WOGf-69Vvf9QrOgwp5OjGuAUdsuh_LTUsbI2iypRZFUccuCyTG8Kir3bm70gc8BLhrkokp1HzAYCsaPHRV5bVKrdTYdSdLq5gWMwNhvDLIDKX0tnixufcsdYQEG1h2njOlkuUOoYtgK6P7crgYmVVnxp-bdOOeJFz4nUJayDLHuTBar2opEcnkHg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Shrine to the Vicus, Rome.<br><br>Compital shrine of an unnamed Esquiline neighborhood near the Clivus Suburanus, discovered in 1888 in situ near S. Martino ai Monti.<br><br> An isolated structure surrounded by street pavement, the compitum was refurbished in 10 BC with an inscribed marble statue base for Mercury behind a marble-revetted podium (c. 4 x 5 m), perhaps for an aedicular shrine; an older, altar-like structure of travertine behind the statue base was carefully preserved in the course of these building measures.<br><br> According to its inscription, Augustus dedicated the statue in 10 BC from the collected monies submitted to him in his absence by the Roman people on New Year’s day; this closely follows Suetonius’ report of Augustus’ practice in the case of neighborhoods ‘such as Apollo Sandalarius, Iuppiter Tragoedus, and others’. Due to its archaeological context, the reported modalities of its realization, and its secure date several years before the introduction of the Regiones Quattuordecim in 7 BC, the monument is of singular importance for the incipient Augustan transformation of Rome’s vicus-neighborhoods and their compital cults.<br><br> Unfortunately, the name of this vicus is not mentioned in the inscription and remains unknown.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gw0EtMLjkGCjyZ4wVgq6Rgp2tfY_dlWvgMJ5Oi95qLCaLNy96ixbfdxe1eDOS8r4-xFCcPg2adsjPqU7X1hDsRd9aFBMM9KfjEAsI1yRVie8sbkEKdQnQWUwXOYsm-3wmSYzcIM73PRPDDdQrl779-Fi5Q65FHZG0LQ3DLbK8w_X7yFbXup5C6aoNa3Y-lWiCQHSoeDVawElQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyFRWywJN-smJIedXYPK9WOGf-69Vvf9QrOgwp5OjGuAUdsuh_LTUsbI2iypRZFUccuCyTG8Kir3bm70gc8BLhrkokp1HzAYCsaPHRV5bVKrdTYdSdLq5gWMwNhvDLIDKX0tnixufcsdYQEG1h2njOlkuUOoYtgK6P7crgYmVVnxp-bdOOeJFz4nUJayDLHuTBar2opEcnkHg?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gw0EtMLjkGCjyZ4wVgq6Rgp2tfY_dlWvgMJ5Oi95qLCaLNy96ixbfdxe1eDOS8r4-xFCcPg2adsjPqU7X1hDsRd9aFBMM9KfjEAsI1yRVie8sbkEKdQnQWUwXOYsm-3wmSYzcIM73PRPDDdQrl779-Fi5Q65FHZG0LQ3DLbK8w_X7yFbXup5C6aoNa3Y-lWiCQHSoeDVawElQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
