      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Vici Aesculatorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to the Vicus Aesculati, Rome.<br><br>The Augustan-era compital shrine of the Vicus Aesc(u)leti, whose marble altar and travertine pavement were found in situ during late-19th c. construction work in Via Arenula, SW of the Circus Flaminius near the Tiber, where the shrine faced an ancient NW-SE thoroughfare.<br><br> Both the altar and its pavement carry dedicatory inscriptions of the vicomagistri ‘of the 9th year’; thus they probably date from c. 2 AD. The compital aedicula, remains of which are also preserved, seems to have been restored under Domitian. The findspot of the compital sanctuary firmly anchors the location of the Vicus Aesc(u)leti neighborhood, and suggests that the thoroughfare in front of it be identified as the neighborhood’s eponymous street.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.475547,41.892134,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Vici Cornicularii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to the Vicus Cornicularius, Rome.<br><br>Inscriptions belonging to the marble compital aedicula (shrine) of the vicus Cornicularius were found in the course of foundation work for the Nuova Esattoria at the foot of the Oppian’s S slope. <br><br>The oldest inscription dates from 22 AD and lists the magistri of the vicus ‘in the 27th year’, which means that this vicus was established in 6/5 BC; a second inscription, which dates between A.D. 53 and 55, records the location of the vicus in Regio III. While the precise findspot within the lot of the Esattoria has not been documented, the inscriptions were “evidently found in situ”, thus the area of the vicus-neighborhood can be approximated with some certainty.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.495674,41.889958,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sacellum Vici Salutis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shrine to the Vicus Salutis, Rome.<br><br>Compital shrine marking the intersection of the Alta Semita and Vicus Salutis, restored by Agrippa while aedile in 33 BC. <br><br>The findspot of this fragmentary inscription within S. Maria Maddalena suggests the location of the crossroads and indicates that this portion of the Quirinal had a residential function in the Augustan era. This shrine is of especial importance as it establishes a firm association between its location and the toponym ‘Salutis’ which allows the collis Salutaris, the Temple of Salus and the Porta Salutis to be placed nearby.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487793,41.899593,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tarentum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Dis and Proserpina, Rome<br><br>Sacred precinct enclosing the subterranean altar of Dis and Proserpina, located at the extreme NW periphery of the Campus Martius.<br><br> Stressing its vicinity to the Tiber is Servius, who identifies the Tarentum with a part of the river itself. Ovid mentions the Vada Tarenti, which was probably the original ford where the Via Tecta crossed the river in the NW Campus Martius to continue N as the Via Triumphalis. The sharp turn that the Euripus takes in the NW Campus Martius before entering the Tiber has been interpreted as an attempt to preserve the topographic integrity of the pre-existing sanctuary of the Tarentum.<br><br>Since no ancient evidence mentions a temple dedicated to Dis and Proserpina in Rome, it seems that only the altar of Dis and Proserpina was located inside the Tarentum; like other subterranean altars, it was a sacred location in itself and did not need a temple. Most probably the Tarentum consisted of an open paved area.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.465359,41.900788,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6tZfaS6X4z5TXfJ3Tkz_EXSkrG4M9mzKDyB5hH366Zvk1gyRqk4vYBatL87oklvUiKzsrY9CCCh4h1qpBWePndxsE55uU9XuOkbNQp8b4xpPiY4ha11pXaoiigt9FZt27PJuWaySgaWNz_OEjJes9dQiEJRZQoFyrB6xgv6jMsLoUZ9WR2DzqSIfyE4YlsfCEmbZd36ak2j5IwFU6DoNxYHxtgzGSKBrxxaY_CR6mW_sd_x2LtVklfs7bvsAyT4ORSrWf8miuKrSBb1qnnrRVwMyCTF-6l7_A2p_tXc4Au8ivmgOCY2JLt3-T9usCL9VMtbUiZxceWqqFXpDp6elxjxWS91WR93WIV2ZOSH7afyj9HaAVwP9ZusNVqbdzE1d9fV-paoyAdsJVUmyw_o9qJTyCz1DgmnYskq-cwcMma4nF-WeBnfobdrZeLppaUr5XDA-0YYRSN6Gfob41hqpwmpzcd6YcD5LNjHqYqmG7fu1qFvkerFGwNjvPw14oT-7ZheUl15FhZGsxKhWTrV_cB78nV-jh4v969rukhds94qJEYxaa5nbFAsbgi7GjCKpG2H25lmv7MXlFs1KuIqMCPP2h0zTQPlWEDsY572DY5kyUDjqrn5RHW0Bq9no1KzLgcmjXwCdU_MRldw_fQyBf73iVt369A8kKoWvl_9ZcsxEwVGGREO1fehSGT4FiCArfvYk6l04hhdA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple, Rome.<br><br>One of the earliest of the four known temples in the sacral area near Largo Argentina, Temple A was built on virgin soil c. 250-225 BC.<br><br> First erected as a small prostyle, tetrastyle shrine in Grotta Oscura tufa (9.5 x 16 m on a podium H. 4 m.), the temple was expanded and elaborated in the 2nd c. BC probably to bring it in line with the style of Temple C. At this point, the building was faced with Monteverde tufa, expanded to a hexastyle prostyle temple, and received a fine peperino altar (12 x 14.5 m) built over the old staircase. In its final modification, during the third quarter of the 1st c. BC, the building acquired a new tufa podium (15 x 27.5 m) and a ring of 30 Anio tufa columns with Attic bases and Corinthian capitals. The column shafts were coated with white stucco to appear as marble. <br><br>In the 12th c., this temple was incorporated into the Church of S. Nicola dei Caesarini or Calcarari (‘lime-burners’), hence its fine state of preservation. This temple has been identified as the Temple of Iuturna, built by Q. Lutatius Catulus (cos. 241) after his sea-victory over Carthage, and as the Temple of Feronia vowed by L. Aemilius Papus at the battle of Telamon in 225 BC; neither hypothesis is entirely satisfactory.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6lDhH5WqfAl5N6Dx4XwT_7xHx-Q2Ztu8P8U-4OeXbjz1Q98ylTsn_CU6QduJMySFFVLWVoFTHZe6-BPhX4VGnq0H5EXvClYoGUUta0loV9TOI1QrczZQ1OO7MQxVV3OPdLTNpZMLo8r8m0NDra1-zLkvQCoZHk5M8Poubcd1hrPFZkt0NO6zLXn2UkRLdYvZBYhP2nR1o6Hel6U1gLhVc8-tZh6WCmKBO-vKBtwtt4f2iNHsWMq_trPYtCfMPnCd7Xw6Pr6Mdl0X2vHQG9P5WNH-9JnO7_V45mkQh7JJ0y8O6qhVunJwMknZAeoN4fDLCrLseNP49scjrmSzjTsVog9rSD9NT_HXqMUzFs1iSgz0Bgf05ZuvUmZWLY2Esmt1ch0Jz9myC8EXNkQ0YQc01XZ-wuJhLXjZsjXt-k9djly00B5w3uJ31DUs3wSCMTQOQBVyiRdHrVF5Fjpgnx4N1pRdiFT9voSmylduE9MBKcl4kvzHo5LCMkmOZunu3W7oAW2ZNfbJoGASvjhH8OPtdf3MyOWS2yI6XzVxLH21nxI2RJ70w-4dN3tzUrMDADc1RVG1UCMT756Y_dPxCzKK2N1_UWTx0lOougK6C0QNy71y5ici5GeL_3LDrq4yeTdbrqEzzzVwcfNcr7y642swTmlHL__YXZCIePW7oqb4l_2HNSfjYCNJJuzfKQBvgVwBC_QkxvJGGnbQ" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
