      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4Wcctvh-kq7K803F8VnP_AATUSgIKy68AzbQ20K-BsS12QDN4Uv3utY6AtZm-pydFrW26jhgrsUwq_PjCwlRhgUnYOFPXp2GFDcnVafOMuET0kzYikiRXbJqzigXcuOJKCeFwffuqwHBXL2O_PfM30HvZOa1RI_fQIYyeTp1GEyB_jZHenOFs0PzriqVCFMQB2PEKjiahb7mJqp4l3gBBH-7TbvFQP4FJWmBw6tbVxo382TMhzHd4J499pYaEvfwDkGue9LEpJsMjJUfW519wIj2FkSwZioXUud1TGis-ULRK8gXklTKm7Pchqtps-U1FVHvLrTqTWd4iQksccjcNVnHYveiIjRHYQV4FK3E4hb4wfdViS42gjw10hxECql0GLDpxKy7KsBdjt_OvZuGq6HxWg1cKCQAOZ-tlh4_WD8x43sIre3IvSnWcvub4GlqA9ALOvku_ON3dE4lP0fm_OvW8fs6E9FwQkYJ_iTFwiyHNAIDBO2JyvH4Rdg0fWIub38DbUt0TDEVM9YaZFooe1kFFeklQWCKZLY5spZVlmUpa3r2v36veVcHgfTXnQAH7G6dIFCYfpv2TODkv8BTnFbcKClh-E6HFDcoPI9s_TVv6o7p0_5s7_BgFZIbMNjDarCrBOUBsytlOndE4TMAd1gY3CcvO2LqM67ZrsxCA0B0nL_y1TGsJlsa1KJ3ml8Z3HAVDTWvNuWg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple, Rome.<br><br>One of the four known temples in the sacral area near Largo Argentina, Temple D is the least well-known, and lies, largely unexcavated, below Via Florida. <br><br>From its earliest phase, c.200-150 BC, only a cement podium remains; during a second phase, dated to the late 2nd-early 1st c. BC, a larger cement podium (23.5 x 37 m) was built, engulfing the earlier shrine; this phase is dated by remains of the tufa pavement which unified the surrounding area at that time. The superstructure was rectangular in plan, with a colonnaded façade; fluted plaster pilasters were set into the brick-faced cella, which had a marble-covered cement floor and an unusually large marble threshold block. The superstructure, probably a Domitianic restoration, gives few clues to the building’s appearance in the Augustan era.<br><br>Temple D has been identified as the Temple of the Lares Permarini, vowed by L. Aemilius Regillus in 190 BC during a naval battle against Antiochus III, and dedicated by M. Aemilius Lepidus in 179 BC. This identification is not accepted, since the Temple of the Lares Permarini is known from the fasti Praenestini to have stood ‘within the Porticus Minucia’. Thus, Temple D remains without a solid identification.]]></description>
