      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Antonini et Faustinae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6QS-HUP_O71IaelZ4R26o40W1ciEgPojaf-ARkQ2U0GuXQKJfNC3YQgBS0obbpc3h4hXJ1AyRfox3J98sqJ8fU5tVMTO_pQhvc1rk4OvFf1SjxskchkBH36YdrbM3IEhJmNx4B9K02tBUqA0caIO5YGpZ-ip5pAEOtEeJA0crByH6SdJrOKpsvo4nfV8MmOASdmYv7pTrBuk9xDv_nUvZ1cPUosI5Raq_5hE2c9JG52Qg2Q01zVDLn8AejGiEEtJQsdlNX41U4zlKpy6ys94JCy5KDPrwV7oFS-QVZpdpxlAUY_1TQV-jvptw9tuCRVVfNRw1HIQrRayqJCWLmYUjFzBL3BG2zgNWKbSUOPm02c38JP5Su2sIlfiToWdvJyCnqeSPSGL9p244E_elJAdwIZXMOu6o9vdsfBUj0RcEsn5Zn5B9ict8vvn0uQZux7xUq0UDGVUPyzvOUghIqF6tccJARae0rMz1HaUwnpQKINAd1iCZMrbTQwRPQ6T6mP00KBxSLvE37R5qi-GOlTzVw-DayU9HwSD3HYp3LAGpgxKQqG4Wbw27VGPH4-hJFMc0spC3pk4tBkTh-u3pPAptIWwmcqtXeNh-FUDaTwPLqwFayEcWigas_CDSZiYC7uISgS9WXkK7AZ9OZe-cQggHYelQ8LYTPUQdEFz_b9AWQxqbb6hmAJzQDrrfgTmdfQoDxpaQgiePzeQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Antoninus and Faustina, Rome.<br><br>The temple was constructed by the Emperor Antoninus Pius, beginning in 141 AD. It was initially dedicated to his deceased and deified wife, Faustina the Elder. Because of this, Faustina was the first Roman empress with a permanent presence in the Forum Romanum.<br><br> When Antoninus Pius was deified after his death in 161 AD, the temple was re-dedicated to both Antoninus and Faustina by his successor, Marcus Aurelius.<br><br>The building stands on a high platform of large grey peperino tufa blocks. The later of two dedicatory inscriptions says, "Divo Antonino et Divae Faustinae Ex S.C." meaning, “For the divine Antoninus and for the divine Faustina, by decree of the Senate.”<br><br>The eight monolithic Corinthian columns of its pronaos are 17 metres (56 ft) in height. The rich bas-reliefs of the frieze under the cornice, featuring griffins, acanthus scrolls, and candelabra, were often copied from the sixteenth through the nineteenth centuries.<br><br>Based on numismatic evidence, the temple was originally fenced off from the Via Sacra and a large, seated statue of Faustina would have been inside of the cella. Fragments of this statue and one of Antoninus Pius, which was added later, were discovered in front of the Temple.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gymi_Izn51AtG8q1eJqcaLmgIrBA3Ng1rhEDyZnocBTmi7L-5bEn-DbO7jIIdq2tzVhFpbkHaXm7gbMK63oJTZ8nbhhZVDHUwhlhZPtmvdETwDEC-MXonCI_Awb3DOzx_2cPWlz7Ly5Sl4JFFHOGxoy-Cz4K204vpjW5SquIBgzGuNWX-ttB3-cJHD43dE7ShgUObRxLuL_SA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GweOsmfREWEyHVUola9QSz3KJB2ldzwFBGza42-PXqDgJ9cBdmjP8gl1BNtAxr3mTZtBssM89-VP7ULRbZE1oEcpjpf_jpthgsgIDZYhTki1LAO2RpbE7YXkmgDhLPAPwgSJJaTtMS0m1RzqQg97leQ2dg3IDdUYGR6fKeIK02-r0hwIDWiosV4zEsh5sYjm9pdVBFRh4hePQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
