      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Apollonis Palatini (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7MVbEZAF0f2Rc5wI1a3ZuAbCueB3qSKkjJcshbeTk7IiR0T6JcNcrqVlZb4Ihf_WS_lV9SK6D9OsyA0mXr8qjz_Wio6vtFhvgPyHytYBfd-BnmoUllOMEIzENDlLZUk8gFvQGWk9IeCslsnEOWEhYH2ZJcLutxkbdilCiWL0wPKQcZK8Gw4Z9Ztz-zQ501Z_qiw7l1Kvkk6la4mCcfg7GIJUqijDIm4mZ950nQEWy40rmmROzPlxgv8lWy7emUBPCrSQm683SmnlMiu3mPLFjq27k86lcMQXJ3vNAiRzaz2U2Ox0g4ybOVfYF6OMlWFlRAr5g0j7075weTm-t1XNLCgv1K104WzvdwD-IFL3PKKIe_SvUMKlOKdcUksRlrs823oR6vKz6zRzWjkyoFzZ1t32TeDgLWPjivmtgmj_WCn3T4RjZWwqSnVn-TqOTplyfNvw5utASo_RicnLKroyMcz0OoZMweIeZmOZANAFfkdKbkPYQW5YhVCTH46QJVAxiq8DHYVbUQmQr-pRa8fXsNrDTF4UvyUHKZUM2j8-eK7VDA_4xGyhMCyqpTbDCAtrxlQx2UW8rjRf1FNORPDlnXbCYndnK-8iUW4NsU5ZJp5TWfutadlSwrtYfYqkp0NVKwdliqa5fnAQlHT2AJeNoTFMl0nCLlhIcB5NwgzEu4xD-4DjYXYUkJtcZ7ruhUmk5r0abZw6MYFw" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Apollo Palatinus, Rome.<br><br>Temple of Apollo built on the SW Palatine by Augustus and considered one of his most magnificent buildings. <br><br>Construction probably began in 36 BC following Octavian’s victory at Naulochus over Sextus Pompey and was soon mirrored by C. Sosius, who rebuilt the Temple of Apollo Medicus around 34 BC. After the battle at Actium in 31 BC, Augustus’ temple became an ex voto of the victory of Octavian over Marc Antony and was dedicated on 9 October 28 BC.<br><br>Tradition holds that the temple was built on land Augustus had intended for his residence, but consecrated to Apollo following the interpretation of the haruspices (soothsayers) after the spot had been struck by a thunderbolt.<br><br>The temple is hexastyle, pseudo-peripteral, and has an almost square cella (20.5 x 19 m). Elevated on a high podium of opus caementicium and opus quadratum of tufa and travertine, the temple was reached by a lengthy staircase. The opus quadratum superstructure was constructed entirely of Carrara (Luna) marble. The early-Augustan date suggested by the literary evidence is supported by the archaeological finds; for instance, a Corinthian column fragment dates stylistically to the beginning of the Augustan era. Further, either the temple or the Portico of the Danaids was adorned with polychrome terracotta relief plaques dating to 36-28 BC. The pediment was decorated with Archaic Greek statues made of Parian marble attributed to the 6th c. BC Chian sculptors Bupalos and Athenis. Additional decoration is attested in Propertius , who reports that the central acroteria represented the chariot of Helios, and that the double doors of the cella were decorated with ivory reliefs depicting the Celtic attack on Delphi and the myth of the Niobids. The altar of the temple may have been located to the S in the Area Apollinis, where the Severan Marble Plan preserves an image of a cruciform monument.<br><br>The temple formed an integral part of a group of Augustan buildings on the SW Palatine, which included his own residence, an open terraced area framed by porticoes, and the Greek and Latin Library. Excavations revealed that the temple was situated just E of Augustus’ residence which physically embraced it on the N and W, while on a lower S terrace private vaulted ramps connected the domus to the level of the sanctuary. The temple and its surrounding buildings formed a complex overlooking the Circus Maximus that emulated the Hellenistic palace-sanctuary scheme exemplified in Attalid Pergamon.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6j9DV7NorCF97ln1vgHtHxAauChIVZDfJahjE7BW-HiNVukRQlPHATbahl5n9UP4LcIPsra98BaPiwZTm-kQCIb959nxwp5_sdxOc8eWqa3wIOk6pC7gdyuIwJ91Mojp_PtvSgk5Pc1M04Em0k7sswkgGYj8rnwW61R6lns0rX1LxZfHQPJ314ESRSag4vMMKp20cEcY_luqmWKUT0hhBFC0ZlysJPAFG0CXfXJJVpsFW78lh9VTJLL0djAauuVUx5OLvsLDqHwbYUZ-dbOUUlVLCB3NColw4ng8T06KyYGF1av0lNqn_TofOPcx1p4X23btlT990IO8ksCfmHM5c2JizUPGcy8E61Iuh5560peP3ZhA1UNy9hsbQcxOtb_Al8x0lxcpYrE79edLDrWpZmwkL1k6C346Yt-v8JHsD_rc975NonDpa6L9bTRvpTGAol8cA5o3gEr4k5lfJQ46cIFA5vdaspY_gYUhHrLKbPTO46NrdQVzf0mE9A51geuKeYm1IAJNCGPtOMPgJXX25ieCIOsSl6WeOsGrvE