      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Bellonae Pulvinensis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Bellona Pulvinensis, Rome.<br><br>Sanctuary of the Cappadocian goddess Ma, who was assimilated to Bellona in Rome, located inside the Porta Collina and known only from epigraphic and literary evidence. <br><br>The goddess Bellona of this temple was known by two epithets: PVLVINENSIS and PEDISEQVA. Palmer plausibly argues that the temple was dedicated by L. Cornelius Sulla to commemorate his victory over the Samnites at the Colline Gate in 82 BC. Martial complains that the noisy racket of an inspired throng of Bellona’s worshippers on the Quirinal deprived him of sleep.<br><br>Inscriptions uncovered during the 1872 excavations for the Ministero delle Finanze locate the Vicus Bellonae near the Porta Collina, and it is likely that the temple stood near the eponymous neighborhood. The shrine was located within a grove and placed on a pulvinus, “a terraced embankment with building and plantings”, near the Porta Collina and Campus Sceleratus.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-A52714</styleUrl>
