      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Bellonae Rufiliae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Bellona Rufilia, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of Bellona Rufilia is attested on the tombstone set up by C. Calidius Custos for L. Cornelius Ianuarius, who is described as a ‘devotee of the Temple of Bellona Rufilia in the region of Isis and Serapis’. <br><br>It is argued that the deceased was a descendant of a freedman of L. Cornelius Sulla, who had been put in charge of a temple first erected by P. Cornelius Rufinus, the last of Sulla’s ancestors to hold the consulship, after his triumph over the Samnites in 290 BC. The inscription mentions ISIS ET SERAPIS, the temple from which Regio III: Isis et Serapis took its name; the Temple of Bellona Rufilia may thus be located close by. Since the inscription was found in the Villa Palombara, just E of the Servian Wall, it seems best to place this temple near the Wall, between the Iseum and the road leading out of the Colosseum Valley (“Via Tusculana”).]]></description>
