      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Castoris et Pollucis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4pyGOoBh2SHdqNBtV7P4F3JaEgfDP3z4Hxm8XFCXPxn8XBZEnHdpxevf6dWgB8fVqqRMrhMAkVLNNgghO2PROy1wbKdH9Y7Y2L3dTKV_eQ9x_pzJjxFP7G_2wHvbUmlUHFbeWhf90nwtMGQKsxAyLts5fkStqrOV0kmDm9Ju99xLiuzbdoFt6MZjBGbCMkeUMNF_0FLcVPIk0OzHsJWTdA5eY1K8mh158ojggo9i9jx4UfTFuDR2YquRBcVpM0BgtgZA2eryz5w6zP_oi2e0JUBv4b48bjYc1hG23wQCdq1hFFmryAwZUg-3prfaze0mb0RGKCCRLj5Op1JnIqQ_f33dTdESUr4d2xpxcgVCxZekUAEOOe05AzrOEAyhelXgoFfLwwHCASAVVeAuSF4gfyqcERZfW9CZiRwVsgWVJLfLPBcw-jipLSUBT_wV9FtQeKED18RBJOKfHNzMYZEWdjnY6KZw8aIovCJKZLez0fRawbnPA99V0XHgdcYl7y47xsypeuKXwR7XbwDxXYfTihFXnymyUhyfofV_usRipeXqriKdPxCm3WuwYDu_1U6O1a8WoXS5w8MQKtVvJv7Hqi_CYmc8Re97QykTXAM4MJXVwAdeiaF9iKRYQHDhDwwK4mA4OIY2Nyx-r1eUf2AfvpNPResQktG-72t6TVreLNpE0937Cou6zAfbo4lpTaKkxj6y4mFKqB" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Castor and Pollux, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of Castor on the S side of the Forum, between the Basilica Iulia and the Lacus Iuturnae, dedicated in 484 BC by the son of the dictator Postumius who, according to Roman tradition, had vowed the temple at the battle of Lake Regillus in 493 BC.<br><br> The temple was rebuilt by L. Caecilius Metellus Delmaticus in 117 BC, and Cicero notes that Verres made certain repairs to it. The Metellan temple may have burned in the fire of either 14 or 9 BC, but the sources do not record this; in any case the temple was completely rebuilt by Tiberius and dedicated in the names of Tiberius and his dead brother Drusus in 6 AD.<br><br>Caligula converted the temple into a vestibule for his palace, an arrangement which Claudius eradicated, and Domitian is credited with a restoration, but there are no further traces of major renovations and scholars agree that the existing remains are mainly Augustan.<br><br>The temple, which was octastyle and peripteral in its Augustan phase, was approached by one frontal and two lateral staircases; in the Augustan period the platform to which the lateral staircases provided access served as a speaker’s platform. At a later date this arrangement was modified and the staircase became a single frontal flight, as shown on the Severan Marble Plan.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5xgiIJCf9owMiTBI3uYniB_GTbDpIvrJTStM8M9BP_IWTtICFS_7LCkgSsTwzsxI8d6MrDvkfm5RCCj7Uwf_2wAuoz0Yq-K5teS0LjWzrggZrnpcKk39MZPCXiYEytnhJ5E3sExnF2DhyfbxQrhjFWNolWGy0HU2zIjsyFf9ZlKT4uxDLxhRnloIfHoViEanhYqLyjpa4VjT67T0_a0ady5vCwzvbr6VNHtKQLBRSm5PInNxhWYm2f5t-lHkRtOyytU_-ij6npIQQCz5WmCCqDK1FtMzrmSNSUlztsC6qCtBMV3cpTr34ROxJ55kkBlqwV5B5v8o9gmvH97_8CD-9LOHMHKzy7GLELusUwVeHOgfg2pST_2e1l0wGFB9IbjC54Ec-uweONiG-xwtCYoIovpf0kouXmryGV57MIk1kfwNRWaoB8sOEv8Zp5n671AzJoo0pjoDj1LK-XoMg7L2JWac4gGpG9MnrXZjCbYk05lFRtwYD31oAi9msLgyf9Ef7EsrGNvdSd_E3YjkUVY5NlyoUAQJxtl-1_TRwoCunqQYK9TJsrIZlrncisuRjUpO0W6eGqlYgb3pAkl_CC2GCzwBo2vK4P-gj6H8ddwvh74uyDdR7SqVR2xhY1RhdQgGzN4chssP8Vs6dulbmIOxKg48sp5Pfnl0ZON2wr8yEm6nV7Bjv1No0vX83e8CKEKy-T1_QlSr5gZg" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
