      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Cereris, Libri Patris et Liberae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Ceres, Liber Pater and Libera, Rome.<br><br>Joint temple to Ceres, Liber Pater, and Libera, usually abbreviated to ‘aedes Cereris’, located close by the Circus Maximus and near the temples of Flora and Luna; no physical remains have been identified. <br><br>Reputed for its image of Ceres, the ‘first bronze image of a divinity at Rome’, and for the exquisite artworks displayed there, the temple also served as the headquarters of the plebeian aediles and housed their archive, with copies of the Senate’s decrees. In addition, the temple possessed the asylum Cereris, a place of refuge where bread was distributed to the poor.<br><br> Thus, the Temple of Ceres formed a defining place of plebeian self-identification. The building had a strikingly old-fashioned appearance and Vitruvius cites it as the primary example of the Tuscan style soon before it was destroyed by fire in 31 BC. Remarkably, Augustus only began its rebuilding (along with the nearby Temple of Flora, and the Temple of Ianus at the Forum Holitorium), and its rededication did not take place before 17 AD, which means that this important sanctuary remained both a ruin and a construction site throughout the Augustan period.]]></description>
