      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Dianae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Diana, Rome.<br><br>Temple vowed during the Ligurian war in 187 BC and dedicated in 179 BC by M. Aemilius Lepidus in conjunction with his Temple to Iuno Regina, both located in circo Flaminio. <br><br>Unfortunately, neither the remains nor the exact location of the Temple of Diana have been positively identified. Augustus does not mention restoring the Aemilian shrine in his Res Gestae, though it could have been among the 82 buildings he restored but did not name individually. <br><br>Coarelli postulates an Augustan restoration of Diana’s shrine based on aurei of c. 29-27 BC which depicts Diana on the obverse and a small distyle, prostyle Tuscan shrine with a naval trophy in the cella on the reverse. Coarelli equates the numismatic depiction of this shrine with one of the two small, square sacella depicted in the exedra behind the Theatrum Marcelli on the Severan Marble Plan.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-097138</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.47914,41.891487,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Dianae Aventinae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Diana Aventina, Rome.<br><br>Temple precinct of Diana Aventina in the Lauretum on the Aventine, the main sanctuary of the goddess in Rome and a place of strong plebeian self-representation. <br><br>Allegedly founded by the slave-born Servius Tullius, the sanctuary was once the assembly-place of the Latin cities under Rome’s leadership and also a notable asylum; its festival on the Ides of August continued to be the holiday of slaves. Further, the sanctuary served as an important archive for legislation; a bronze pillar, still standing in the Augustan period, included the Icilian law of the mid-5th c. BC de Aventino publicando which provided allotments of Aventine land to the plebs.<br><br>No remains of this temple have been identified, yet the building and its precinct are represented on fragment 22 of the Severan Marble Plan, next to the Temple of Minerva. <br><br>Under the Augustan program of ‘adorning the city’, the temple was rebuilt or restored by L. Cornificius. Since the name DIANA CORNIF. is attested independently, the label CORNIFICIA[... on the Marble Plan clearly refers to Diana Cornificia or Cornificiana and thus establishes the identification. The footprint of the octostyle podium temple shown on the 3rd c. AD fragment is presumably still that of Cornificius’ building; it is surrounded by a precinct with a double-colonnaded portico along its flank.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-097138</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.48173,41.883972,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Dianae Plancianae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Diana Planciana, Rome.<br><br>Republican temple of Diana Planciana on the collis Latiaris of the Quirinal; its name and probable location are known from inscriptions commemorating an AEDITVMVS (temple keeper) of the sanctuary. <br><br>The temple has been linked to Cn. Plancius (curule aedile in 55 or 54 BC), an affiliation possibly supported by the appearance of Diana in his coinage. L. Munatius Plancus is another possible donour for the temple. Either way, the temple was certainly erected before Augustus ended the senatorial tradition of eponymous temple dedications.<br><br>The temple was located near the intersection of Via Panisperna and Salita del Grillo, where fragments of an archaistic female statue, perhaps the cult statue, were recovered along with the two inscriptions. Since the travertine architectural remains found on this site have been lost we must still be cautious, but the mention of imperial freedmen as AEDITVI suggests that the cult site was of some size.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-097138</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487547,41.89604,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Dii Fidii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Dius Fidius, Rome.<br><br>Temple of Dius Fidius, the god of oaths and treaties, located on the Quirinal near the Porta Sanqualis and Temple of Quirinus.<br><br> It was believed to have been built by Tarquinius Priscus and later dedicated by Spurius Postumius Albinus, though no remains of the temple survive; the structure would have been open to the sky, given the nature of the deity. An inscription found near S. Silvestro allows the sanctuary to be located in the vicinity of the church.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
