      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Divi Iulii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v62QzS4FBCR57WtLyMslIIOwhWstoruElVjWab1gfZlFWN5gTid2lMMrpqQXsR4yKo1HEsJg3GUqm4bZ9tLItWY6HoB16n0H009sXrWeuZsZ0keVBlrmbv3YXgQCTBGBXNHTYeEJa0Yr2H4U_NIdC-OpQDRD1k23BB0vqEXRHw7ub4W4BKIfnr8YW4I6g8Ql4bAlLz_S37ZI5giwA-QjH_qYiISSc1SiKSViyejLTA_d2kz80rdUnOczEOWcdUNWLK0GDXF9hogc96Cro9LogDcY5JhJ2XnnbwYcgwV1ASiURrN2gcB-381B-RK27w8jksRGnUJx-Y0mbs2bHEOEDBXeLYSutuliM5I4XkzjKgSdmWyMwmI_o--dLzD14t70VMgl3fLT-fgW7WgnMl6KiOrH_rAt0XPxIByRf-iaCR4Wa1C93UF2JK3CcHNJuC9SB2RX2DDT9CGCvS6JY-K_yajUczxwpH6crQn5DLBZKndmzUyQXWb3ar_q9-k8e7D17Q0mWum6-fwXhaYR2Oo9kAisgi4TpRWtuhnjmouP2h95NCMkcstCKGmwOUvzCdsVLc6QFQq5I6w0hUGA2z0YPkuiLxfpxk43aVQ6ouVLZ0Tbnc9tt-C2NUEb414uZDZa2c6hAZIPn_qBpPiadNTX65iLm5pn_G4do4UzGwE4rR8ry1SDvVTm9NykAVor2HKh5p8ZE7tcPHn" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of the Divine Julius, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of the Deified Julius Caesar on the E side of the Forum. Erected on the site where the people had burned Caesar’s corpse, the temple was begun in 42 BC by the Triumvirs and completed by Octavian. It was dedicated on 18 August 29 BC. <br><br>The temple stood on a platform c. 3.5 m high to which lateral ramps on either side of the structure provided access. These ramps, which continued as arcuated porches along the sides of the structure and along the NE wall of the Regia, have been identified as the porticus Iulia . The ramps may have been connected behind the temple by means of a cryptoporticus. A semicircular exedra in the center of the platform contains the remains of a small circular structure, probably the altar built by Octavian to replace the original altar and column marking the site of Caesar’s funeral pyre (which the consul P. Cornelius Dolabella had removed in 42 BC. This exedra was subsequently walled off, an action Coarelli connects with Augustus’ decision to restrict the temple’s right of asylum by denying access to the altar. Most scholars identify the platform on which the temple stood as the rostra aedis Divi Iulii, also known as the rostra Iulia.<br><br> The combination of temple and speaker’s platform was very common, however, and the plan for the Temple of Divus Julius seems to have been modelled closely on that of the Temple of Venus Genetrix.<br><br>The temple itself rose on a second platform, approached by a frontal staircase. Very little of the temple has survived, but extensive excavations of 1950-54 uncovered most of the structure’s ground-plan.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5tmvHAR1daq7DSw6CbdgwLSWjzwx6lf2QPXNyHz-dFCXnARJPyxtnm1hVeAr1eSzqvmo6J1lnBp2BVs-kWBwmrQzQrUhrvX1-xsVvZ0fBN2UqIsutougdlzf5y34detiGrc_zbnhMTEdzPgmqjY0jO7VbI3RP-QFcJ473ys0UXaU_WQcqxPxwD7vcg0bWFudDElzxSq2ie3-OcERPJM8kXdy1HKAmf8TnDm-gaOF3JXRv-Vm9gwqMKLsj69Zg-T4npziKi5ee_hxuZOeAwlYye5tOsTLLJSRdduQKOyF3qhaBG-8sGGdlC8mnudfNJnbSJOZh5dt5wWvIXCsMxxnNddnQ4Wj1NUvdjws4bJydsDcuf9d6GVOL6Q_rSh3R96t6s5Dt8I2fXtIAefrilLDDJjtyTnBq4sKbFMtszhe93ngnEZwuVN62StnjTjgC_uRVH-hGSRBZ0UVL2fNxF9IbBxm9tcXpBZy3ME8AeAfCNWsOOGD0NRi6JTsukO8FMLFnKe78luhT6ZBrmj21siKoalnE9dvIFVBuNk5PhYOyWuHT70lYNPkotq6BzuLIZqs6Hei_4xEk9rWlhSFz_HZLIk_oDrXT6POZoo5dRly-xuJvYAVVzJZ0w_i86DYYI0xQ3_Xn3vfEHbEmW5gUzdm0CaAa1XuPrjp5GgcVMFicL70p2zN_mqAmhlznpwI1ynbNA9WhtuyX2" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzAPJbXnthAEEaEyz0WOIUxB5AibKgAGnjumBcf8qEgRZT2RDNKwozU9j5vWUx76WpxPvSZgW3wJ4BLPsMKZHlCErNIWN8b2deZjApKW410LVLDg2Iy7ygN-rXMurfcIN7JDgyb4o59ls_uxlnc4hAYseRHcAjWDSn1ybWZgTPTqF4l0_It6LVs1fZ5a-MxdHdGmKajHcrq7A?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxqqUuYHJ0Aw1toiHbPKvncYsStfFrmF8rLqQwyTvCMrpH8wnJqjrpUKiEZuX6WiyPc-xFYC9CYs-ochRX879fVaPnOeVyaCN6M7r00r2uwNErCUM81xiwoRsLvsOi0RlpzK7SuJVXIt_PNOYGK36f4iaA2wuxa67zQlyEnAhvMFpOLhkrY6Q_9IvUZtbIrdYiWWP2rYH0HwTw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
