          <coordinates>
            12.497989,41.907464,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Fortunae Respicientis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Fortuna Respiciens, Rome.<br><br>Sanctuary of Fortuna Respiciens (‘Solicitous Fortune’) located on the E slopes of the Palatine.<br><br> The cult site is associated with a 2nd c. BC terracotta pediment depicting the goddess seated on an altar. This pediment was recovered in the late 1800s along the Via di S. Gregorio, between the Claudian-Neronian aqueduct and the Portale del Vignola (now the E entrance to the Palatine archaeological site) at a level predating the fire of 64 AD.<br><br> Though no building remains can be associated with the pedimental sculptures (which indicate a pediment of some size: 15-16 m across, 2 m high), the temple was surely in the area of its findspot, as the region was known as the vicus Fortunae Respicientis by 2nd c. AD, and the 4th-c. Regionary Catalogues list Fortuna Respiciens between the Curiae Veteres (at the NE corner of the Palatine) and the Severan Septizodium (at the SE corner of the Palatine).]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-E65100</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.489962,41.888475,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Hadriani (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7s6qTWGKokteFdjzXjcxD87EVSom908ayyoprzFjBe66Ky2l8le7dsyg_VQSb9XoVazHkmv9dQIX4q7Gl2yehvC_eNkXBfG-3qi5kmop-ZJB8GSVFf5z9WCHIpsmziUbweusnpZLOrCv02VLUAsxJId9MvVgwL1QKeVpUV6PAUrhV4e8QmArV_PJ9sOnJ2y8qXYyShOX6w_EAOceSnMiKMq99yrCaWT2fdPSnAc8U-1ugScITQuc3v5naCyyvlxaaLVj1wz5RFsrPUQlxxNa4nHS26LQhLSrEnCtrUiaENN51KYyTOiTULeQLSNe8Ev1s82WyCoBBsjWQiW55Ech_0yYTpnBJwm15VI2QYHEo6cAoEAkBVcCzQlmE6QYvCeVEjuV4QIs2ArwdOUcaOsUk1ydTJdVNKis1grT9KzAqrU7BWK0AhjM72pPcHc6wHthwafcI-aBBB7v_qwj1lCCOkfDVsTOBSrJ9iQj7AezljOsBqPqLMF-ylkijKtFJTa5iCvKdKy63Rm5HyC4N5WxMPMyK2lxsI8Zx-XvaR-PlDI-bLECpIzh19wyNo9f0F7b8JgL_OMDMp84RqdXwmVgUxD8H0o2zqjKBG3s91GwpqNFjroDbO0JfsmLm8AUIlBAyNcMQZUdjgBvIj8jT6BN6YEtJjA-jDeK37J-I8loC2X1OJ32Se5LPvVB6Dxghm_HVrz2KjQRI8rQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Hadrian, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of Hadrian was dedicated to the deified emperor Hadrian on the Campus Martius by his adoptive son and successor Antoninus Pius in 145 AD. <br><br>This temple was previously known as the Basilica of Neptune but has since been properly attributed as the Temple of Hadrian completed under Antoninus Pius. With one cella wall and eleven columns from the external colonnade surviving, the remains of the temple have been incorporated into a later building in the Piazza di Pietra (Piazza of Stone – derived from use of the temple's stones to build the piazza), whereby its facade, alongside the architrave which was reconstructed later on, was incorporated into a 17th-century papal palace by Carlo Fontana, now occupied by the Borsa Italiana, Italy's main stock exchange. While only part of the structure remains, excavations and scholarship have provided us with information regarding its construction techniques and stylistic influences, helping us recreate the building dynamics and significance of the Temple of Hadrian in Imperial Rome.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5Xw2YVDF4-ekragCApgsRc3FYBGicYc8Qy7XaZRZ1wRQDpPBSlObiQ7GUudLHmGgUeT3h0Q_FZtN97nyF6kNHXr-JreWHohTe_tM3rqkFwtxYxhL9OmV9Wn_mnC_bUFRafnXJvXryApxL8UlZYAYYYP1RruTuCuTRtIX6ujhMlXU9G_rZTkZIxVM4uzfHO44TyIdrLSeFdERbOZnaZ4SXpZKhpYhErwxb4t6-SP17OMZH04TU05kdYnJySwTPB41L4hjD1RRYW-QFHISPYwbK_UshGmwMDR8IELodR_RMJc4JwnJ6BkNaH0GREjGmBH-HHTj7xorNl4WFJCZ_hAznGKVhiSHyvrm7YFGEyo2Z3bohUSjJPlwUBjP1yk5r2QTRuw5-akFpwPCPDYQswEiS1bX8-wwqF6D8gwwiUHWYz86VzELsWBax2ezvuP7eGjE5ds78AnyK7yEqHd7m4Q6mmeoZ4u5oyRUF8U1FGa_0RyTkfo7hG34CmhuXcd8Eze4PTBKB5MmlcgeSFEhmUIIT6-Q6Be0pCkHZhc5nd1NAaGKvMUf97WWGTDg6MYbjl_PEM7f55N-LVnuYN5RCaON7vlj1z-PZT1-Z_uxajaZ0TM0VUc81Md76_SgRPtRWwolDCzv0Xl34pjOR8-FnFFYoGCJ_XUsZLcl0fyWfkp94gx86ZUqOWvNwnKJ2w45cVyp_yf7ZHe67L3w" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-673AB7</styleUrl>
