      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Hercules, Rome.<br><br>Round temple in the Forum Bovarium near S. Maria in Cosmedin, probably located north of the church. Torn down in the 15th c., it is known in some detail through reconstruction drawings of the Renaissance, which show an exterior colonnade in the Tuscan order, possibly the result of an Augustan-period renovation. <br><br>This temple may have been larger than the well-preserved Round Temple by the Tiber. Its location at the Forum Boarium near the Circus Maximus is undisputed and must have distinguished its location from the Round Temple by the Tiber, which stood apart from the Forum Boarium.<br><br> Found at this site was a bronze statue of a young Hercules and a series of praetorian inscriptions (the earliest dated to 193 AD) mostly addressed to HERCVLES INVICTVS.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.48208,41.888446,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis Cubantis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Hercules Cubans, Rome.<br><br>A small aedicular sanctuary dedicated to Hercules Cubans in Trans Tiberim, listed in the Notitia and the Curiosum for Regio XIV: Transtiberim.<br><br> In 1889, along Viale Trastevere some 600 m N of the Trastevere train station, a rock-carved aedicular shrine of Hercules was excavated along with two inscribed altars and numerous sculptures. The inscriptions on the altars and on the epistyle of the rock-cut niche mention the dedication by L. Domitius Permissus to Hercules. A series of small tufa sculptures further secures the identification of the cult with Hercules Cubans; the wider sculptural assemblage from the area suggests that the sanctuary existed from the 1st c. BC through the end of the 3rd c. AD. This area is thought to have been situated within the limits of the Horti Caesaris.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.466546,41.875977,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis Invicti (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Hercules Invictus, Rome.<br><br>A Temple to Hercules near the Circus Maximus, without identified remains, was rebuilt or restored by Pompey the Great (according to both the epithet Pompeianus used by Vitruvius and Pliny’s specification in aede Pompei Magni).<br><br> Vitruvius mentions the temple as an example of the old-fashioned, Tuscan style, so a rectangular ground-plan is certain and a rather early date for the original structure can be assumed. The temple stood by the Circus Maximus, perhaps not too far from the Temple of Ceres near the Circus’ W end, since Vitruviusdescribes both as ad circum Maximum.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.482273,41.887611,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis Magni Custodis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Hercules Magnus Custos, Rome.<br><br>Sanctuary, and probably temple, in the area of the Circus Flaminius that was dedicated to Hercules as protector of the circus.<br><br> Ovid relates that the opus was sanctioned by Sulla, and that it was located after consultation with the Sibylline Books in the opposite part of the Circus Flaminius from the Temple of Bellona. Yet Sulla may have only restored a cult site originally built during the 3rd c. BC.<br><br> An inscription found in the Circus Flaminius area attests to the existence, already in the early Republican period, of a collegium in charge of the organization of games (ludi) in honour of Hercules.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.476035,41.893683,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis Musarum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5LSd6N0Rl3jHfER8wBQ1B1WCpSQDWhRUAYC7zAxgAnNT7_qtBfNk7Wi6iecqgiMhIaa0cCZh45mxdLLEhLmzfmBNuEta3O_6iqZEhudP_7EqfD_lY9tg-Ky3TWr92ZZIlRpD4nJTwwuiZW6dZ7hIALlseifIMz1p1-UeDdWFf-d_kxALADD0sn7LN5CTnGDcvKnbiA5rakOZhOHAka8yoATlCCQANaVyI0y2BUIzwdHtbkg64RWr-Flu3T341iV3a_Vqz1WRi7j5CvjShDEXUrLcIWBd0TTOa4AaebY9l2dDECkhzzzi_hxfxHVLvNmKek0gQqpd3ji0edwKHgLe2MLuF6iPhRSgbMJjNHu0jy317wtVzLetgaXWdXpjzLDCrAA3n6NvIO3wlgT1gNN6q-UkHNmQF-hotuJNYkBAZjWcDcYNl_WevCGmnBwVP6hDgp6tP20FYos8rdosObeWdLebqJ5cwAIjnh5e4UtieXlb0OCwVToA2zHuG8vZgiblAZ7NOaFIeVFU2jixli2neDOpshEAawHfv8soirnjepiLM8zLQz1CbTk5EtGJViii9KL_BkxotSX1btCrz6VuAr_CQseaaVxPO-WqKI9Y9Zez4DmQRiza5R4bvh9TEeQ3o7r151e8Uur5kfy8fAn7BBRn96t1EREIVQYEpsXBPzthqf3UwX7YlJByaGf47M5AZ7EgAetmM4JQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Hercules Musarum, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of Hercules Musarum was a temple dedicated to Hercules in ancient Rome, near the Circus Flaminius.<br><br>It was built by Marcus Fulvius Nobilior, who conquered of the Greek city of Ambracia in 189 BC. It was probably completed and dedicated during his triumph in 187 BC. The epithet 'Musarum' means 'of the Muses' and refers to Nobilior's discovery that Hercules was known in Greece as 'Musagetes' or 'leader of the Muses'. The temple contained copies of the fasti and statues taken from Ambracia, including statues of the Muses. The Portico of Metellus was later built near the temple.<br><br>In 29 BC Lucius Marcius Philippus restored the temple and built a portico around it, later known as the Portico of Philippus. Part of the temple's floorplan is known from a fragment of the 3rd century Forma Urbis Romae.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-4E342E</styleUrl>
