            12.498386,41.91086,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Herculis Victoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6mqP4WZicORA1FIYebV8PP86wOnCQ-HO07fT0gVrYBE6kt3yKdPnUilaax41yNQvCPvJgDF-uKZNunNgaIehgCt1fTEPCTVT576ZrfOWxrF0zJPYz91VncvI0bbyK0IhIBNLrdCm29IMMUwDGxF6KgP4qpnAONj0eL-S99fh9tCRMrta3cUJ8TeE_v4_CgNO2oR5G_wT4BLdefZcMJeeHKKKz2MwavSslLnM7pC4YEaSEOjUKjks_I7mOp9lZ4prSR62c4pzytTO4os9PCbdr2mW45FXSnb7bxFT_3SGjwjUNcxh9kDLHqi33NY4FEvTar7qDFFZeYJ74x5LPgMkq520QSb4-QUtGEljqion1oZK5qQx5tRD14YOfShFgDyClvGDNWOqTS1c24r11WrLejzltAwtJaO0cavuSdP1OSDmzB0ubmuA1bXMvr_v_0sKWTh45FKKGYNbrQQwLRZbrOiui6jUgMmW4cJICar9vNsSxeG5TL1mdGjhg8VX0gnj1UfUetkZHhxIfhHjkhuxV1GbY7GRzHgPKsezcYRYrhbezwkwaVw8QQA2maF3VaPqiB7e8kcMGOLkRgYK7s2CqyJp4JTaZKwRyIS27ZSllY9RvxzP14piyR8JAcCKhIq2AZYKUO4rzTimEarpClausKfqKIU_oKENnpQDij2O8UjuHz_CfTE3gHBtOGwsedI4wt_oFACBo" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Hercules Victor, Rome.<br><br>The well-preserved Round Temple by the Tiber, with its ring of 20 Corinthian columns surrounding a circular cella, had been attributed to the first half of the 1st c. BC, and intensive re-examination of its architecture has suggested a more precise date in the early 1st c. BC. <br><br>Sometime in the decades after Augustus, major repair work was carried out on about half of the colonnade; at this point Luna marble was used in place of the original, imported Pentelic marble — a remarkably luxurious material at the time of its construction. Further, the presence of Greek masons can be plausibly deduced from the workmanship of the original capitals, whereas other details of the temple’s execution are at odds with Greek practices (esp. the flat, not curved, ends of the fluting on the column shafts) and are ascribed to local workshops. <br><br>The temple was elevated on an artificial platform, some 12 m above the Tiber and 2-3 m above the Forum Boarium; it stood outside the Forum Boarium, and probably just beyond the Republican circuit wall as well. In addition to the Greek marble and workmen, an inscribed statue base found next to the temple attests to the presence of a work by the 2nd c. BC Greek sculptor Scopas the Younger, possibly the Hercules Olivarius listed by the late-antique Regionary Catalogues (Regio XI) in the area NW of the Circus Maximus.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7ozC8TTDhv233IIYaDT0VeL9HjoLIC9AroP1q0vogsdQeL2d4FFFP4KdJyI2n4izmIl8C3iFyKSfRKQhIW_e0xQUpNNhuKi0UBtVwnq0p-JQb4ERUA4RldvWz0U1K6LVWuB7P2AyvGa6mvH_2B-DJfW3Ly_a8hFaQoucqMmr8fIWl414L9CGnkh10oY-CR3DYpugxxzx-T670pPgtdvnHsQ51cHlVr9ERm2Cv9q7JKXQtbmOKn-L6mD_Gi0DAYp3Nh33MNheTRlxDQq9Q28O7n41W5Za0XswyYzmtqPjb2UqfItGpyUyWiVjNqp3hNzYRhobV7q2_LyndktykespfJbWJByQsS3NJ54Yq6SyqDiUef6GWDLdvOBzWjDmJqN22NNUzd--aI_i9dUfVY0NAyd0D4kzItSE-G3ta0-70A90RdC0pXSEUWQo6AAbSfleXULRQ7Rv-qjNmwnDLyvufuCJsiu2rCI6wLTZgDjjlPxOEBpmPYMsSh2psLMBBLShxxGREJjURy3p0LMmBfI_YrZd-lDWU2Mhl6oXNKijVeqbDqmjSqM0bX1Jrh1f6tW_Bf92AtIJMyZDX4Ycl4NVNIXdOUZOS8q1kzmu3qdEUIgb3qh_sbC9soSpE7Nkb9ad6HkOE1eRbC-DAVm6XuyIzMLofqYVAwIiyN_tMR8erky0cTLLK0UEtwWjprG7QCQoZiGBu-9kOzvA" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
