      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Honoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Honos, Rome.<br><br>Double Temple to Honos and Virtus built ex manubiis by C. Marius in 101 BC; no archaeological remains are known, but Vitruvius describes it as a peristyle temple without a portico in the rear.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488803,41.890996,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Honoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Honos, Rome.<br><br>Double Temple to Honos and Virtus, without archaeological remains, close by the Porta Capena and the sanctuary of the Camenae in the vicinity of the Vicus Honoris et Virtutis of Regio I.<br><br> M. Claudius Marcellus, the conqueror of Syracuse, is reported to have restored an older Temple of Honos as well as added a new cella for Virtus; these were dedicated in 205 BC by his son. <br><br>The temple was renowned for its superb artworks dedicated by Marcellus from the spoils of Syracuse, many of which had disappeared by Livy’s time. The sanctuary received a new and specifically Augustan meaning in 19 BC when the senate erected the Altar of Fortuna Redux in front of the temple; there, by the gate where Augustus entered the city, the altar commemorated his safe and singularly successful return from the East with the Roman standards regained from the Parthians.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.490749,41.883952,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Honoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Honos, Rome.<br><br>Temple dedicated to Honos, possibly also to Virtus, located outside the Porta Collina, and known only through literary and epigraphic evidence.<br><br> A dedication to Honos found c. 35m from the Colline Gate confirms the location given by Cicero. An inscription to Virtus, found between the Baths of Diocletian and Via XX Settembre, may point to the presence of a double cult at this site, as seen at the city’s two other temples to Honos. No archaeological remains have been found and the exact site, form and size of the temple are unknown.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.499438,41.907552,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Iani (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4Ka27Q-UrPrYJ_Bq_yOGmXiFl6a-7psNmN8sAQncp19ricINumeZFspOXYzgyLmq-gY3QGq-BfYdIUKtVeUU-oQI8tWiB8oijwen32IsncDYK10Pfg8AiicCe-pDF22ZjK5AC0p9WlkTFjivWh0A6WUWbT0O24ic-CRdvDp26CYCxGNxx42EfJ7Vt6QGLC6aS01JR5Yi_zB_zASoc-euD-OFb9Yt8rbLEv8ss-ubKLQbwX4ETESZ7b-eYLtLYhUoPwqE66ho97QV5JXUqyqa4SV-U4l1a9aEvt40tFTPv15KgvDYDdajfZjzjiB7PqiyL6Ie8lMIzD1lACZ2BOUF0KDFo8tTAm4BMTppBwxfCJ61-nIidb91Cls9C7R45Bwuu76hsrGxkTsxq3E-KISPWD2-4EnMxKMPHJr_F8eVc30W_Ya5se0b_TZILkH9y004KOyi9sy2C5xBIwvr-eNcL2Rh0CeeB1G1ycF8rzvTgq5fc2tbLfI7YNX3C2pOOodLSdGWSqLfD0lE19CPxj25pp1y_AjEpqPd786AsqEDxd2cDJrgXkWbJITHme6f7xc0rNRftHzPF2SIWskNwgL8LDZXj54ZTX7vBVuaZ1ZggVRFxH1GuzLg5nka3TxXwnuw1OWcgKJEbLG8UbFInC566rxqTWOV__aD-5qDP2tE22NWH4MuJ-BcuTE8L9yPj-DMqjNGtrGnu4" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Janus, Rome.<br><br>The northernmost of the three preserved temples in the Forum Holitorium, dedicated to Ianus and located outside the Porta Carmentalis.<br><br> The identification is based on the description of the temple as ‘iuxta’ or ‘ad’ the Theatre of Marcellus. Built by C. Duilius soon after 260 BC, Augustus began to reconstruct the temple, but the project was only completed by Tiberius who rededicated the temple in 17 AD.<br><br> The S side of the temple is partly incorporated into the Church of S. Nicola in Carcere and the ground-plan can easily be restored, as remains of the temple have also been excavated outside the church. These remains have been dated to the Augustan period on the basis of their architectural decoration.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4m5GN92wJNfFdQCNxtmBIcIGkGbs1LDWZMLkQWdp3pSu_l-MZwCMvZcL5TgShCECZCXiUvBQtNgJ8qOh7Hd3JlHcRqy7PipYFx99lIPJHu1kzMpi_upA5nSVnq9vGSM_i6AsaDvNycYTMdqPiP3foHkiDY1wCz7PaXdEQZ0szY8xPCcUExSpjr_IlV8DFVZsrwsQ89LusUihP6m8ihsTEmYinfskGlCRUFho_PDfGMVBIFQWoy3kyPo0wodjnrbPCC1_8mNKePpk2ReDtZydmIB9lpgLG54u_Z8d_X-RJ5yx1SAtLe6ylFRezIQtIj1c2JCSbpZbTKSWfDM7Zqv46_cOQcEz2n6YP-wzG6GVJUH0p-3iwZFJ-Xraxtn_p5i-iziyjrgj5MJElCqeH0JTo-84jcbIjEZ0ecKyYhLKl7yFVqHNvzHiSAYalXt-IrGeMSnvu3N-7E1ihBdOVd1PZaKAXIZbZA9URXMMlaOLKIa7ZZFK68JQxJgQHnZCD2864SKOMIakpsJMR6eckltV9mUFOH2seMIneSN9StSOVJaypyLr-AjZHyA9RSBmgxlw9x2t1rnSnoaft4ZhOCwO6G2ghy7Dvc5-ude7QJxI3ko751urG2nlBpqueEBcEofZfK3fgNip-ULJPGurer63GZ5mXS6BLLjWJh-5pNPRvgPMMoRtZCyLnJzJC2ztJxEsR5_LWQ4d2PAA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v74e4Qy7rqloSHj2202bhXcM_X5_oTKE0reGT8JUcP9kVEiuSp2OkMqxti8AWJ1AAa6_0y4RvQumfEQkrQFFI9KUmzriMlEUS4hoUkmc-mDLPZ8j-Dt-HH9hzMLuCmMEN03_9Xm1sdxn8-KguraILHzOucuayZ3Bh5XPZvZPzYFWzGSUexIt4UnMcCFCMvwnt3K_on-AAgqqSq4skRDU5nUhO-h8-ZDALPsHuNdZevqD-ed1MT6SCfciJeKTVutgBbq6Mqf0seVlzM7JbpYO5yimG9ObF-hww3kI5X9iEffJgsHorPjqzhYtL6Bcn6oIVsRGxtg5sFKU7tr8ctrlTW7Ux874C7Tx2l27qm0M7GSl5Xqqwm8Pu6XzEHYz-AsXo4rrDweYaLGfHzFPBo-5HTHr3v_3uEtIgmX2mcEmTLSh1-Jhyss8_s4_u4Obl0asi8PgJytZkQjyxrsKLfGZO3gLuzYzjUux-CzgBsBqTgqDZmoAKY9ghS9LZa2ecQdqcstIOX7yJeRF7bMdk9rJcgqX7t-z48QhAZbQa8sbwwbdhmiQPgC49vFuaCX9jx8VrPiOVKXCnmvaWIxvG1R9W2tJgW7Y7jdfUcCn6BV1ro5MAUuqeTA3CaMQPE3Oe04Z3UsyuZS6DM057MwfYBbbrNt0G7l-YvgtmLf5UyUpoaE0iBwNlXhLViqGiAiNss85BxcS97mhzDKVA" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
