      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Iunonis Lucinae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Juno Lucina, Rome.<br><br>This Temple to Iuno Lucina on the Esquiline was founded by Roman matrons in 375 BC and only later became an aedes publica. <br><br>It was restored in 190 and 41 BC. It was situated in a small grove, or lucus on the Cispian as listed in the sacraria Argeorum. The general consensus thus places this temple just N of the Clivus Suburanus on the S slopes of the Cispian. But this location is not consistent with an inscription relating to the restoration of the perimeter wall by Q. Pedius in 41 BC, found under the Monastery of S. Francisco de Paula, apparently in situ. The monastery lies S of Via di S. Lucia in Selci (the Clivus Suburanus), and so on the Oppian, not the Cispian. Unfortunately, this ambiguity cannot be resolved.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-C2185B</styleUrl>
