      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Iovis Statoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5cyA3StYs-2bg_ZcfsIIwVlkJTMMdk7bIvZmveMsqp3TAPTmf9yxbhy7-GqpoZRwEa9PRxIk11S2wuDxStmb8VzfCShOR-kPywP7x4wUww08u4ipJ2uevvkFTxwss1QGnVFh_9QWj-fWUwNBKXmppAyQpm9fj2kruknOyzNmUWBJJOWWaDR0iGMhTXVnadBuWlDM9Ap64RCPwSSptLj4L6U-eDlbwa3qr_jxhZH2_xkIQZL0Tni0qnJtE27kCcQ2FaWZSHWclWp75QQ3wTq2QrVnBuXVIe3alGNdXe3xzlxMcafLjfvWPw3ukmYC76xxJfsDFbedOeadv1xVNBs7tlwVx7Cjs65sxfVuIYrs1Xr2_9AnJ9Zd4dI9yn3HctbX48Qd3yEwFtSHFg1nECAReEvKyQ_S3hHaYN3Uw3JI6BAiTVUJYpbzgZzfbogAf-9-ch3DuAzvK36zirW0NJxeOAhk33yYxdNz6prrxAn_nrftOkAd3R0mcP0qTzQq5SQXYzk3oJtpbNYFNqsYAbfrzH1kAO-H4sbxTIjr6wFOzyWHhc2Asd89trUen-GAjWYyxvFa137s0XeQBnMoNqqTK-JUEmxEutenjxqTvLLZu5tcn9zNaz07LrNn9Zu81JMvub3KrOD5jst8QUleMtOXJCllnEwzZrHjXUQ5I3qXAnIac0lMFwFAs6FhFouWc6AostK0g8mLSdsw" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Jupiter Stator, Rome.<br><br>Temple to Iuppiter Stator (‘Stayer’) said to be vowed by Romulus during a battle with the Sabines near the Porta Mugonia.<br><br> According to Livy, the Romulean temple was never built, and the site remained a fanum (consecrated location) until 294 BC when the consul M. Atilius Regulus vowed a temple to Iuppiter Stator in the heat of battle. The Roman Senate, having twice been obligated to erect a temple, now ordered the aedes built. <br><br>In the late-Republican period Cicero convened the Senate there, and Ovid records its dedication day as June 27.<br><br>The location of this temple has been subject to heated debate. Literary sources frequently connect it with the Porta Mugonia, a Palatine gate which can be placed with some confidence along the “Clivus Palatinus” S of the Sacra Via. The temple was also near the Nova Via and the house of Tarquinius Priscus. Ovid places it ‘in front of the Palatine’, and Plutarch at ‘the beginning of the Sacra Via as you ascend the Palatine hill’. Combined, these sources suggest the area around the Arch of Titus, and traditionally the temple has been associated with a concrete podium found just E of that arch.]]></description>
