      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Iovis Victoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Jupiter Victor, Rome.<br><br>An early-Republican cult site of Iuppiter Victor on the collis Quirinalis near the Temple of Quirinus. <br><br>The dies natalis of the ‘templum’ was April 13. A 3rd c. BC inscription found in the papal gardens on the Quirinal during the 17th c. records the restoration of a shrine to Iuppiter Victor: [D]IOVEI VICTORE T.MEFV[…] M. F. III VIR [RESTI]TVIT and suggests the location of the cult site. <br><br>This sanctuary may have been vowed in 295 BC by Q. Fabius Maximus Rullianus, and dedicated a few years later, perhaps in 289 BC by Q. Fabius Maximus Gurges, who was the son of Rullianus and consul that year.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-9C27B0</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.489214,41.901623,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Iuturnae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Juturna, Rome.<br><br>Temple to Iuturna of uncertain appearance located in the Campus Martius, perhaps near the terminus of the Aqua Virgo.<br><br> The temple was built by a Lutatius Catulus in c. 241 BC and dedicated on 11 January. In the late 70s or early 60s BC, C. Aelius Staienus set up before the temple gilt-bronze statues and a titulus recording the number of kings he restored to friendship with Rome.<br><br>Ovid gives the temple’s location at the terminus of the Aqua Virgo. Frontinus locates the end of the Virgo’s arcade ‘alongside the front of the Saepta’. Since the aqueduct entered the city, and the Campus Martius, from the N, it seems reasonable to assume that the temple of Iuturna was located near the N end of the Saepta. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.477478,41.899073,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Larium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of the Lares, Rome.<br><br>Temple of the Lares on the Velia or on the lower Palatine, restored by Augustus in 4 BC.<br><br> The aedes was located in summa Sacra via, ‘at the heights of the Sacra Via’, and ‘on the Palatine near the Porta Mugionia’. Combined, these suggest that the area near the Arch of Titus, just W of the Sacra Via, was home to the Lares cult.<br><br> Also nearby was the fanum Orbonae and the Temple of Iuppiter Stator, which shared a dies natalis with the Temple of the Lares . Coarelli proposes that the Temple to the Lares would have been small, perhaps no larger than a compital shrine.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488718,41.890816,0
          </coordinates>
