      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Martis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5wqU0UhDBqG0bMToOFNpoPz9bDwL4aWYdi7dcxNLImy5-ZvBB9ysUBwzjl77jdp32ocgJCamUY-UMYHANnYgW9HTeYDXuA_JjPQmEb3NsvRj7WGV8F-C-RPqUB8tKsoI4zzUP4P0HktahNUdw5_4RvXFz_GIOl2SKSk-hJZZXbrmn3d4bRlw5GLfnqKdq5GDpE3qOfaHwpZrhKeCxIAN0Vbj1A0grF-LHaBjyO5VBqiWec9kAiDpIP5a4j69O3SZDv2obkyfb4Bj1JqTrle7GU4CwiiZGBdKUZUZ5J__fzE3FrwdYpYTZLnql8CyKSD02bjpEP3XllWthpWLwpJaNL1lTnpREFp6HUBYTs1g5iGU75zzaJ5vhBlVRC_fEx-grUXeK83wUmgO-k8Qfwh2HxO3CyL88C5imsYxe878-UzTJCgyJS1_SfOsonEDIfQGGR3MOASVnCB7wBjcQnaVtsKJQ4a3tbgj67EhgzgzuJbxijxBYT9kAtzbaq2uwLnqBWqlqu_MGm8Vlvv5MxFpVOFux_XCDs5DKtoxdEXL1hSsqPQUssIzruF7pd4GkJTVHT2xmB_NUm_XE6xIHAMexG5rWlHbndclyhJzx_pX-h6u1HZtFWVr_V--_HyeAnAkMsbnQIIS8kybodeeC9SnKzyDS0paoU09g1lPYjAWXyQltGuWjwIZVLyF_feJEC49sBzrrCxyz9yQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Mars, Rome.<br><br>Republican Temple to Mars located near the Circus Flaminius, erected ex manubiis by D. Brutus Callaicus between 135 and 130 BC, after his Spanish triumph.<br><br> The temple was planned by Hermodorus of Salamis and contained the cult statues of both Mars and Venus.<br><br>Remains of a hexastyle peripteral temple near the Church of S. Salvatore in Campo (in the N Circus Flaminius) were first identified in 1838; its architectural elements are assigned to the early Republican period on account of their mixture of native Italic and Hellenistic Greek features. A fragment of the Severan Marble Plan that depicts part of a peripteral temple with a long, narrow cella and an aditus — just as Cicero describes the aedes Martis— is placed in the area of S. Salvatore in Campo. Moreover, archival documents found by Tortorici report the discovery in S. Salvatore of fragments of a colossal statue depicting a goddess, perhaps the cult statue of Venus described by Pliny.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-A52714</styleUrl>
