      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Martis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Mars, Rome.<br><br>Rome’s main Temple of Mars was situated outside the city on the N side of the Via Appia between its first and second milestones. It was vowed in 388 BC by T. Quinctius.<br><br>The Temple of Mars was an important landmark in ancient Rome; it is frequently mentioned in connection with the paving and repaving of the Via Appia and the surrounding neighborhood was known as ‘ad Martis’. Further, the sloped section of the Via Appia leading up to the temple was known as the clivus Martis.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.50258,41.873039,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Martis Ultoris (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6kxf8IZa23Lgpi-ogeI-l0eVYc2aMd1R03OQ_TucYelQCWchZvpnzV6T1dOAAkTKZMZpazQlZGT_jHlxUFibC5zCT80ijba6K0ZSZIHaXs23WNJBTIVd8qUKh_juoxy31cjqMdxFU45oeRh2T6LZxCMI9LJOBxuEixv7qGEKjC33u8THfo-AW6254cZYuDvNlm_t2bfilD1isKgUi4jf-CfDDIS2OHUwXGR7qWpMkeW4ZGtcNriqQyjhBanro6xTdlW57yW5-cVuAA35R8UqeLvurWsU07_2eA5DSewxNUYaIeWwGRQS0ilAFKutqwboZEHBrHOGXoyWh8G4IjFqN3PkIc3NoFNDyTYTZ3z9CxqPa-U0u4rSLKS8xLnFVtzsrQB1cQGwp0-YHru9NqqeA451s6DUag8zCZqxGmHr_3XKt3-QGzoqIezZcPVHdHSppqfBIIvv7zhr5q6Jpzpj7uwChaOEPI39E7P2ZG2kAg9VD7_V2MuPobqEBxEviuviTeiq1Df42fxdXZM9JYRsc_YfWfC9D_OhYIv_lcBH4cRQqs2nB0GEyYfiOzwGLgXS7Td_z86Yke-apdVubHjWx8x23h81PHiqXg59FbkHzqzuG7MAkXXmVEFpLmJSaQpKKE5TEg53K6uSfgTE_EYw98zrrCXuNhF3VWw0_CRFBpJGAiEH7RDwGp-bOgjRFSzy0vYW-8dpYa" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Mars Ultor, Rome.<br><br>The triumvir Octavian vowed to build a temple honoring Mars, the Roman God of War, during the battle of Philippi in 42 BC.<br><br>It was dedicated in 2 BC and in 19 AD Tiberius added two triumphal arches either side of the temple in honour of Drusus the Younger and Germanicus and their victories in Germania.<br><br>Before battle, generals set off from the Temple of Mars, after attending an inaugural ceremony. Other ceremonies took place in the temple including the assumption of the toga virilis by young men. The Senate met at the Temple when discussing war and the victorious generals dedicated their spoils from their triumphs to Mars at the altar. Arms or booty recovered from battle were often stored in the Forum as well.[6] Another use that Augustus made of the Temple was to store the standards taken by the Parthians from Crassus during his failed campaign, after their retrieval through Augustus' diplomacy in 20 BC, as depicted by the Augustus of Prima Porta. Three Aquilae were lost in 9 AD in the Battle of the Teutoburg Forest of the Legions Legio XVII, Legio XVIII and Legio XIX; all three were recovered-one in 14 AD from the Marsi and one in 15 AD from the Bructeri; the 3rd was recovered in 41 AD from the Chauci-and all three placed within the Temple of Mars the Avenger.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5eLI4xCVSCjzqCLyH31Qbp8fYnKWa9sKm6VsTQMI5vRn0E7BuVL7ULR7atQ1tJKiexqpqOwDMhdro-ziKWQI7ih78I4jekJYuTo146DiZOP9g1JwTmEPbunWYxempG-BToAjRD_QycTPrLSukbmwvGRGtoPwFbXyE3y_ragGPyeLlkadbtqXF5e6xWWVTzuaiMGQyL7p3FWB_kXnVab1n0odyI01IYvc-9jzy89c6dIaD-X87kAgt9LCeZ3cjBNCpXj6io2skne6iivK24U1T5_25QcIb215NuyihdZcxxNHvYIwfKYuukrb5aGvPGzu5IYkYfZFsXhWTeibw1xdaVW-mPmmWrfgstAJKtDKxF2PC0iX9jd9AIB7DwF0U3rdo3LTeeW-jkysT49MPDTF71vhjnAM02cbF6tvEyHsIDyVTeUOs5EcRfbWH__exdjrE-CmQ2uTZbyTh_1GoZ_fBTJ-qgEPFeWASbU3bngUJ4dNruUaM787a_y_yreYVphjGadSs054kZSMkTJlVRgee8Djq10-uDEVx_Dqr42AZxkgWzONiba0G_OdLvkWd97B1NghgD1Pu9YXsFfYDSX4Nv4uOmNIAk9DTMwuufC0aUvPMGMCuPIJE7PBl8d9RhO6eSE3m0Gpv3zV8JFYx1B6EpG5Dn5hozZ2a1E8D1xIzY8ftWh3vylhJKZ8xFfNWXRHm_HhTmg82cRQ" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
