      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Minervae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Minerva, Rome.<br><br>Temple of Minerva on the Aventine, apparently in a prominent place, but without identified remains.<br><br> It is mentioned in reference to events of the late 3rd c. BC and depicted on the Severan Marble Plan. <br><br>The temple was rebuilt by Augustus, together with (on the Aventine) the temples of Iuppiter Libertas and Iuno Regina. Its precise location depends on the position of the Marble Plan’s fragment, which has recently been disputed, but the most plausible solution still speaks for the traditional placement in the N center of the hill.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.481682,41.884527,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Minervae Captae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Minerva Capta, Rome.<br><br>A shrine of Minerva, is mentioned by Varro, who places it on a road leading up to the Caelian, and a shrine or sanctuary of Minerva Capta is mentioned by Ovid, who puts it on the crest of the same hill.<br><br> It seems likely that both authors refer to the same place. Following the discovery of a statue of Minerva and an inscription recording a dedication to Minerva on the N slopes of the Caelian, this shrine has generally been placed on the “Via Tusculana” in the area of SS. Quattro Coronati.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.49862,41.888128,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Minervae Medicae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Minerva Medica, Rome.<br><br>This temple is listed in the Notitia and Curiosum within Regio V: Esquiliae.<br><br>Its location has been determined on the basis of votive deposits found in Via Carlo Botta between Via Buonarotti and Via Machiavelli, but there are no surviving architectural remains. The votive offerings, found in situ in the storage chambers of the temple, included a dedication to Minerva, representations of the goddess, and dedications to a healing deity, all dated from the 4th to 1st c. BC. An Imperial-period statue of Minerva was discovered nearby, along with statues of other deities.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-1A237E</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.501715,41.891109,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Neptuni (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5SC_52S_bHPvksLEFqv-PyomkAuqf82BuFptvWYX-Rv_Napkdg-Xb3aIt7Ss5_z82E5LGfhQZWJ6LaN42Q505Bs2kXsp_HhKljc6_QeFhJFLGWG2XABNXxptRSpTj0akuEN8SnV_c_vpi1XSBVHCTaJejAtIJ378rnHMIRKUsg2s0xSrNQ0thxWkzxeg4Ih3VC0rtHmvLLaRtTijcQ8Rmib-mm0PvLCLhPFX2algpjMYmhBQX1-zJa8Sog-adxxE4hysFfl53dhu1u-gdWBfVXMN5GF-alAgmTD0pLyrhwimT0S4wTslSGmaK7oWjZ12jewH-RwI3UqyQbd78sRuE9YZRlnyXVG_RkkRTmByAzJXhlRSm-yfJzpr1PAzNzp-9d9Co9Y9H6icVkBx7bRIVTuSBKWBxTB8pqTG5GXcoDT6ccOAB9iqvLJ2OVZ17eGOJw8DuntGYW3gLNC--n28cirlwMRDRfO2v0TYjCrwEh8SkxgCEp-l2thl-5BfNqpqEpFxxkKSu9SgW89LYROsh25Exq5sFX4ASQ-mb3HiNKwKHewj3FOtrnFKc8lkOzFXUlBI5t30MFBq9ic9pPEn3SNrH-ZaPBOUvBTmNZGeKy1RE-DTz1MtJn_66hn3QCo_TT6ElD06epDcyF98umWS2bFLFxb5t40vJ2eLE_7GpNtaSgiWE0wc9MQiSA1p7WL_1oiayzLU1Cow" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Neptune, Rome.<br><br>Tetrastyle temple dedicated to Neptune, located in the area of the Circus Flaminius and recently identified with the remains of a tufa opus quadratum podium, of which only the W flank is preserved.<br><br> Additional archaeological remains found nearby in Piazza Costaguti substantiate this location and suggest a 2nd c. BC date. A fragmentary relief from Ostia, which depicts a temple to Neptune next to a shrine of Hercules, had been used to locate the former in the NW corner of the Circus Flaminius, next to the sanctuary of Hercules Musarum, placing the Temple of Neptune in the exact place in which the recent remains were found.<br><br> The podium recently found in Piazza Costaguti should be connected to this structure. Pliny mentions cult statues of Neptune, Thetis, Achilles and the Nereids, all by Scopas, a 2nd-c. Greek artist working in Rome. The so-called Altar of Domitius Ahenobarbus, in actuality a large base for statuary honoring its donour Marcus Antonius, was erected in the vicinity of the temple sometime in the early 1st c. BC.]]></description>
