      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Pacis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6_AH8Lin35kXSphuQhZnuE74stUxyganMfTJTXEYmy_6LaBufsYhyLmL1nAv8brWkOft567zHp96Cz-Lyu8Ux1sZqYXtcGDqzY_Fh9o64K3BTsLIy4BS6dU2MepbaR9ni7okJSe1D-fwNirmLOl9qoXwgKslo7-N1WfUzGutMPMe8PuCrO82qIIxvGcv0A31GBQb8xnLAYFjYaxqv3lWfzJBQb1vutyIcuPEHtnh14s2F6oam897BR83X__bgZdwIX_GdZsh22SwCLnx87EDefv3iXfBMwrEr_gWvA-j8qgY49kvrBGsZtaIHsRGu7sYh1FdyK-msx6AtBRrFH5PgiljVFLcXymlXJVspmSwQV8DutLig5h_WJdIAFtp1_n5-bDfMMyh3LGifFO8947IHHq1Av_LxFshdALD8CXr2RhIhJ0QLF-HIhuKP-cadJ9HZzWWzr0GsaHb6jTiEDPoq5_pEYWfWIQXZQE85VxyAwK3olUlI2GF-5X8P1UkWVpcPUysvUZ4C5WLtMsTttug8mjuCt2jWxyf7BljzZKQLpN7Fk8URh7q4EGSE0mo5OkZwQI_RYsDeIPfQQlTQQyngR2gXj6VnKN__63U3pd0Wg0dPRnI9U5GrWIHE7Msfb1LBQBC4ECVhXAAw_tMQcpUnlGljoEpLAuZSv79E1GuFZsI53AKOdBazhVbbbAzfcIpHugvG7BPNGkg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Pax, Rome.<br><br>The Temple of Peace, also known as the Forum of Vespasian, was built in Rome in 71 AD under Emperor Vespasian in honour to Pax, the Roman goddess of peace. It faces the Velian Hill, toward the famous Colosseum, and was on the southeast side of the Argiletum.<br><br>The funds to create this grand monument were acquired through Vespasian's sacking of Jerusalem during the Jewish-Roman Wars. The interior and surrounding buildings were decorated with the treasures collected there by the Roman army.<br><br>Because Vespasian was both a leading general and later appointed emperor during civil war, the Temple of Peace was especially important to him as a leader. A grand and significant monument such as this is vital to the promotion of a powerful, strong public image of the emperor, and is a symbol of the peace and prosperity Vespasian was able to bring the empire.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
