      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Portuni (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6vGvDpUWBDRQhoCZvRFnTNxON1wO4Kd8givBKgwT-dN1UFfKQdBXCQwQTQc0D3pp7M3PGRwAK8g49XruRmajX6iHu083a3Gp2TVnT6girbvIutdZGSJdo_krSURWRyr1zpeTVQWdzPufUCtL9wNpPYzccf0DCTLqTHrjRm3cn0U-smTYVKaprjXxpVUrg3kF4yoMzRLM6qDvsMTYxDmm0Uc-wVtLs-aMrvAP25hcSDHHnbxGyfIPVBWHORKNl1317Sdhjbd3pODT4D6PCm8RwbREDDjBu5Mqu0C-4VF5RI6JXv5noC9aqBknfUpY_8ZhY3-Rr22HcN8rRVot4UG0oY3oJe2QT-wTuGP0RJdx6dpeaY1RDBFeqsULnOfs_1fY5wRGyWIgB4bLYq_HlIHgpQN6q0GXKiSMGthBV20GgY9ir028QarY1JGiYlTctpyyvdFfkXb6Ftt27invhB3rmOLEGVNlDIkM9IN6X11Wq9kcMGo0BUV3BJvmCygOxQRso65pXqD5SNR-ILepKEK5nQDlaIM8vrlrTnUwn0ETGzIMMSndoj-q6C-X5BB6EOQrsca8uITw8Sw0iPJvw22DpfM_Feh2pkiV1Ya9A5EO336WQhgIWBXIH_5zu7UiUjB4GL_YoJHAgZKOUfOta97Ee_7oCSb03dzJiTMOsGYtIh6X4orWNA3T40_wEe9uUoBefqn2XA41q1hw" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Temple of Portunus, Rome.<br><br>The well-preserved Ionic temple between the Tiber and the Forum Boarium, formerly attributed to Fortuna Virilis, is now widely agreed to be that of Portunus, a divinity whose temple was near the Portus Tiberinus and whose festival, held on the same day as the temple’s foundation day, was celebrated at the Pons Aemilius.<br><br> The structure attests to two phases: the earlier dates to the late 4th or early 3rd c. BC, had a 6-m-tall podium of Grotta Oscura blocks, and was fronted by a tall, arched passageway (H. 6 m, also in Grotta Oscura tufa) at its N-facing façade. The current temple was built on the same podium around 80/70 BC; the new building was surrounded by much higher terrain (probably a result of the artificial elevation of the area in the early 2nd c. B.C.), and it kept, though at a slightly different angle, the unusual N orientation towards both the viaduct leading to the Pons Aemilius and, more importantly, the Portus Tiberinus which was just N of this street.<br><br> Tetrastyle and pseudoperipteral in its arrangement, the temple is essentially built of Anio tufa, with special elements articulated in travertine. In the immediate vicinity stood two identical travertine bases erected for C. and L. Caesar which may have flanked the temple or the nearby “Fornix Augusti”.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value>https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6vGvDpUWBDRQhoCZvRFnTNxON1wO4Kd8givBKgwT-dN1UFfKQdBXCQwQTQc0D3pp7M3PGRwAK8g49XruRmajX6iHu083a3Gp2TVnT6girbvIutdZGSJdo_krSURWRyr1zpeTVQWdzPufUCtL9wNpPYzccf0DCTLqTHrjRm3cn0U-smTYVKaprjXxpVUrg3kF4yoMzRLM6qDvsMTYxDmm0Uc-wVtLs-aMrvAP25hcSDHHnbxGyfIPVBWHORKNl1317Sdhjbd3pODT4D6PCm8RwbREDDjBu5Mqu0C-4VF5RI6JXv5noC9aqBknfUpY_8ZhY3-Rr22HcN8rRVot4UG0oY3oJe2QT-wTuGP0RJdx6dpeaY1RDBFeqsULnOfs_1fY5wRGyWIgB4bLYq_HlIHgpQN6q0GXKiSMGthBV20GgY9ir028QarY1JGiYlTctpyyvdFfkXb6Ftt27invhB3rmOLEGVNlDIkM9IN6X11Wq9kcMGo0BUV3BJvmCygOxQRso65pXqD5SNR-ILepKEK5nQDlaIM8vrlrTnUwn0ETGzIMMSndoj-q6C-X5BB6EOQrsca8uITw8Sw0iPJvw22DpfM_Feh2pkiV1Ya9A5EO336WQhgIWBXIH_5zu7UiUjB4GL_YoJHAgZKOUfOta97Ee_7oCSb03dzJiTMOsGYtIh6X4orWNA3T40_wEe9uUoBefqn2XA41q1hw</value>
