      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Quirini (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Quirinus, Rome.<br><br>Temple to Quirinus located atop the collis Quirinalis. First built in the 4th c. BC, the temple burned in 49 BC and was restored by 45 BC.<br><br> Augustus rebuilt and rededicated the monumental aedes in 16 BC. Quirinus was a deity of special interest to the gens Iulia, and a statue of Julius Caesar was erected in the precinct. Vitruvius describes the temple as dipteral, octastyle and Doric, with a pronaos and posticum; given the time at which he wrote (c. 30 B.C.), Vitruvius probably would have seen the restoration of 45 BC. Since Dio relates that the Augustan temple had 76 columns, it must have been dipteral like its predecessor. The temple precinct was enclosed by a portico known as the porticus Quirini, and was home to a shrine of Quirinus’ consort Hora as well as Rome’s first sundial, a gift of L. Papirius Cursor in 293 BC.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.489058,41.902171,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Temple of Salutis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Salus, Rome.<br><br>Temple to Salus, dedicated by C. Iunius Bubulcus Brutus in 302 BC and built atop the collis Salutaris, one of the four peaks of the Quirinal. <br><br>Though the temple suffered from frequent lightning strikes and fires, the original 4th c. BC paintings by Fabius Pictor survived until the reign of Claudius; this suggests that the temple weathered the damage well, and may have retained an Archaic appearance, with proportions and decoration suitable to its late 4th c. dedication, through the Augustan period. As with the other major temples of the Quirinal, no architectural remains survive and the temple’s location is debated.<br><br>The place name vicus Salutis is known from an inscription dating to 33 BC found beneath S. Maria Maddelena which names the neighborhood. Its findspot is in close proximity to the Porta Salutaris (c. 150 m distant); together, the place names suggest that the temple, the aedes Salutis, stood nearby in the E section of the Palazzo del Quirinale, a locale well within the bounds of the collis Salutaris.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
