      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Telli (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Tellus, Rome.<br><br>Temple dedicated to Tellus, located on the Carinae. <br><br>No archaeological remains survive, though its 3rd c. AD form is depicted on the Severan Marble Plan. <br><br>Originally vowed by P. Sempronius Sophus in 268 BC, the temple was built by the Roman people. For a time Cicero lived in close proximity to the temple, and he reports that some Romans asserted that he was responsible for safeguarding it. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.492488,41.893691,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Tempestatis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Tempestas, Rome.<br><br>Temple to the Tempestates, or Tempestas, vowed by L. Cornelius Scipio, which stood on the Via Appia near the Temple of Mars; its exact site is not known. <br><br>Two dedication days, one preserved in the fasti Antiates maiores (December 23) and the other in Ovid (June 1), suggest that the temple may have been refounded by Augustus. <br><br>The temple probably stood near the family tomb of the Cornelii Scipiones, where its founder was interred; this connection between family tomb and temple is mirrored further N on the Via Appia, where the Temple of Honos et Virtus, dedicated by the son of M. Claudius Marcellus, stands in close proximity to his family monumenta.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
