      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Veneris Libitinae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Venus Libitina, Rome.<br><br>The Esquiline grove of Libitina, a goddess associated with funerals and with Venus; the name is derived from libido, ‘desire’. It included a templum to Venus-Libitina. <br><br>This grove was probably the headquarters of the libitinarii, professional undertakers. An inscription found outside the Porta Esquilina, which mentions a guild of flute-players, who also took part in funerals, puts this grove in the Campus Esquilinus just S of the Macellum Liviae; archaic architectural terracottas have been discovered in this area. <br><br>The temple seems to have been an early Republican foundation and its survival into the Imperial period is attested. In the Augustan period this was also a commercial area. Late-Republican tombstones attest that two freedmen, a butcher and a clothes-dealer, had businesses here.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-FF5252</styleUrl>
