          <coordinates>
            12.485221,41.889437,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Virtutis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Virtus, Rome.<br><br>Double Temple to Honos and Virtus built ex manubiis by C. Marius in 101 BC; no archaeological remains are known, but Vitruvius describes it as a peristyle temple without a portico in the rear.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488768,41.89093,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Virtutis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Virtus, Rome.<br><br>Double Temple to Honos and Virtus, without archaeological remains, close by the Porta Capena and the sanctuary of the Camenae in the vicinity of the Vicus Honoris et Virtutis of Regio I.<br><br>M. Claudius Marcellus, the conqueror of Syracuse, is reported to have restored an older Temple of Honos as well as added a new cella for Virtus; these were dedicated in 205 BC by his son. <br><br>The temple was renowned for its superb artworks dedicated by Marcellus from the spoils of Syracuse, many of which had disappeared by Livy’s time. The sanctuary received a new and specifically Augustan meaning in 19 BC when the senate erected the Altar of Fortuna Redux in front of the temple; there, by the gate where Augustus entered the city, the altar commemorated his safe and singularly successful return from the East with the Roman standards regained from the Parthians.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.49091,41.884008,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Templum Virtutis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Temple of Virtus, Rome.<br><br>Temple dedicated to Honos, possibly also to Virtus, located outside the Porta Collina, and known only through literary and epigraphic evidence.<br><br>A dedication to Honos found c. 35m from the Colline Gate confirms the location given by Cicero. An inscription to Virtus, found between the Baths of Diocletian and Via XX Settembre, may point to the presence of a double cult at this site, as seen at the city’s two other temples to Honos. No archaeological remains have been found and the exact site, form and size of the temple are unknown.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1548-795548</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.4993616,41.9074867,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Residential</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Scant remains of a late-Republican or Augustan residence on the Vicus Tuscus just N of the Horrea Agrippiana were recovered beneath S. Maria Antiqua during excavations in the 1980s; in an initial report, the finds (reticulate walls and opus spicatum pavements) were cautiously identified with either a commercial or residential structure.<br><br> After further study, Hurst has characterized the material as the atrium of “a grand house of late-Republican or Augustan date” that may have been home to Germanicus during the Augustan era. Regardless of the suggested proprietor, the home did not survive long after the Augustan period, as it was built over by 25-30 AD.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.485294,41.891135,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Large residence along the lower W slope of the Palatine, located just above and S of the Horrea Agrippiana.<br><br> Remains include a reticulate foundation wall parallel to the W side of the hill, as well as other foundation and dividing walls in tufa blocks that Krause interprets as tabernae (shops or stalls) opening onto a street, and Papi as two cryptoportici. Carandini has associated this residence with that of M. Antony, which burned in 25 BC, though this is far from universally accepted.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.485396,41.890355,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Recent excavations on the NW Palatine in the area of the Domus Tiberiana have revealed a long reticulate retaining wall (L. 59.30 m) parallel to the W side of the hill as well as two walls running perpendicular to it (L. 10.5 and 14.4 m; Krause 192: sectors 3-6-9, at level V); the excavators identify these as probable foundations of a late-Republican house which would have faced the Velabrum.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.485556,41.890267,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a large residence and bath complex dated to the late 1st c. BC were recovered on the NW Palatine.<br><br> A heated room (c. 21 x 30 m) cut into the living rock and paved with mosaics had plastered walls and vaults decorated with stuccos; at a higher level of the same area reticulate remains of an indeterminate plan have been documented. The residence occupied at least 600 sq. m.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.485983,41.890558,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Residence on the NW corner of the Palatine dated to the mid 1st c. BC.<br><br> Excavated just N of an Imperial-era street (often labeled the ‘Clivus Victoriae’) within three Hadrianic tabernae were cement and opus reticulatum substructures belonging to a series of rectangular rooms.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487138,41.89077,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>A luxurious late-Republican or Augustan residence on the NW Palatine was recovered during recent excavations; unearthed were three vaulted rooms revetted in marble and mosaics and, behind these, a brief (c. 5 m) segment of a cryptoporticus cut into the tufa hillside. Some 19 m distant are remains in opus reticulatum associated with the same house.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.486711,41.890191,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyDzi8abKtCAjsT2cWqFtihdP1pZWiUP2wjuIfLlA-qCbP08F_QWzOz-DLCFoKYZW85DoXvm2ISQGgOG0NNAorrJphEvE3dpCt1BPU9yVrIXwQnEDzqHAsceq6VzYvGv01RvID1Ow0m5aIpThc_Pe7irv6PJREgFUSNKK_IEObU4X8g-2DyJonVnzsibO3D80MqP-3fgpyUHw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Residence, Rome.<br><br>A lavishly decorated, rectangular hall of a late-Republican/early-Augustan residence, commonly known as the “Aula Isiaca”, is located on the Palatine beneath the “Basilica” of the Flavian Palace; the room, which features an apsidal E end and a barrel-vaulted ceiling, is the only portion of the domus which survives the later Neronian and Flavian interventions on the site. <br><br>While the opus caementicium and opus reticulatum walls provide a general late-Republican/early-Augustan timeframe, the late Second-Style wall-paintings, which cover the extant walls and vault, allow for a date c. 19 BC. There is surprisingly little scholarly speculation as to the proprietor of this once-prominent residence, though Coarelli hypothesizes Mark Antony may have owned it and, after his death, Agrippa; meanwhile, Ling speculates that the room may have been part of the *Domus of Augustus.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwKCl6kyQFCD6AuMaRnlf9aA61_-sK65IFxy7kfNez-CD0iXATDsPAqccr6lhqp-SkqhC_5i8Me1B3cbsktSJkLY_7_UcIm1VN8tYvqn3MGpRz466yXGKhCjlBzPNsjyGQ43W-gtjC-C3y1z-S7jGiCfwQWMO2VKgdWIMO-ANa0-E6djvD_JgGrK08hos6jJ7aJmg71dBsHdg?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyIIYiyH3kqTE9On20SNUYi-vUhJYfKe-XnOCpbQYKt1SDaeNHG0LoGVKKtzGsBtubT2yyKXP3XZpE0bhskGoji3IVpCAsY4dSHkZ7dFt86TuluSgt8pWnxyXygziPV-f7rT8dbwUqomuCCCf0LON3M0QnyvwHbLXt0mrvBxcmMxDkIx3ClX5ZLHNk8Pe1qP7uAlLNDqGwvLA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyDzi8abKtCAjsT2cWqFtihdP1pZWiUP2wjuIfLlA-qCbP08F_QWzOz-DLCFoKYZW85DoXvm2ISQGgOG0NNAorrJphEvE3dpCt1BPU9yVrIXwQnEDzqHAsceq6VzYvGv01RvID1Ow0m5aIpThc_Pe7irv6PJREgFUSNKK_IEObU4X8g-2DyJonVnzsibO3D80MqP-3fgpyUHw?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwKCl6kyQFCD6AuMaRnlf9aA61_-sK65IFxy7kfNez-CD0iXATDsPAqccr6lhqp-SkqhC_5i8Me1B3cbsktSJkLY_7_UcIm1VN8tYvqn3MGpRz466yXGKhCjlBzPNsjyGQ43W-gtjC-C3y1z-S7jGiCfwQWMO2VKgdWIMO-ANa0-E6djvD_JgGrK08hos6jJ7aJmg71dBsHdg?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyIIYiyH3kqTE9On20SNUYi-vUhJYfKe-XnOCpbQYKt1SDaeNHG0LoGVKKtzGsBtubT2yyKXP3XZpE0bhskGoji3IVpCAsY4dSHkZ7dFt86TuluSgt8pWnxyXygziPV-f7rT8dbwUqomuCCCf0LON3M0QnyvwHbLXt0mrvBxcmMxDkIx3ClX5ZLHNk8Pe1qP7uAlLNDqGwvLA?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.486962,41.889252,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>The cryptoporticus and peristyle of a luxurious early 1st c. AD residence were found on the NE Palatine below the present-day Vigna Barberini.<br><br> Extant is one corner of a marble-clad gallery fronted by a colonnade and paved in opus sectile; perhaps this peristyle enclosed a garden. Under the gallery is a cryptoporticus which follows the same orientation. The excavators date the residence to the Julio-Claudian period, while Papi suggests an Augustan date. Other remains of late-Republican/Augustan date recovered nearby may also belong to this residence.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488658,41.889268,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Late-Republican residence on the lower slopes of the N Palatine, located at the intersection of the Nova Via and “Clivus Palatinus”, next to the Domus of M. Aemilius Scaurus.<br><br> Excavations in 1983-85 unearthed remains of a house with an irregular rectangular ground-plan (c. 33 x 25 m) that was entered from the Nova Via . Walls in opus reticulatum and a black-and-white mosaic date the residence to the late 1st c. BC. The house shows evidence of fire damage, perhaps incurred during the Neronian blaze of 64 AD.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488193,41.890726,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a large residence along the Sacra Via between the Domus Publica and the Domus of M. Aemilius Scaurus, dating to the 1st c. BC (area of c. 1000 sq. m); extant are a number of subterranean rooms, probably servants’ quarters, a large garden, and fragmentary remains of the living area on the ground floor. The entrance to the residence, which was from the Sacra Via, was flanked by two tabernae (shops). ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487518,41.891468,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>A well-appointed residence located beneath the NW corner of the platform of the Temple of Venus and Roma.<br><br> Extant remains include a circular hall flanked by four rectangular galleries, each preserving rich marble and mosaic pavements; their design and workmanship suggest a late-Republican or Augustan date. Recently Cn. Domitius Ahenobarbus (cos. 32 AD) has been proposed as one proprietor of this elegant and prominent residence. Previously, these remains were identified with Nero’s Domus Transitoria, but this association has been conclusively rejected.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488882,41.891305,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Large Republican-era residence built on the Velia; excavated and destroyed in 1931 during construction of Via dell’Impero. <br><br>The ruins included a circular room designed as either a water basin or nymphaeum joined to four parallel rectangular rooms. Aligned with the N border of this structure, and following the same orientation, was a cryptoporticus which framed a rectangular garden. The walls of this domus were of opus incertum, which points to a Republican date.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.48958,41.891579,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a Republican residence were unearthed at the E foot of the Velia during the construction of the modern subway system. <br><br>Though destroyed soon after their discovery, archival documents attest to walls of opus quadratum in tufa and a fine mosaic pavement, which probably belonged to a residence. The location of the remains suggests the domus may have fronted onto the eponymous street of the Vicus Compiti Acili).]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.491052,41.890624,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Republican residence on the W slope of the Caelian discovered and destroyed in 1939-40 during construction of Rome’s subway system.<br><br> The house is notable for its Third-Style wall-paintings; also recovered are walls of large tufa blocks, a black-and-white mosaic pavement, and a small marble staircase. These remains offer precious testimony of the luxurious residences that dotted the hill in the Augustan period.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.490767,41.888235,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Extensive remains of house walls have been found on the SW edge of the Aventine plateau, under the Convent of S. Anselmo.<br><br> Based on a inscribed lead pipe, the residence has been associated with a Pactumeia Lucilia (c. 100-150 AD); while specific chronological elements for interpreting the complex walls are lacking, they belong to “numerous phases, from the 1st c. BC to the 3rd, and perhaps 4th, c. AD”. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.478618,41.882106,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Recent excavations on the S slope of the Caelian, outside the Servian Wall, have revealed walls in opus reticulatum and a mosaic pavement.<br><br> The mosaic is dated to the Augustan era through a stylistic comparison to one recovered from the Domus of Augustus on the Palatine. While the remains are too scant to determine reliably the building’s original form, this structure, along with the Domus: Caelius Mons, offers valuable testimony of the presence of luxury architecture on the hill during the Augustan era.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.498415,41.884923,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Augustan-era residence on the E Caelian, located outside the Servian Wall near the “Via Caelimontana”.<br><br> Excavations in 1989-91 unearthed portions of two large structures, perhaps insulae, built in opus reticulatum that faced a row of small shops across a street. The E edifice housed a luxurious, ample residence; remains of a central impluvium (perhaps part of an atrium), two fine pavements, a small courtyard with a fountain, and a large underground cistern suggest that the domus was well-appointed. The W structure, which was separated from the E domus by a small alleyway, has rather scant remains that defy easy interpretation, though one room may have served as a shop. In addition, the NE sides of these two buildings, which are in perfect alignment with the row of shops opposite, suggest the axis of an Augustan-era street, which probably continued E to intersect the “Via Caelimontana”, which lay some 50 m distant; while the excavators hypothetically extend this street across the Caelian.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.49908,41.886576,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Two rooms, probably belonging to a Republican residence on the Caelian, were recovered in 1958 during street work E of the Lateran Basilica.<br><br> The walls were built of opus mixtum, and one room was revetted in marble; both rooms had fine polychrome mosaic floors. No elements allowing for a precise date were recovered, but the building technique of the walls is ascribed to the mid-1st c. BC.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.506546,41.88664,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>This large residence in Via Buonarroti near the Chiesa Cluny was excavated and drawn in the late 19th c. <br><br>On the basis of several walls in opus reticulatum, it is dated to the Augustan period. Due to its location E of the Servian Wall, it seems to have been part of the Horti Maecenatis.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.501631,41.891479,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>The residential quarters of the horti of Maecenas, adjacent to the “Auditorium Maecenatis”, may be identified with the series of rooms discovered under the Cinema Brancaccio on the W side of the Servian Wall. <br><br>An Augustan date is suggested by the use of opus reticulatum, and by sculptural finds which included marble column capitals from the Augustan period. The (partial) ground-plan is taken from 19th-c. excavation drawings. The S and N parts stood on slightly different axes, but both may be integrated with the orientation of the “Auditorium”.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.500311,41.893352,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Substantial remains of a mid-1st c. BC residence stand at the foot of the Viminal near the intersection of Via Cimarra and Via dei Ciancaleoni, c. 3 m below the present ground level.<br><br> Excavated in 1969-70, extant portions of the luxurious home include a peristyle and cryptoporticus, as well as fragmentary wall-paintings and floors of black-and-white mosaic and opus sectile. The building is dated to c. 50 BC by the construction techniques and by comparing its hellenistic plan with other late-Republican structures.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.493031,41.89607,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>This late-Republican house on the Cispian was discovered in 1847 at the corner of Via Sforza and Via dei Quattro Cantoni. Its modern name ‘Casa dei Paesaggi dell’Odissea’ is derived from a fresco featuring the adventures of Odysseus. <br><br>This house is only partially known, but excavations suggest that the Odyssey frieze was part of a portico or cryptoportico, and that the house was developed in terraces on the crest of the hill. The house is dated to the late Republic on the basis of opus reticulatum walls of the same type as the Theatrum Pompeium, and on the basis of a stylistic analysis of the frieze itself.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.496485,41.896153,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of an extremely long-lived residence have been located beneath S. Pudenziana on the Viminalis. <br><br>Though most of the walls date to the 2nd c. AD, a portion in opus reticulatum, as well as substantial remains of late-Republican or Augustan mosaics, suggest a residence of the early-Augustan era. One room preserves indications of an earlier pavement of hydraulic cement with inserted fragments of colored marble; Coarelli dates this floor to the 2nd c. BC, which suggests the residence was continuously inhabited for c. 400 years.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.495832,41.898434,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Several partial opus reticulatum walls of late-Republican or Augustan date abut the Agger near the Porta Viminalis and have been identified as a residence. <br><br>This identification seems based upon the preconception of the Viminalis as a “residential quarter” rather than on the extant physical evidence, which is quite fragmented. Even if one elects not to accept this as a residence, the walls offer valuable testimony for the intrusion of buildings into the Agger by the Augustan era.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.501141,41.902234,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a private residence built in opus reticulatum featuring mosaic pavements, stucco decorations, and wall-paintings, were excavated just W of the Via Salaria, E of the “Sepulcra Salaria”, and N of the *Horti Sallustiani.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.49743,41.911026,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a late-Republican residence on the Alta Semita within the Horti Sallustiani were recovered beneath the basement of the Caserma dei Corazzieri, once part of the convent of S. Susanna. <br><br>Abutting the tufa remains of the Servian Wall were two levels of walls in opus reticulatum; these belonged to a balneum (bath) or nymphaeum. The complex was substantially rebuilt after the Augustan era. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.49353,41.904483,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of a mid-1st c. BC residence standing along the N face of the Viminalis. <br><br>Excavations in 1871 recovered portions of 6 parallel rooms facing SE with walls of opus reticulatum and black-and-white mosaic floors. The construction technique and the mosaic designs are similar to those of the Domus of Livia on the Palatine, and suggest a date of the mid-1st c. BC for the complex. Associated brick walls with Flavian-era wall-paintings reveal that the domus remained active sometime after the Augustan period.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.492941,41.899822,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Augustana (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5F6aKSJEv1jTM-ZAKKtZZ2X5S-8JTqpkergAUj4Qr70rgzuCyL9UEd4z0-BfFNEdwY3OUrrl1r9KrFeCqVJBewAP4205TFoobGTm-rlT_OB1zS8Otef1XxqPBf_QsmiB5mZSTnT98cy7opVFvkituT9x7wNAG20j9DumbsIxI3YCJq_UGVWtE__F7Y6bqf87WwH5tVPLi0mgSpAMKqnsI7KujFh4dV-7S59JGXg7Oj3BaS7QBUuu4PUCuwI0EoyB0_q8hV5zc3uexak1cvIjKm9yYAcz4Ft-2e6wGdSBuRTx_uKxWAW_EdAJxyHSjfV6lEBb30pzGKR5SlVVb5o84ngfmDA-hks7ZMvgfULw04QFbke0K7Djr9LpgCeMT10zL8R6Sc5_bqMicHFaJZmNH7dAlmZe7TF2zwAVzieC9Jp2EgBXvs0uz544PPD_sS7WuKp2wVCN0i1W2MTLWkAGEr_I70tRlyi-dJJ9BlITZrGxTvRNOghiHi3snelYKF3pMdDVuItQHAx0gm4dYGn_FlNYFg06mM822jM6yQSRIzX81c-ZKdSD8zA2eUwDmyBeMG08HjKe3D5nGXWaqqzEosmVmfPIo6WbPfL3FqZFFvCNYcCnk7pGqE20f1Sejt2PJBocm_WCQkgIJ7oA0qbzNGIad_Ye9CvgBFryxf2WrqBd3an3is0kRoTAyG-1ICmMD2_pRhv0vG" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Imperial Palace, Rome.<br><br>The Domus Augustana is the modern name for the so-called domestic wing of the vast Palace of Domitian on the Palatine Hill.  It was the official residence in the city of the emperors from the late 1st to the 3rd c. AD.<br><br>Its name is not directly related to Augustus and should not be confused with the Domus Augusti.<br><br>The central section consists of at least four main parts: the "2nd Peristyle" to the northeast, the central "3rd Peristyle", the courtyard complex and the exedra on the southwest. The two peristyles to the north may have had public functions.<br><br>The 2nd Peristyle garden is partly exposed but little is known of its architecture.<br><br>The 3rd Peristyle was filled almost completely with a huge pool as wide as that of the Domus Flavia and included a seascape perhaps of Greek mythology on an island and with sculpture in the water. Other sources say that a temple was built on the island, namely a temple of Minerva. On its southwest side the walls still stand to a considerable height with several rooms around a semicircular hall.<br><br>"Courtyard" garden of the Domus Augustana looking east to the Severan Baths<br>The great exedra is a long curving arcaded gallery linking the two wings and overlooking the Circus Maximus allowing the emperor to watch the races. It may have been an ornamental façade, perhaps added by Trajan when the seats of the circus were carried up thus far.<br><br>From this curved terrace on the south-west, a large arched opening led into a courtyard, surrounded by a colonnade, behind which were rooms of elaborate plan. It seems that this complex was reserved for the private quarters of the emperor. This hypothesis is reinforced by the fact that the southern section was built a little later and some details suggest that it was not Rabirius who directed the work. The courtyard complex has two levels, the upper containing complex sets of rooms and the lower, 10 m below, consists of a pool with an unusual design of islands.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7voKQEJAV9_ueXOX_sDiAvVEJWivSVzxBLiwGpxlFWsoRGnyLHYpeOhX811eTMUF8NALw42rdSNt4YUbVWd0RSlroBhVKjr4nnODwS26nPa_DVYUoDXkzOA_Hz1yNCr78R7_oH2Jyz5VOWTzfiLXC-uuu4Siy7Ca6JE3TC2ZLvoW81V4VF2VTHeZNTsWRf8peP1hY0nzw6vHU4OQbW5pjwuTgAx_sJej7XGKPSgkG1fCRzqbjnyvc3tlebirVslw__DyKg1_enp6i2rPqbRZDKcL1kgDLKFbD2_MaMimRqiZ5dkIdoOow07E4Mma8mhh7w_u0jJRoiVT5WFFOnXXdyPu-vxsE9kSiNqzpk-JNAhiN3JbRya5sr6KwpFHxvw2ECsJLg9MUMXT6y6SXTTo5HyTK8pmX8bg73eETA0KwH2gzV8m6nU4gb3uAdbDaMA3a_OukziqVjSHvVJXigN9LBAco_g9k2ZFSkRaJpk8fMqG7c6Iybavpz_5kRWzW7D_d29mXIuHpTt20fXYjfgaY5Vh5KD55UHfGKtPXU2shss36SoXtcSqY6b9m3ixsiklHoS7ib3xjzlBZtkdZFzBm9y-e1IKPaYIElZOqYn8SYgeFRv79LZ5UTAkmK2jP4arkIgPLWxLKnJblQOzeLQS4v2pRSPY5VociphwdScxcv_hMTNe7LhCF6CsoKG2dO1UE7iuKnuCVUUg" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6iHYQdMK2X9AfqI79EkH0_ex98EyVribU3xsI-6n1tD7_l6Ol4hBiU-zStraJrdV3GZalWXXjh8rJ7i3R4GMMhk-6YMaPh9pCE73FEcvcg7MBN0BPsW_JudieWiq_rRGoKpGCwkwsXpU_-dzM9ruUa96ZuIYNTCN0A3qHGjJqzH8XluruMihclY0vNYw6xaYrWceugjqrLk-7Keujtg9Zfm5USjsIR3zLyRoowe2MrfICFTHykwRp3ssVFqUJGu3YlYzPeBoNeyD_AztCB5MMeOIkSsJbjG0Zb2jAnfdMEfPrZjr6zoqO9gK68jVk7sHNpXg_B5IPRSAGLsn2-4gt5ydYzzOmHAwFCYn-g0sfU3pG2q8ApX4H4YcKjbFYzlhp6Nr8ya1hUYJ5Vj5QH5VeI7CrvTd-WPMCUHaganbtKxbm0pMrIn8BD6rjEeFQiGICp0nguazAJz7Hn2Y05vyMfaU80tn-xKNHr9foxZoDbN_e8oH4FZfARGbkQBzn7SlByFDoR41HVFuSGkm0kZ9j1rHOB_qJc9m8wjk9CdQjUBholiuFd1UzROopUCtBuyI7_N8_1a883d1-ulQnImggPiYGk89ZsP5rCWEZ-WbxayWP1VBk-ilxcDsjmYKoW1ANB8HiuF_BdXXWY7OoqvKWpN8gxxSA3u5yp2EosXusKL-5h_I41bQ3X1GLUZ9dyjv0ylkn0SWTkmw" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
