      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Augusti (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7Q_bZ5Ww42jX3GuQ4eHs909hKwx3r9i1gPnb2gemA1oLyBiqjIwiRWr_elisx2gtjw3JPmjTkQXY1tyivWAVOkmi5PN56nUjHRUHuF4vKsIHmIBZ-qeSn-6QiPKvekQPdmQ9kcOTmzS_qvDhL17S08DLAHIXdXZO5crwXFI-GTkBkFVZBjyynTHHet7FJyfH68dGlqo_7WJe4owDTIEYKtZbxNGGtBP58s-J2LouMbV7yFanV7Y0KwI1oNqYd_ymRpFLtxg2A72gfxOOmIcoP_J-tzwp8ax7x4XjL2D-4K4btr-OKww6Sn80tGwHRfQAt6XUs6DWBdEbp8kKj49h9pYtQ6do6mIsWWb5KGeZOu-8ESshdahVy8PiYVq8EV06V9OLW9PEKegtKHmPv1E4bLFHbY5vUHxI9CjNJK3M2X3mrsexCfaw5h1yKToi-RiUUXL6iMOCKLdRKC4O4xBHBv7LCpFqhGarZnA7GoOYZxXQIOENBcFff-pEa54cKVNoIPT0BE7DqxchjLFi1KTht-V0FFk4vMxmOqoF_heJu_O17dVfgxa2pqh8mwB1rvwYqP4bFs5YuTpjHJ7RH-gAzkfO9d__BvHMmT1lou1sMR30vWaU4y9BoSIIcLp6k8jhAly4sQS-vffFWuJT9al55HZzB9ciUzNKGTFtzTg85sulh4NyDRHhr2R0kIp2SlZOR6Dm2xCkG5zw" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Palace of Augustus, Rome. <br><br>The house of Augustus stood on the SW Palatine slope which rose above the Circus Maximus between Scalae Caci, the Temple of Apollo, and the house of Livia, from which it was separated by a narrow alley.<br><br> The residence itself occupies an area of c. 350 sq. m. and forms part of a larger complex, over 12,000 sq. m., which included a Latin and a Greek library, porticoes and the dominating Temple of Apollo. <br><br>The house was conceived in such a way as to embrace the temple on the N, W and S, so that the residence of the princeps and his protective god were visually perceived as one and the same. The house was probably one of many occupied by the imperial family on the SW Palatine, and its construction reflects the planning and restructuring of the zone under Augustus who transformed the area into an urban block with streets and passages.<br><br>Augustus, who once lived in a house which belonged to orator C. Licinius Macer Calvus next to the Forum Romanum and later resided in the house of Hortensius, had his Palatine residence built after he returned to Rome from his victory at Naulochos in 36 BC. His domus incorporated late-Republican houses, including those of the families of Q. Hortensius Hortalus and Q. Lutatius Catulus. Suetonius reports that the house of Hortensius was small and modest without marble revetment or mosaics; the residence may have been destroyed during the construction of the Temple of Apollo and the house of Augustus. The house of Catulus, on the other hand, was considered one of the finest houses of its time; Augustus acquired this residence perhaps as early as 36 BC but certainly by 29 BC, when he gave it to Agrippa. Since Augustus’ grandsons Gaius and Lucius were tutored in the atrium of the Catulinae domus, this house almost certainly belonged to their parents Julia and Agrippa and formed part of Augustus’ Palatine residence. The domus has been identified with the remains of a late 2nd- to early 1st c. BC house (extant are a peristyle, early Second-Style frescoes resembling those of the *“Casa dei Grifi”, a fine mosaic pavement, and a basin for fish) discovered immediately SW of the house of Livia and extending toward the Scalae Caci.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4MXAuRD1yOUjjYCRyJVEy_bb8_X8kbP0VlUQitLpFaFoN2H-YVtaDgu05KxVAbUiK6hWy-goROFQVZAzf7tDxm69tAkfBFi5l8UwFl3EyMPNUPjwljqbJhXNOzRFLmjDBwrZ8Bazs6PGiXcdgMstmlwXlJfewXRUF69xT1iuCsc6YmV8HV1Hy5szGr9BX6OW6MaT3iCCrBruCuHWc20E7O66t1yhV5Ke4HcbupkGtpIc3GZLa2WoMfSnxvN3nzmDUtwx2jxxwRyaN-rg37aA0bipXC2XrsZ3Ul0AYPcqs_rv2uJqOqCa45_ZUP05wKFTkw1UNabBBj_HSXX3mkx_fiVR-E56cDXZQMr3yqnMcpW1BLbliGGzkty6VhDmWcMuIz7MKSn15UxhjWia9PPo1d8ktAwH9xKkEPj94c3fzyxy-XtTTfKvulGk5at0tvfdhfg0P0HcQ7HxP5eq2QTnjm_T3UUblCyn1fY_aibua5vOCs_5WtIDAQ_Wkaty2O1KJqq4PjY1tgGgiQNYEpVkUCDAFhM_TDBiAQqF4LOXdWwfqLrXA7XvGqzA0qcxpfQ7iSs7UmLK3vIsQRvLINgfA5d9ThsTyHIl4o85Bwh0llzKGFzpStAgZI3hCJ6Ft0rIymZTseym1IoJrWgl8Uuwc9PLlYyxGzH8W9HdFSFHgCqzAzEQuPtWVwiqW63UuJyaOn9znLVjat" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
