      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Aurea (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx79ksnuDMIO7uhNpPlHhvJJsCTCMRDG1L07AiH7bxPbgPRuzqE9Bcom1mjIWYVU_r5fvzrcvVCfWnkBNUj8kjdjFrPG0NCw82PFhhu62p-tcO3NOcYcPHRO5t8-Rb3rs9vBq3sKhXTMG5oVre0tXsEyYvbZxXtpMe_TR01fxfheh7PIfYR8DhKhivyDG8jzHJPJ6iR62vxow?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Imperial Palace, Rome.<br><br>Construction for the Golden Palace of Nero began after the great fire of 64 AD and was nearly completed before Nero's death in 68 AD, a remarkably short time for such an enormous project.<br><br>The Domus Aurea complex covered parts of the slopes of the Palatine, Oppian and Caelian hills, with a man-made lake in the marshy valley. Its size can only be approximated, as much of it has not been excavated. Some scholars place it at over 300 acres (1.2 km2), while others estimate its size to have been under 100 acres (0.40 km2). Suetonius describes the complex as "ruinously prodigal" as it included groves of trees, pastures with flocks, vineyards and an artificial lake—rus in urbe, "countryside in the city".<br><br>The Golden House was designed as a place of entertainment, as shown by the presence of 300 rooms without any sleeping quarters. The main palace building was on the Esquiline Hill. No kitchens or latrines have been discovered. Contemporary conveniences such as heating pipes have also not been discovered.<br><br>Rooms sheathed in dazzling polished white marble had richly varied floor plans, complete with niches and exedras that concentrated or dispersed the daylight. There were pools in the floors and fountains splashing in the corridors.<br><br>Some of the extravagances of the Domus Aurea had repercussions for the future. The architects designed two of the principal dining rooms to flank an octagonal court, surmounted by a dome with a giant central oculus to let in light. It was an early use of Roman concrete construction. One innovation was destined to have an enormous influence on the art of the future: Nero placed mosaics, previously restricted to floors, in the vaulted ceilings. Only fragments have survived, but that technique was to be copied extensively, eventually ending up as a fundamental feature of Christian art: the apse mosaics that decorate so many churches in Rome, Ravenna, Sicily and Constantinople.<br><br>Celer and Severus, his engineers, also created an ingenious mechanism, cranked by slaves, that made the ceiling underneath the dome revolve like the heavens, while perfume was sprayed and rose petals were dropped on the assembled diners.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx44LyWPo1qRUkt4Guq-QgStYToR4w5k0m85WnY83oaqJYhsDFcb25-uJ7A70Shog5W_d0hmtLjjiCWfd23TawwaeUgNhXLJWQpsV_mwWYY-3HRGsCThZL9NRHb6lkHGy3CK49FDlZs-ZW12hCUFLlAJ0vhuEkYlsHwZRz3VW1E_gtX3qZUgvBP__2jFqViVNuuzWNicvTab7Y?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwG1RUqt4xOrK24eYm6JCcA9kzBV2ArceiK6nqLc7WUZJuu99XvntzmbEebeypJ3rajy1Si4Uiy6y-7ct0o_-PqY_F9505qPLC06PZna9vwXEetVlGLeHx4DPy4fzfKLjlebW_X_EzRYNBxBLKxnxeUtUI91-sfNOSX3rqY2Ho82lHsy8e_YiWdJUzTgWa5eI9aWvmE90gcdxs?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
