      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Gryphorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxzFGXv2YPG4i1QaQ6LJol2GG_Y2sESC7-sWDinewhawA1JfyCcL4xRddiEk7w5Kv2FBUng5MoYfkeBH3l85fPbP6hEWgBO3IL9bGW86-dAyNpD9o6DAn17i3B2vXpI-NfnC9rBS0-gM8coeQtV5MLt0YQi2s2zQhuir2dXYkAcm75n61spDek6aBRkadYMzMD7JEMLGvYpig?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Residence, Rome.<br><br>Remains of the House of the Gryphons, a luxurious Republican residence, are extant on the Palatine under the “Lararium” of the Flavian Palace.<br><br> Commonly known as the “Casa dei Grifi” after its stucco decorations, the house is admired for its fine early Second Style wall-paintings. Originally constructed in opus incertum during the mid-2nd c. BC, the building received various modifications in opus quasi reticulatum afterwards, perhaps as a consequence of the same fire in 111 BC that ravaged the Temple of Magna Mater.<br><br> A number of proprietors have been proposed for the residence, though none persuasively enough to adopt here: Numerius Fabius Pictor (c. 93 BC) and the Fabii in general, Q. Hortensius, a Catiline.<br><br> Only a few subterranean rooms survive the numerous interventions on the site; Nero and the Flavian emperors took a great toll on the residence, but even by the Augustan era the subterranean rooms had been infilled and foundations for a new residence “ruthlessly driven through” their vaults.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxjfSMtKiC_nAtf_1tW6OCKgRbx4MODZnY8zH5lisyT3wOVe890iBRaIBvT0gHQpsQKtZhtI5TbvFO-nT8r4kLO8N1XzToG_STEzPxkJ7ovm0pYmddE7HBOpRfOOeWpcHBUda0pe9b5CFRVB_8HAEvfjwMC0xG-C4LHNA0rPPUNfJMnFiHhRA1EIuTuwPCT3zBjqtc2LaZdjw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1603-A52714</styleUrl>
