      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus Liviae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v6xv_CNR0USYVRx4Dq7h7b5jCTPIDldGieKiw_BHpX8KGERGjJZfA7JHpPzHILGmaxRqzQ-qbcJwZcMjpSOxjlmBpgObX0572bjGZ5OlibES2apzVr8O0oV0vSfdO7B5ZWFvDqtbxZSF8PXeeYQS4PaV6dT0ZkQ2iCONdXE7iCiZoFag0e3Dj-lQEaWnh3ivwUcSrhBRU-SXFp7dAZFFCLLJ8d-ETh5gTRpUPd7oV7u0XH3QsQXgmOGLQ_8gs7g7DEftwXTtVY5YjtHNxMAQjZBXCJmT0OLTGEq6zYMtQ3mrc2w5Ipi_TBMSkzFzw4enqCMF3GyZi93XWvNq4L7M1D-KvYka4JxH-evZFr9KcmNHxLk57sgMvuU80KDSZ7qPWNi1P-OCAf8gbMWMax9KTptgdmpXc2dqaH7wevvEPB9KwRv1rgtLt2rqH2I2R_pG9vcWkN9nDOGq9RMqdLxtVDO8fH5C4iPIjfuubr6O-d68_J9ViQ_BUgymSsXhKl9KlXrh-i1c58QHePAb6Ypv_cxeSXytk4uUF-4Pc7MMIJkYTXQe87PksFKxRlUVGDwrDZhEcv-6hvfYJ9vJ3CgtdqbF7ETI9ZvNFIoOk0rnjD_G_dmms-mCLd1-shRss2KBmjiksHnpvAzXCSOTCKl-91dImJnYyXIvw3V--wBeROfBNWH9WfbU7iPHWm7LRuf4TEi57P6KeNB" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Imperial Palace of Livia, Rome.<br><br>A late-Republican house on the SW Palatine is attributed to Livia based upon a lead pipe inscribed IVLIA AVG[VSTA] discovered on the site and its proximity to the domus of Augustus; this identification is supported by a passage in Suetonius naming the Palatine as the birthplace of Tiberius.<br><br> The residence stood on the slope of the SW Palatine just below the terrace of the Temple of Victoria, and was just N of the Palatine residence of Augustus; since only a narrow alley separated the two houses, these remains have been interpreted as apartments reserved for Livia after her marriage to Augustus.<br><br>Excavations carried out in 1722-24 by the Farnese family, in 1869 by P. Rosa, and in the 1950s by G. Carettoni have clarified the building phases of the residence. Originally, the one-storey residence was built in somewhat irregular reticulate masonry; this phase dates from the first half of the 1st c. BC, perhaps from 75-50 BC. Many associate this phase of the house with Hortensius, which Augustus is known to have incorporated into his own residence. Livia’s house was organized in two sections, each centered on a courtyard. The focal point of the residence in the pre-Augustan era was the subterranean W atrium; opening onto this space were three large rooms: a central triclinium  flanked by two alae; to the SW lay a fourth room, commonly called the tablinum. The use of these terms is purely conventional, and the attributed functions are not supported by archaeological evidence. A stairway and ramped corridor paved with a black-and-white mosaic provided access to this courtyard from the exterior; the original entrance to the house, however, must have been from the E. A long corridor connected the W wing to the E half of the residence, which was located on a higher level; a series of small service rooms lined the S side of this connecting passageway. The E courtyardhad an impluvium and was surrounded by small service rooms.<br><br>After Augustus acquired this section of the Palatine, substantial renovations were made to the house, including the restructuring of its W half, which was decorated with advanced Second-Style wall-paintings; especial attention was paid to the rooms situated on the S and E sides, though the alterations to the E portion of the house remain obscure. The chambers around the E court were rebuilt and a second, perhaps also a third, storey was added above the rooms surrounding the E atrium. The second phase is characterized by typical Augustan dusky-red concrete faced with opus quasi reticulatum. Coarelli proposes that restorations were made to the house after the fire of  3 AD, which damaged other buildings in the region.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4-6IaeJL2wciQpU5oFiuEv_r2_itY4fLLeovTX6KMothHbpD5oexnMvELxzmeUiFmA_MBcnXDtTHxSYCQneGUzrKSFx0F-NxCRIF6YGWkHWPQFYJOpH65XDWY-w8zdK0KMYIQh71s8HEj-IyaWf-7NC5x03-ShQAHy22bkpjK5EBXNXndJkXUYngqKFw-9oX5uq4y1gK8FEZpG1RYRlXpnheuia9jp__-Vzq0syoShypdAnEEPp0pTpMjB_dYkcHLQUNMhnLqcBx3WcK4yEmFdAuUTuhVWDIy95cMjEqhy_sR3jRpBYmLQ2XzLgXPWXyCoFJ0VPgI9AHqZ93JGcHqTBvHN5wdIzt8ImoXySdm-Xhj3-4urRQwt5pKyLmS2t8jmHurgyuHBw8PRvr68tUUMPf5uK9p2p06ou7XS5LlXI4EaPRuh6nbw3KWs4o0-fYcvW-r-Tcb_ydn8DgqDxuFNDEBs7FGbC34F5texJgaONiazDx1d6mnNReQBsbOlAzsfKhCTRQQYpthNrHvw6lncYVJMeqNutjzpzPEWSB_imknCsmH2rDRY1QwWb_w3r6-4_SAHzWch0f1H3VsJYZ0K3d9eOrEUMp6gAMPYQC7B0g9EU4R33C1ZjoTl0qqXd3kbN_VH04RzGbgi957ZUfSxUXU-4bJavRduYX0CUXkSoivKz5cc1R5Q0arskWHcNuyI-4rlas9h3w" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v4psNl9KWTf5c-jVo8V4XgKRryC0KNmQ-U8l8hQk4Gx27Vs8JMf8QGoVDO5R-_xBxu4oBVMG8KTcEAKBQxRxq5QZ3cQSDoCRw8CJZS_XtQNJVXaLt7jHnsba3VTlbtvySohqB_8pBY3bzd510UPDsWAje0aTt8C1bMi3zFpeqYdxNps072oADNAEwniY64MrAbMhRNw-EAZ9lBmiV224abPS8N1CAXa7ZlRgOrYNDaqCGPaLX-AyMGryM7pjWZYb0vVfmyP13v__ajyj4vCB1QtMxqggDst28XgGgaoFO4KQ9GZWpJACV9E2ohvFHTds9RK5vrHQ0qObN7UIo1Z-eHNXbFxnNHwoC6Bj39OgqzGDN7QYk0_tjlVQDR1cBWn27I76_gAb5-EFO_mmW3y-xmvKDiAQvktxl76i6G5hScZqHFh1gNaH-V1RwmEs1LIA6YNs0NNnJ1EM8NwG1UCAMynRw-CW1_TAXZHk_h43WWIMvzosXDbzisneXT_c9IEjYoSdHOgMv4ugdcICdBQ4kxSOUl2vCMVDJkEPkMSTDNO07793XAua7QE0jvgytlNapgFDa0owLWOB59762C12LQ4r4GSOjw97GE3X7LDE1-iM_PD3j4ZbpygLM2uCIbogmAAzQndrLimth23g5wa4_r52WOr9UVCE9XebL0rxpeTWBkUraOD70w2hoLQoHHgDu63WxFjzsJs7A" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
