      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus M. Aemilii Scauri (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residence, Rome.<br><br>M. Aemilius Scaurus (pr. 56 BC) was the mid-1st c. BC proprietor of a magnificent aristocratic residence on the lower Palatine, at the intersection of the Sacra Via and Clivus Palatinus. <br><br>Excavations in 1983-85 unearthed remains of at least one, possibly two houses, in this area. The house dates to the second quarter of the 1st c. BC based upon its building materials and mosaics; its atrium and portions of a series of heated bathing rooms are preserved. Carandini connected the Asconius passage with these remains convincingly to identify them as the domus of M. Aemilius Scaurus. In 53 BC Scaurus sold the house to P. Clodius Pulcher and in 17 BC the famed Hymettan marble columns of its atrium were transferred from the domus to the Theatre of Marcellus. Its owner in the Augustan era remains unknown, though Clodius’ heir P. Claudius Pulcher (cos. 21/22 AD), is one possibility. By the mid-1st c. AD C. Caecina Largus (cos. 43 AD) was proprietor.]]></description>
