      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residences, Rome.<br><br>Opus incertum walls belonging to several houses of the 2nd c. BC have been found inside the Servian Wall on the brow of the Aventine overlooking the Tiber (just NW of S. Sabina). <br><br>Nearby, reticulate walls, of late-Republican or early-Imperial date, reach into the area just outside the wall; to facilitate circulation, four passageways were opened in the wall, and thus the wall’s defensive function had clearly been abandoned.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1604-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.479569,41.884976,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residences, Rome.<br><br>Housing remains of Republican-Augustan date have come to light at the S foot of the Oppian under the Nuova Esattoria to the E of the ludus Magnus in the Colosseum valley.<br><br> Pavements and walls, destroyed in the Neronian fire of 64 AD, were found just S of the modern Via Labicana; a black mosaic pavement with marble insets from a domus of Republican/Augustan date, also destroyed by fire, was found in the S part of the Nuova Esattoria.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1604-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.496012,41.88989,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Domus (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Residences, Rome.<br><br>Remains of two Republican-era houses have been unearthed on the Carinae beneath the nave of S. Pietro in Vincoli. The first was originally built as early as the 3rd c. BC in opus quadratum, and then remodeled during the 1st c. BC when it was fitted with mosaic pavements. Remains of several rooms, an atrium and a cistern survive beneath the nave of the church.<br><br> Adjacent to and just W of this house stood a second residence which was built during the 1st c. BC on a larger and more luxurious scale. This home had walls in opus incertum and opus reticulatum as well as beautiful mosaics and striking opus signinum pavements. Though the two residences share the same orientation, the differences in the building techniques and in the elevation of their pavements suggest that the two were separate structures, rather than a unified complex.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1604-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.493055,41.893849,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Insula (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v76UFR_B31bqeaQgEcqDiMVoeA5bDlpZJJXhb9pfGqEsAvLxMs9LnbJ8s82nYsriK0cqsgpY2KrRCwQq2CBJemi8JUN2GRt_tWvNDy4rGms_Gg_tjAvvI3iaoE3fRktdZ_9ePaFZByqKjYC83ocq3CGd253vn7AaWHkh2fMVtcRKf5E_qsjWd-pTpzlAsJi2hm-yDsIJXnbhRW0WseN2uEEb1gX4uPqHxCc_4kRYCEPYlmVfnfTKf53s4zFchIfk8AEQ2TIIRp3gLdcaR4rgq5rU9dRpYJ2x-GCg6TZR-vnZutM9BPhBsbTYRM5bic85DTe7HURpbYjfuXmlKAnT-4UX6-mNQ4jKlAlEpuKf6GwhnSFMPwOeUj9vuqgBymCj7J16AcPVrifsL4FSYU5LwhP-X5VW_wuBbBHzXxqgqeKDwFIOIVNLsc3EFHObxd2_2cWVqv6FBNeQTrJN7MAXPYBadrwFVk9ePb0iUL7fi91Q23yhGd_JbGQ5oZ8KALIaVdRrV-Z6ywroG-tNKD3y24YeLs05YyyAPf10pEGxx7aI41fmDo9F34ZpYGKc2sUmEyjvxpdGmjepcvUX5R5S6qh2YWM-gwZRE42QrmcqEcII4qPx73Xy8wiVjK9Vk2pZsmzr66fYzP5btmxzg8RLQ8_EIBsnecQfObdlc5esGzUJ16JcK1XGmOAV3QJpYINH36CODD5ihudkA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Apartment block, Rome.<br><br>The Insula dell'Ara Coeli is a rare surviving example of an insula, the kind of apartment blocks where many Roman city dwellers resided. It was built during the 2nd c. AD, and rediscovered, under an old church, when Benito Mussolini initiated a plan for massive urban renewal of Rome's historic Capitoline Hill neighbourhood.<br><br>Four floors remain. The ground floor consisted of shops that faced the surrounding streets, with the owners using ladders to access living quarters immediately above. A mezzanine lay above the shop level. The two remaining floors seem to have been designed for human residence. The third floor seemed to be large, spacious apartments. The fourth floor had a corridor with a series of three room suites leading off of it. Archeologist Andrew Wallace-Hadrill suggested that with a little imagination, these suites are comparable to apartments in which many 21st century Roman families live. Archeologists believe the structure was originally built with at least five stories.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7NVB5tcvIty0PFNUjnPcRVyzvFXjtSrV3v9-xjvTujG29dj72F7DUnZz0XKzoYt0ZED7rAfF7f01G7rI4hocqupEa8UBChwQHeRxgMPWsM2AuQGRr7ug81BZI7HvIkaZTDQEbGTniaMM41Jmhxt7W0gBWHc4dAuo1X94XsuTKfxiJLeU7foytom-R-ehg0q4P2c0u86NJBAUG8sRa-jDotl3PEDl8HK5PQuap_RP0AIYfLTV-7p9QdfG2zHlxJmujufU1KN-f0yRDv-Q5fCyJzidJyvYwDcgA7WxYuVecOptCnvCgSBfdi8ZxUKVpvsk0P4DjSpUQaGvLoAa3nXVKwmcjdK049WO23gm5GminVKv6nO5yK-K5MtyddVP-VRtNbRyvJvAkcF1DI6-qeiaK25_byPfwS7uAgqmcLkGFg6AALOuR8Qa96oznXZ9ZjjgiuEWSTT5mf3Msy9Axjf5LHrwFcp0G7cSffiMjMzCpk4xzmsfC_O09m7oi4WqboWH0kYYqQSTscMGSWHxFrD_UE_AUY6NIgW6JbUKb_kkyj9UD9JOo0bjnmfVJGjH9zrwv0mks-gocEr0h-xc-SwTIWP61ews6fp7pfct3wHU8iEQzPs5qsHM-1FQHY-xPwj_UzfdIlw0FUlTPKK962wiUDPtBiz_o3EnodRdL4sWLuXPVHcR4tB8AtoyYw0YuDp-tt_oIGgWCU" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1604-A52714</styleUrl>
