      <Placemark>
        <name>Horrea Seiana (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzekbUXKmWJcvuTbBPJc_ZZABtL_2wE60x7e5CTeWoCsNWzTtrHzsbF37s7Nipg2P2pDZUjvc8l57_6_5RF1IErM0jxFpgVj2JmHYACSpa5jTkeGLSEIronFM_p3Dx4uVcVSXgFis49K1oug8Fk_125rhresyFQdMAOI_-apQNpLIFzFMKjbnapF3ahJ4PeggMoaWun7ruO-A?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Warehouse of Seius, Rome.<br><br>A prominent Republican warehouse complex in the Emporium area, not far from the Tiber, known only from epigraphic evidence.<br><br> Based on the findspots of a number of inscriptions which were excavated in situ, Lanciani located the horrea just SE of the so-called Porticus Aemilia. Based on the name of the monument, the foundation of the horrea is associated with the Seii family-clan, and specifically with M. Seius, who was an aedile in 74 BC. During building construction in 1911 between Via G. Branca, Via G. Battista Bodoni and Via Beniamino Franklin, remains of possible warehouse structures showing extensive use of opus reticulatum were uncovered. Even if the Horrea Seiana can be associated with these scanty remains, no coherent plan can be deduced without further systematic excavations in the area.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1693-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
