      <Placemark>
        <name>Macellum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Market, Rome.<br><br>Food market rebuilt by M. Fulvius Nobilior in 179 BC near the Forum Romanum and the busy commercial area of the Argiletum.<br><br> The exact location of the Macellum was debated until Morselli and Tortorici identified architectural remains belonging to the structure. It is now securely placed behind the Basilica Paulli in the region later occupied by the Forum Transitorium and Templum Pacis.<br><br> The archaeological remains, however, are too scant to restore fully the Macellum’s ground-plan or elevation. An indication of the structure’s extent is provided by literary accounts which suggest that the macellum stretched from the Argiletum to the Corneta, a small alley NE of the Basilica Paulli.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1685-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.486412,41.892705,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Macellum Liviae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Market of Livia, Rome.<br><br>The Macellum Liviae, a market building on the Esquiline, is mentioned only by late-antique and mediaeval sources, which place it near the Church of S. Vito, the arch of Gallienus , the Nymphaeum Alexandri and S. Eusebio.<br><br> It was thus identified by Lanciani with remains that came to light in Via Principe Amedeo and Via Napoleone III outside the Servian Wall, just N of the Porta Esquilina. This structure consists of a rectangular open court (in which stood a fountain) surrounded by porticoes and shops; the walls date from different periods, but one wall of opus reticulatum may be dated to the 1st c. AD. However, the strong connection between the macellum and Livia in the later literary sources dates the first phase of this structure to the Augustan period. It is not mentioned by Augustan sources. <br><br>The identification of the structure outside the Servian Wall as the Macellum Liviae follows both the mediaeval and modern communis opinio; the remains date mostly from the mid-2nd c. AD, but given the survival of one wall in opus reticulatum, it is possible that later renovations followed the original Augustan ground-plan.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1685-A52714</styleUrl>
