      <Placemark>
        <name>Officinae Minii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Rome.<br><br>Workshops located on the slope of the collis Quirinalis between the Temple of Quirinus and Temple of Flora where mercury ore imported from Spain was refined to produce minium (cinnabar, a red pigment). <br><br>This artisanal process is described in some detail by Pliny. The workshops were probably established during the second half of the 1st c. BC. Though mentioned by both Vitruvius and Pliny, neither author preserves the ancient name of the workshops. Current scholarship typically employs the name “Officinae Minii”.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1686-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487069,41.901734,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Porticus Aemilia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gyko-B6wiZQ5P1YL9eE62lEIgxI17Epz0XrvGPsG9iM35WllIquxJNbd-A6JcmhSqzJO5_S0sxF8yOUpENrqPUYlhF_bRiptoJVooiWfmfF-ROjixct2L9HRSYOu95B_xpnw7mPV1mEtugLcPGNyaJXpPdQl4j7ML1TEusScxHTTw2zg213i0S6isXz4nlF-FwMBtChh2pKsA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Warehouses of Aemilius, Rome.<br><br>The portico was built in 193 BC by aediles Marcus Aemilius Lepidus and Lucius Aemilius Paulus from which the name of the structure and association to the gens Aemilia is derived. It was subsequently rebuilt in 174 BC by censors Quintus Fulvius Flaccus and Aulus Postumius Albinus.<br><br>The development of the Porticus coincided with the rapid growth of the city after the Second Punic War. This increase, in both trade and population, placed stresses on the limited space available in the Forum Boarium and underlined the importance of the river link to Rome's main port at Ostia. The Porticus and the Emporium appear to have worked together to facilitate the unloading, storage, and possibly redistribution of goods and foodstuffs, including imported grain for the corn dole, and appear to have been in continuous operation until the 6th century AD.<br><br>The opus incertum building, in the form of a thin rectangle running parallel to the Tiber, was very large; approximately 487 metres (1,598 ft) long, 60 metres (200 ft) deep and subdivided by 294 pillars in rows of seven. These created a series of 50 aisles, each 8.30 metres (27.2 ft) across. They were roofed by a series of overlapping vaults that rose in line with the slope of the hill, offering protection from the elements while allowing in light and air. The total covered surface was 25,000 square metres (270,000 sq ft).<br><br>The building was set back approximately 90 metres (300 ft) from the Emporium and river access, possibly to mitigate the effects of seasonal flooding. By the Trajanic period this space was filled with additional commercial structures, and the large utilitarian spaces in the Porticus were put to a variety of uses, often modified or subdivided to suit the needs of their users.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwtTsR6Gd0kYcscwLekjpS9_el6_91kvrHewkPWNEXkvMaeB_BM4ilKwXB7kc9l-G3a9PPLccPLIvFn3M5_eKeBRSpW_Exix5Thoo3R9eL3QeuD217Zhk8ziy2OiT_I5QeDirFWdNfiwoT-_qpbRMGYhI3hqw1msOgznihanMweCtmd-NeoDJ6KaXzKPF2bDdMWIu35xXpI6Q?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxU-WyCT-AudtmBfLe4s00O4KC_VmDBzd0o4gliJdQjl5cdEF1dTn7rXisP2mqjpL4VdV6NrfaGoahZ6U_d1KJsCxwYvv9OsoeZAvxMvBTi1LUwG1w7yL3bZQmpHPsKLkE4Cey7yt-6Ji2Jqk9q_m30BbRpsZqMJD8O-tNERYFbnsJOQ4hcaiOag2U6T9O7RrUtURPeVS1FaQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzgyUbJ7tA1Weqqg6SmB_9Rq7PkLb9D1hqKbiWKSqXwoo0Z9bWDm0OpE2NSSHVl-BJx3ynpf-IhwWcjfY6eFHaWaZT_cnenxI1SlOtY9GsedUFlKh0h5CoWuBo5BUpfFsAUH8NZdrcJVlHy43fM0OAu2o_1p1vMTSJ3xLjN2TVJ5EFTBi0YILhjpStb8cunjMJ5mhT_OL7xoA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1693-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gyko-B6wiZQ5P1YL9eE62lEIgxI17Epz0XrvGPsG9iM35WllIquxJNbd-A6JcmhSqzJO5_S0sxF8yOUpENrqPUYlhF_bRiptoJVooiWfmfF-ROjixct2L9HRSYOu95B_xpnw7mPV1mEtugLcPGNyaJXpPdQl4j7ML1TEusScxHTTw2zg213i0S6isXz4nlF-FwMBtChh2pKsA?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GwtTsR6Gd0kYcscwLekjpS9_el6_91kvrHewkPWNEXkvMaeB_BM4ilKwXB7kc9l-G3a9PPLccPLIvFn3M5_eKeBRSpW_Exix5Thoo3R9eL3QeuD217Zhk8ziy2OiT_I5QeDirFWdNfiwoT-_qpbRMGYhI3hqw1msOgznihanMweCtmd-NeoDJ6KaXzKPF2bDdMWIu35xXpI6Q?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxU-WyCT-AudtmBfLe4s00O4KC_VmDBzd0o4gliJdQjl5cdEF1dTn7rXisP2mqjpL4VdV6NrfaGoahZ6U_d1KJsCxwYvv9OsoeZAvxMvBTi1LUwG1w7yL3bZQmpHPsKLkE4Cey7yt-6Ji2Jqk9q_m30BbRpsZqMJD8O-tNERYFbnsJOQ4hcaiOag2U6T9O7RrUtURPeVS1FaQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383 https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzgyUbJ7tA1Weqqg6SmB_9Rq7PkLb9D1hqKbiWKSqXwoo0Z9bWDm0OpE2NSSHVl-BJx3ynpf-IhwWcjfY6eFHaWaZT_cnenxI1SlOtY9GsedUFlKh0h5CoWuBo5BUpfFsAUH8NZdrcJVlHy43fM0OAu2o_1p1vMTSJ3xLjN2TVJ5EFTBi0YILhjpStb8cunjMJ5mhT_OL7xoA?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
