        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.474737,41.880799,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tabernae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shops, Rome.<br><br>A line of tabernae preceded by a portico located just N of the Column of Trajan were brought to light during excavations in 1929-34; they showed signs of having been destroyed to make way for the construction of the Forum of Trajan. The shops were built of brick with a travertine socle, yet at least one wall rested on opus incertum ruins with the same orientation. These shops are difficult to date (c. 1st c. AD), but even if later than 14 AD, the earlier remains indicate a late-Republican structure of the same orientation and perhaps function. The tabernae fronted a paved street, of which a few pavers were recovered. Also nearby were traces of an early structure built of tufa; though oriented along the same street, the ruins are too fragmentary to reconstruct.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.484344,41.896095,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tabernae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shops, Rome.<br><br>Residence on the NW corner of the Palatine dated to the mid-1st c. BC. Excavated just N of an Imperial-era street (often labeled the 'Clivus Victoriae’) within three Hadrianic tabernae were cement and opus reticulatum substructures belonging to a series of rectangular rooms.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.487315,41.890792,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tabernae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shops, Rome.<br><br>A row of spacious tabernae nearly 50 m long stretched along the N slope of the Caelius Mons, just W of the apse of SS. Quattro Coronati.<br><br> Built in opus reticulatum with a limited use of brick, the structure dates to the early-Imperial period. Fragments of walls along the rear of the shops indicate that the building joined other structures to the NW, though the form of these is unknown. Portions of walls in front of the tabernae suggest that they faced onto a second row of shops, and perhaps served as a market building. Note that these tabernae extend into the hypothetical course of Colini’s Vicus Statae Matris, thus excluding the adoption of this 2nd c. AD street plan for the Augustan era .]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.497706,41.887938,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Tabernae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Shops, Rome.<br><br>A row of tabernae over 40 m long was excavated in 1989-91 on the E slope of the Caelian well outside the Servian Wall. The heavy reticulate rear wall of the shops served to retain the hillside. The remains suggest that each shop was c. 5 m wide and 7 to 10 m deep, with the larger ones divided into two rooms. The roofing technique remains unknown, as does the full extent of the row, since it may continue at both ends. These shops, along with an adjacent residence, define the axis of an Augustan-era street.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1684-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.499294,41.886875,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
    </Folder>
    <Folder>
      <name>Roman Army</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Castra Cohortum Urbanae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Urban Cohorts barracks, Rome.<br><br>Three permanent urban cohorts, established by Augustus and reorganized by Tiberius, constituted the body in charge of maintaining public order in the city and were commanded by the praefectus urbi.<br><br>Initially they were perhaps also housed in the Castra praetoria, since in emergencies they were used as auxiliary troops of the praetorian cohorts for the defense of the Emperor. Subsequently, at least from the time of the Emperor Septimius Severus , they had their own barracks. We know from sources that this was in the VII Augustan region (Via Lata), probably in the area of the current Piazza di Spagna .]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.4818524,41.9062309,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Castra Misenatium (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Sailors barracks, Rome.<br><br>A special detachment of the sailors (classiarii) of the military fleet stationed in Miseno was used for the maneuver of the velarium which protected the spectators of the Colosseum from the sun or rain and probably also for the naumachias .<br><br>The detachment was housed in a barracks near the amphitheater, which was probably located at the thermal baths of Trajan , on the slopes of Opium, where an inscription was found relating to an extension of the barracks in the 3rd c. AD.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.4960144,41.8921181,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Castra Nova Equitum Singularium Augusti (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyLhQ3EiSmwEKv9vtOm6-Cxs58KGC1ziQ9YYtUk9m5_NJZKLeFPifDl5ZMFVZNv_Kufu8d8IA379chBZ6LKMHSuhYwegXl1KRyycO3qYOTXYqw_BRpLs8ZRw_Bfogdtqy-hHO8NMsmBgHz0ekxEIx-G1XqLS_Us1yqyWxTlM7IDUWTvG_9j8mSSyUThnsVN82bnzMF-0MvGaw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Imperial Cavalry Guard barracks, Rome.<br><br>The Castra Nova was so-called because it was the newer of the two forts, built to house an enlargement of the cavalry guard. Their previous fort Castra Prioria which lay not far away to the north had been built earlier, possibly under the emperor Domitian, and still housed the original force of 1500 mounted troopers.<br><br>The fort can be precisely dated to 193 AD, at the beginning of the reign of Septimius Severus. The new camp was necessitated by this emperor's increase of the size of his cavalry guard from 1,500 to 2,000 men.<br><br>The site of the camp was always speculated as being beneath the cathedral of St John Lateran (Basilica di San Giovanni in Laterano), but it was identified definitively by excavations between 1934 and 1938 undertaken by Enrico Josi. Josi had obtained permission to explore the area of the basilica’s nave in advance of the construction of a new reinforced concrete floor. Within days it became clear that the remains of the Castra Nova existed in good condition just below the floor level and that the excavation was including a large part of the principia (headquarters) building. The completed excavations were then published by Colini.<br><br>A large two-storey storage building and two barracks were also uncovered, but the greatest interest centered around the principia as its two strong rooms were very well preserved as were several office rooms. On the completion of the investigation the remains were preserved beneath the basilica (along with the remains of the Constantinian cathedral and a Neronian domus) in a spectacular underground archaeological park.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyYWODhuVhe6OT907c_cdP4VknJx5iGKzzeJtIQ64volTkRKJim9Ur_bwaEgBcnnyH1HUWBmyoGqLp-8iQ_e_BC5_w_6UDzg7fTU9cB2-GONX9kCk1ONz09JwgXcSTvGSy_HwpgU27rZM7HDUU7tyarLvwCJM4wNn_DO4JZv-_lYGSD3QgpzH-WOId5A5WlCQyTOc4qauxuIA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GyhImQfwWPpvDjvfTctdGrrzxapqQ2V27rwgz9nP3fp0qIOIHInTb8aTBBqDh_RlfwO8yg0wkHdWZWl1uMnWEVpa2GebIyrdyD-z3i4q9cQKh_2SIhtay75iWjMDyaAswd4pyyaB_jTDxCIaZVnLED-uiVIzNqnl0kxvDE_yuA3_2X05Duw2AD_4cjgephipKv8tpqd3p_rRw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-673AB7</styleUrl>
