      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Castra Peregrina (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Peregrini barracks, Rome.<br><br>he barracks are situated on the Caelian Hill between the Temple of Claudius and Nero's Macellum Magnum. It is centrally located on a hill known for its housing of wealthy Romans. The fourth-century Regionaries list the Castra Peregrina in Regio II.<br><br>The remains of the camp were discovered during digging for the foundations of a convent and hospital and partially excavated from 1904-1909. It can now be found just south-east of the well preserved church Santo Stefano al Monte Celio.<br><br>As the name suggests, the barracks housed a garrison of peregrini; soldiers from the provincial armies detached for special services in Rome. These consisted of a majority of frumentarii[and inscriptions suggest that the Castra Peregrina acted as a central base for the distribution of these men throughout the unarmed provinces (inermes). The frumentarii, who were likely based, and not only housed, at the Castra Peregrina, were initially involved in the provision supply service of Rome, but were later employed as military couriers and members of the secret service.<br><br>Richardson suggests that the brickwork demonstrates an Augustan origin with a 2nd c. AD rebuilding, probably under Severus, and another rebuilding in 3rd c. AD, however the earliest mention of the princeps peregrinorum (camp-commandant) in Rome was during the reign of Trajan. It is also mentioned by Ammianus Marcellinus, showing that it was in use as late as the 4th c. AD.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.4940689,41.8847782,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Castra Peregrina (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx_c8UlOvvMUb6QAa0RRw-J0wj8wSEUJbup4hJenA7R18O2DBqhyJsyk_BNGqRg2czDNKAXoh-m4dgp0Fv4kzMAA3e3XpiYfTvxMEh_DDBbtDWWrNPWXT6_oeyhHtZn2Z5N0j2iL6zunjSmFymT1aO4kxvwiYMx6LUv22YmZ0PRe5rhJf6iZSAQvnMni7zHOlJaWtTrWZF-Qrc?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Peregrini barracks, Rome.<br><br>The Castra were found during the archaeological excavations, conducted in the Amba Aradam-Ipponio station, in November-December 2015 and resumed in March 2016. The area occupies 1750 square meters, and, at ܃9meters deep, part of a castrum was found, dating back to the first half 2nd c. AD, probably at the time of Hadrian. The castrum is composed of a long central corridor with 39 rooms of 16 sq m, 25 of which are quadrangular, with mosaic floors with white/black tesserae and frescoed plaster walls.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1598-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx_c8UlOvvMUb6QAa0RRw-J0wj8wSEUJbup4hJenA7R18O2DBqhyJsyk_BNGqRg2czDNKAXoh-m4dgp0Fv4kzMAA3e3XpiYfTvxMEh_DDBbtDWWrNPWXT6_oeyhHtZn2Z5N0j2iL6zunjSmFymT1aO4kxvwiYMx6LUv22YmZ0PRe5rhJf6iZSAQvnMni7zHOlJaWtTrWZF-Qrc?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
