      <Placemark>
        <name>Navalia (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7vVqFjGkzw4rlNZk4swtNdboJa0h1G_Xa9bieCVvE0HIALKn1sJSQ7z0ut67DrKsFe4ILN0pGhmuXnBuG8NIAVJQkLvA_HeG09gGwrIuliws4v-1IQKlyeAPgfYnJYfMxytoZX0tZLmDDiq9DMGheUV-3h7PhgjEUsdXdfmTnOa6UrZ4lnyRzPlGD9ixZYojMHl3bzHe4aqZlH4Ty9apY2yHKvFYXv-WTc-ah4TML7ErXDgG_6g-iSr-JzzFo4XD-nSEbxUS8i5YYFG-TOZ9Z-0-se2Mng8Gi5fQ_hEuwEOqW2W8BxEG0ta43dwliMJwJVm9nqWKO1EbFoGp_9IA82j19Sf9tbo-h2UNnM91CGQN6Ga1MmeHD5CtmlthH9rOsDLPPGGpJOsbQ2A1y6TSu1Ml0UZ5IhRv3okjx2kuKf4CV6Dy4BiGc5fw7UVsF2oWPc7mzLn7POcwAhkM1Vi-WFNz_jYywGs2bjBu2zzT6WUoREVUWlW5Ypg_-Z0HEW3Uux5bz20vtw9crdOa93BWbAfA85Q8scn0iXbA8FT4_Um6MNlPI7a-TYPdVZhBueVlTEqPheJ8zOXDJ7Xvg4t9q9XRNSjylfkWjAMd_XbBl6ij2p99jVOx2FHDJH3xEut1ISdcafB4rAg_PJH-tHaBSZIBTPeL2tE5O5OBx0sJ_Q-flf1Umyml-BG0SK1S6C9R02cfwqcqb-XA" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Naval arsenal, Rome.<br><br>Naval arsenal, with rows of warship sheds, along the Tiber bank of the SW Campus Martius, stretching opposite the transtiberine prata Quinctia and thus upstream from the Insula Tiberina, but not too far away from the city walls since the porta Navalis was ‘in the vicinity of the Navalia’.<br><br> No remnants are known, unless a sizeable portico of the Republican period belonged, in some way, to the facilities.<br><br> The Navalia complex was already fully established by the 3rd/2nd c. BC, with additional work carried out in the later 2nd c. BC by the prestigious architect Hermodoros of Salamis. The naval arsenal was in regular use when Cato the Younger returned from his Cypriot mission in 56 BC. Its buildings could also be used to keep wild animals until needed in circus games. They were damaged by lightning in 44 BC. The testimonies of Livyand Pliny, both of whom use the navalia as an unqualified point of reference during their lifetimes, contradict the inference of Coarelli that the arsenal had fallen out of use by the mid-1st c. BC. Moreover, Procopius’ 6th-c. AD eyewitness account of a ‘ship of Aeneas’ faithfully preserved in a shipshed ‘in the middle of the city along the Tiber’ seems to apply to the Navalia.<br><br>The Marble Plan fragment from Via Anicia shows part of an edifice situated along the Tiber bank near the Temple of Castor and Pollux in the SW Campus Martius; the structure also appears on the Severan Marble Plan and has been connected with the Navalia.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1623-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value>https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7vVqFjGkzw4rlNZk4swtNdboJa0h1G_Xa9bieCVvE0HIALKn1sJSQ7z0ut67DrKsFe4ILN0pGhmuXnBuG8NIAVJQkLvA_HeG09gGwrIuliws4v-1IQKlyeAPgfYnJYfMxytoZX0tZLmDDiq9DMGheUV-3h7PhgjEUsdXdfmTnOa6UrZ4lnyRzPlGD9ixZYojMHl3bzHe4aqZlH4Ty9apY2yHKvFYXv-WTc-ah4TML7ErXDgG_6g-iSr-JzzFo4XD-nSEbxUS8i5YYFG-TOZ9Z-0-se2Mng8Gi5fQ_hEuwEOqW2W8BxEG0ta43dwliMJwJVm9nqWKO1EbFoGp_9IA82j19Sf9tbo-h2UNnM91CGQN6Ga1MmeHD5CtmlthH9rOsDLPPGGpJOsbQ2A1y6TSu1Ml0UZ5IhRv3okjx2kuKf4CV6Dy4BiGc5fw7UVsF2oWPc7mzLn7POcwAhkM1Vi-WFNz_jYywGs2bjBu2zzT6WUoREVUWlW5Ypg_-Z0HEW3Uux5bz20vtw9crdOa93BWbAfA85Q8scn0iXbA8FT4_Um6MNlPI7a-TYPdVZhBueVlTEqPheJ8zOXDJ7Xvg4t9q9XRNSjylfkWjAMd_XbBl6ij2p99jVOx2FHDJH3xEut1ISdcafB4rAg_PJH-tHaBSZIBTPeL2tE5O5OBx0sJ_Q-flf1Umyml-BG0SK1S6C9R02cfwqcqb-XA</value>
