      <Placemark>
        <name>Necropolis (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Cemetary, Rome.<br><br>Expansive Republican and early-Imperial necropolis on the Collis Hortulorum N of the Horti Sallustiani and clustered around the “Via Sepulcra Salaria”, the cemetery extended as far N as the Sepulcrum: M. Lucilius Paetus, which was easily the largest and most luxurious tomb in the area. <br><br>Many columbaria in opus reticulatum are known to have stood in the area. Buried in these modest tombs were soldiers, merchants, freedmen, and the like. While most of the remains can be dated by their building material to the late-Republican or early-Imperial periods, sporadic depositions continued through the 4th c. AD. <br><br>Despite excavations in the 1960s, evidence for the cemetery is scant, since the ancient remains were largely destroyed by late 19th-c. development. ]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.494395,41.912635,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Pyramis C. Cestii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzGY03kYzgVH6XiY4Jy1NoNuYADB30GCWic_3bU7z_HUPNaYu7eOx73tgbgO371quo8t1rsL17eoYo9G9KtXq3SoF7POa3orn0fc6R7rNqczUusblbYhOHW6OllPh67efG3OeVo_kSpSAjEmvacUhAlyzgLhHsE_y9uP6snjNsXx-mNZfwZtnYIbQkgw6KxImHrplppgrOwEQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Pyramid tomb of C. Cestius, Rome.<br><br>The Egyptianizing pyramid tomb of C. Cestius is located at a prominent intersection of the Via Ostiensis and the street which ran S from the central Aventine; it survives almost in its original state after being incorporated into the 3rd c. AD Aurelian Wall near the Porta Ostiensis.<br><br> One of the inscriptions associated with the tomb provides an identification, and states that the tomb was constructed by Cestius’ heirs according to his will. The inscription on it mentions that Cestius was a praetor, a tribune of the plebs, and a septemvir of the Epulones. The heirs themselves are listed on two inscribed statue bases found nearby; the most prominent figure is Agrippa and his presence allows for a terminus ante quem of 12 BC (the year of his death). A terminus post quem is given by the connection of Cestius with the lex Iulia sumptuaria of 18 BC.<br><br>The sharply pointed shape of the pyramid is strongly reminiscent of the pyramids of Nubia, in particular of the kingdom of Meroë, which had been attacked by Rome in 23 BC. The similarity suggests that Cestius had possibly served in that campaign and perhaps intended the pyramid to serve as a commemoration.<br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzNndC9PQJTd4u3LK6543Bw5mQ7vniIAPbJ4tCx_3-Wgm82jFRXyQLPB57PKNbS-gSb-ig91CWC1qDS0dKTqzWlJHS21Uu_q_5VOaSJJO5iNfZJfuT7qhmU9aec2l8cGpZwggDe7RnkHZVtA4NYv8P_0R-WLw1AkeJG0HnJDlvJZ5FszaE9wnb1OwzAUPTdYhYAIEWgy8v6Rw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br><img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gy3qRn8KHNCUFlNb_9-Vf39VxVKXKHRg-2KAfx6-IXBjG4emI3f2RWFNl8Eiets-BlY4kNmZ221gZhHN4i7Zr30MG7NdCHl7zSa3MuOS1POuP3LyZkx5Akx45iPP5sVLa2VzNAaESSD8xaN0-wrDyrbnHWWig6zyxO5PQXNl9Y9QNoRu2_MDLX4n0h2UAdPcEH-j5C2sT8FtA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
