      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Remoria (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of Remus, Rome.<br><br>A site placed by most ancient sources on the Aventine and connected with Remus’ rival city foundation, specifically as the point where he was thought to have taken auspices and, later, to have been buried.<br><br>Ovid presents what may have been an Augustan-period mainstream view: that Remus took his ill-fated auspices on the mighty Saxum above the Bona Dea sanctuary. It is the location of the Saxum that allows the area of the Remoria to be specified. Since Bona Dea’s sanctuary is listed by the Regionary Catalogues in Regio XII, its location on the Aventine’s SE height (the “Lesser Aventine”) is certain, and the prominent cliff forming its N angle — a strategic point included within the Servian Wall.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-673AB7</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.488977,41.88126,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcra (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tombs, Rome.<br><br>Remains of several tombs were brought to light in the 1920s near the intersection of Viale Regina Margherita and the Via Nomentana. <br><br>Three rectangular tombs, two in opus reticulatum (c. 5.5 x 4.0 m and 4.5 x 3.5 m) and one of opus latericium (c. 3.5 x 3.5 m), were unearthed on the N side of the Via Nomentana. Also in the area was a tomb with two rooms in opus reticulatum, enclosed by a perimeter wall in opus quadratum of tufa blocks. Just S of this were walls of another Republican-era structure and the Aletii monument.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.505921,41.913118,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcra (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tombs, Rome.<br><br>Republican burials found on the S slope of the Caelius Mons, outside the Servian Wall.<br><br> Three tile graves and a tufa sarcophagus, all dated to the Republic, were discovered in virgin earth below remains of the Imperial castra Peregrina during construction of the Convent and Hospital of the Little Company of Mary in 1905-9. Also recovered during the work were two sepulchral inscriptions. These burials offer rare evidence for the course of the Republican defenses across the Caelian.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.497291,41.884661,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcra (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tombs, Rome.<br><br>A group of five Republican tombs on the E heights of the Caelius Mons, located outside the Servian Wall.<br><br> Three Republican chamber tombs were found during the construction of the Hospital of S. Giovanni; the tombs are situated along the “Via Caelimontana”, just outside the line of the Aqua Appia. One of the tombs has an especially well-preserved chamber cut into the living tufa rock (c. 3 x 2.5 m), and the finds associated with it suggest a date in the late 4th to early 3rd c. BC. Fragmentary remains uncovered during the same campaign suggest a fourth tomb stood nearby, while a fifth Republican tomb was found in 1887. These tombs are important, both as rare examples of pre-Imperial building on the Caelius Mons, and as data pertaining to the debated course of the Servian Wall.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.501,41.886616,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb, Rome.<br><br>Huge circular tomb, some 40 m in diameter, situated prominently on the Aventine’s extramural SE lobe in the former Vigna Cavalieri, perhaps not far from a regional road.<br><br> The exterior of the cylinder, whose core of opus caementicium still stands 5.5 m high, was revetted with travertine blocks (of which some fragmented headers are still preserved). A barrel-vaulted radial tunnel, built mainly in opus reticulatum, still provides access to the center tomb where a vaulted ring chamber with 5 niches is constructed around a central pillar; its function may have been to support a cippus or statue on top of the tumulus’ mound. Because of its type and grand size, along with the use of opus reticulatum, the tomb has been attributed to the last years of Republic or first decades of the Principate. No clue exists as to its ancient owner.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.491284,41.876171,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb, Rome.<br><br>This funerary monument, known as the Casa Tonda, was located on the Via Labicana-Praenestina 360 m outside the Porta Esquilina, now beneath Piazza Vittorio Emanuele. <br><br>It consists of a cylinder (diam. 20 m) mounted on a square base (24 m), and is universally dated to the late 1st c. BC. The identity of the tomb’s occupant remains a matter of speculation. Maecenas is a possible candidate, given the proximity of the Horti Maecenatis and the fact that one tradition placed the final resting spot of both Horace and Maecenas ‘on the furthest edges of the Esquiline’.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.505487,41.8942,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb, Rome.<br><br>Republican opus quadratum tomb in tufa and peperino (c. 8 x 8 m) located well outside the Servian Wall on the Via Nomentana.<br><br> The tomb was on the same level as the pre-viaduct remains of the Via Nomentana, though not aligned with the street. The deceased is unknown.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.50252,41.908877,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb, Rome.<br><br>Republican tomb on the W side of the Via Salaria located between the Sepulcrum: M. Aemilius Dulio and the Sepulcrum: Cornelia L. Scipionis f.<br><br> The monument was built in opus quadratum of tufa with a caementicium nucleus (c. 10 x 8 m); a second tomb of the same style may have been built 8 m to the N. Neither tomb owner is known.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.497777,41.911958,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb, Rome.<br><br>Partial remains of a monumental tomb discovered at the NW corner of the Collis Hortulorum on the grounds of the convent of S. Maria del Popolo.<br><br> Foundations of Anio tufa and opus caementicium suggest an Augustan date. These remains have been tentatively associated with the Sepulcrum of the Domitii, but not enough is known about that monument to warrant any firm identification.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.477426,41.91148,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum A. Hirtii (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzUbAb3eKYQbjMifhd23pyMieqMcUWCgUNAOhmVo3aYgfat3nAywfyO7kMyngLKjZNLEidk3KKupfQPThLjQPzXM4iZ8s6HgD56KDTQnyaI_6tI6h7hktmXlQBp4stTnRvdE15qhfROVUxCMc9HJoGI2cuH3F9ZVw7XuXQBSe3we503mkjmRtFrJwHxb0bKyp3Y755S0_D08Q?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of A. Hirtius, Rome.<br><br>Burial monument in the W Campus Martius, built for A. Hirtius, consul in 43 BC and killed during the battle of Mutina the same year. <br><br>A public burial in the Campus Martius was granted to him and his co-consul of 43 BC, C. Vibius Pansa Caetronianus. <br><br>Remains of the tomb precinct have been identified with brick walls (H. 2.65 m) covered by a course of slanted travertine blocks standing beneath Palazzo della Cancelleria at the Euripus. The actual burial chamber inside the precinct has disappeared, perhaps reused as building material (intensive activity of marmorari has been revealed by numerous late-antique inscriptions and the presence in the tomb of fragmentary sculptures from later periods).<br><br> The identification of the structure as the sepulcrum of Hirtius is supported by the presence of three cippi carrying the inscription A. HIRTIVS A. F. which were inserted at the corners of the brick precinct wall. From a passage of Cicero it can be assumed that Hirtius and Pansa found their resting place in the Horti of Mark Antony.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzUbAb3eKYQbjMifhd23pyMieqMcUWCgUNAOhmVo3aYgfat3nAywfyO7kMyngLKjZNLEidk3KKupfQPThLjQPzXM4iZ8s6HgD56KDTQnyaI_6tI6h7hktmXlQBp4stTnRvdE15qhfROVUxCMc9HJoGI2cuH3F9ZVw7XuXQBSe3we503mkjmRtFrJwHxb0bKyp3Y755S0_D08Q?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
