        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.471648,41.897042,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Aletiorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of the Aletii, Rome.<br><br>An inscribed funerary relief of the Aletii family found on the S side of the Via Nomentana. <br><br>The late-Republican travertine relief depicts three male (all labeled C. ALETIVS) and one female (inscribed ALETIA) portrait busts; their hairstyles and garments suggest a Caesarian date. Since this type of sculpture was designed to be encased in the façade of a tomb, it is likely that the monument of this family was located in the vicinity.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.506726,41.913329,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Arruntiorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of the Arruntii, Rome.<br><br>This tomb structure lay N of the ”Via Labicana-Praenestina” and W of the Porta Maggiore, between Via Giolitti and Via di Porta Maggiore; it consisted of three rooms in opus reticulatum with a vaulted stuccoed roof.<br><br> These walls are dated to the Augustan period. Discovered in 1733 and demolished c. 1900, its ground-plan is known from various 18th-c. drawings. An inscription over the entrance drawn by Piranesi records that it was constructed by L. Arruntius (cos. 6 AD) to house the funerary urns of his dependents and descendants.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.513262,41.892291,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum C. Cornelii Galli (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of C. Cornelius Gallus, Rome.<br><br>Early Imperial pyramidal tomb located on the E side of the Tiber, at the intersection of the post-Augustan Via Cornelia and the Via Triumphalis.<br><br> In the Middle Ages, it remained a major topographic landmark, known as the “Romuli sepulcrum”. The actual tomb was destroyed by Pope Alexander VI in 1499 and only the foundations are now preserved.<br><br>The clear Egyptian typology of this tomb easily allows for a date in the Augustan period, a time when an Egyptianizing style strongly influenced architecture in Rome. Most probably its owner was Cornelius Gallus, the first prefect of Egypt. This identification is strengthened by the name of the later street passing nearby, Via Cornelia. A similar pyramidal tomb, the Sepulcrum: C. Cestius, still stands on the S periphery of the city.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.463934,41.902214,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum C. Publii Bibuli (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v5JuKL1Sgqpn-J78xu882w-wQs9aokJr6KJ8PzsQ-McDlG0gPgUtLQSynSsVxCnA6OZomk0kwrxXjBtqJMKAyxn1tdlIBlV7VTxs9blho3u-Q7NWGl7DjTG-UYq6DaURJzOL5O5TZkgb-l9DHi7n04OxHuCVWUZJr36klA94N1CcLOXG_qQtaRN08K8HvPiocMzP5hAEmfQDa8fRxCcZ4D8FXq8JtQLOzrRlFQMbYRkqqar2Jd0UTtPnbBwVmeleFBMeW8IrSOsGRbbRLidTyP6_Khf5x0kd4qAwoigHRLEtRUO8fuRe_JL4Vy5JpVllSiYo7jwCXQKwDSSpzy-N_SV245t2sB4IU3_-moBTOvFz2_Nq3lHb_rsTMvD43_5z4rTzBGxWZYWJptvmjINOerGdos3VxapKy-MR3YcdFRA6T1pHf7O3C1b4wUeGevA3Gzl8cN7-aibAlIKkhRoCHFMLRgWHfZVNU_0mIpy-eEeR0qAsKUEuiU7_NvQ_kTkLAnpKt52Lra9lv32cI8ZX2pkLoDzwuoV9_ayOuHOqhKEbDmks2cFKPluTJhO00EzDrHu6Zb3rUWf7MkhpoB_b8eKPsWqya98UmEB3Et4M7ygmmIFIExZm-piS_-BsFeaeQQRVY_x9PmuanjWrmG88TMPyyVlh7bLJ7-iS9qyLgF2xUxZYSihcOO6yLU0LMXJ9m9gVrpKYXpZGQ" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of C. Publius Bibulus, Rome.<br><br>The façade of this tomb still stands in situ at the foot of the Arx next to the Victor Emanuel Monument; originally, it faced onto the Via Flaminia. <br><br>The structure is dated to the early 1st c. BC, but prosopographical considerations suggest that Bibulus himself lived in the 2nd c. BC and that this is a rebuilding of an earlier tomb. The façade remains, but the rear of the tomb is not known.<br><br><img src="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/umsh/AN6v0v7Edk9loFzxKoN3MAJbigEyeQJ-oVYT8pHSIXHWX-DisLWGQxQ1ew1ZT0lQF8M9oCl2mTW8se3DKX5Ll1fI5hxuMO-OApYB9UraUMvygo85j-hTld26781vkVMHLowt_hJEEJmR42wa29xf3q4EG7EGAKGobdW7xRHxd4t3IRhLI_jSiHCEUE2KzwJoL81VEShtiRVP3piXTIWQZTHgOWrOck4PR80qkz2ufS0daWXZhWCe5eBcoKuPXFDnaTsykBBYnv0y8fCuLIO8DBMfdAcQV2lH9_zyTXJdKZAGxL0dTRZCzol3sy_Vsn5bNgrnW9mn4YtKXDOFTUxXwznq1MGLQXyJrPkWFbu_ued7DlW3zXS6uknlX8xV5ga63wwB2PLq0F_tr75juIS_2ccBkdrG9LBQ_Siz2Xfap4lpXLKK9FThMEk5OIGFIEJ8dH_EWaPss3O2Ej60qkO4cvk5DRQCfO9kGT6XZRbsOlR9r6eX8TLGvKJcKoympw54mSXTRAcMqOI0k7RhEC7-c4RCmiFL4EZZoXgBNVlYGZkOaJIRzdISbABZ2vE1V0HppYBpUuOOuPMWd7Do5QLvwpFLbL-62SoxaLFkPaYu2_Fnjiy2-ME3kTB-neP2KpBaI_0kiHXMfeLCf5UIR2TqiD0aGbUbWHlIHvWDvarWzynoB4By4nm5OdLQ-nJakj1LjI6918SyWAUjRSae8g" height="200" width="auto" />]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
