      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum C. Sulpicii Platorini (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxLbpnHHYTwZzScFeoKQtSccBvd_MKMa07D2PiZuV48kFE3jxeGEwXiAlNyxdZAQT9Of4mSyPYfC4LCCI9_s1sVHkOWhT1Va9yxUzjKz_N-odeHPqPWj40cf6aLDp_srtaGhj23IxZRBZbisz_V4ZIAF3QxzlkaTBAkfQhxQTHAQsUnSwy9hKpGkwbeGaPVz2gqt-sgMWJ2hgc?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of C. Sulpicius Platorinus, Rome<br><br>The monumental clan tomb of the Sulpicii Platorini at the Transtiberine head of the Pons Agrippae, immediately inside the Aurelian Wall, probably of late-Augustan date.<br><br> Discovered and excavated during the construction of the modern Tiber embankments in 188, it was reconstructed in the Museo Nazionale Romano in 1911. It was a squarish structure, 7.44 x 7.12 m in plan, raised on a travertine podium.<br><br>Surviving are several inscriptions associated with the burials accommodated within the tomb, which date from the Augustan to Flavian periods. The building inscription over the entrance mentions C. Sulpicius Platorinus, who is widely accepted as the triumvir monetalis of 18 BC, although the construction of the tomb could also be attributed to a grandson.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GxLbpnHHYTwZzScFeoKQtSccBvd_MKMa07D2PiZuV48kFE3jxeGEwXiAlNyxdZAQT9Of4mSyPYfC4LCCI9_s1sVHkOWhT1Va9yxUzjKz_N-odeHPqPWj40cf6aLDp_srtaGhj23IxZRBZbisz_V4ZIAF3QxzlkaTBAkfQhxQTHAQsUnSwy9hKpGkwbeGaPVz2gqt-sgMWJ2hgc?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
