      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum C. Vibii Pansae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzoxT4q9bHlo32AA27v13iZvseruBTY4DUn-3hY1l645upebIDrDkKJ_no1jrtBBhgQYbM7AsjLttsMfDm2m7Y9os6QkH4qKUVGspvC4P42FKika6kTE1W1VY3-VJEhPRjtlRH9T7OlQmsARN1cK38i-5dvjoO6p_gP-O9EpBVb2m99yI2yqEu4QOq35b9Si3X7lQtHQcZzCA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of C. Vibius Pansa, Rome.<br><br>Funerary monument dedicated to C. Vibius Pansa Cetronianus, consul in 43 BC with A. Hirtius, who died the same year. <br><br>The actual structure has not been found, but a large travertine slab, carrying the dedicatory inscription EX S(ENATVS) C(ONSVLTO) C. VIBIO C.F. CETRONIAN(O) CO(N)S(VLI) was uncovered beneath Palazzo Italiani in the W Campus Martius. The proximity of the Sepulcrum: A. Hirtius to the findspot of Pansa’s dedicatory inscription indicates that the latter had his resting place nearby and shared the honour of a public burial.<br><br>Whether the structure was located inside or outside the pomerium in the Augustan period has been speculated. An inscription dedicated by C. Vibius T.f., a nephew of C. Vibius Pansa Cetronianus, once suggested that he may have built a monumental funerary structure for his wife and brother-in-law in the Campus Martius.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzoxT4q9bHlo32AA27v13iZvseruBTY4DUn-3hY1l645upebIDrDkKJ_no1jrtBBhgQYbM7AsjLttsMfDm2m7Y9os6QkH4qKUVGspvC4P42FKika6kTE1W1VY3-VJEhPRjtlRH9T7OlQmsARN1cK38i-5dvjoO6p_gP-O9EpBVb2m99yI2yqEu4QOq35b9Si3X7lQtHQcZzCA?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
