      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Hilarii Fusci (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx0hNDOu1PlWxPKhqx59r-5tsLBe_8oZFGhTb-idGmK_6vuNXDJEQFD82_qDsWPKRg8Z67XlATN0Eechwegv700JE_K2OBTi8ijFLTvDhM64qUWPJ_THk4ki2p7jAZRIbC2uc7guUP_BJhCCRNBEfm7Ybms7okZ_1FF2TbUcju3VyDRq-p4Xm7P3YUhGx-JYkgscm51pIjYaA?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of Hilarius Fuscus, Rome.<br><br>The Tomb of Hilarus Fuscus is a funerary monument, located near the fourth mile of the Appian Way.<br><br> The tomb was restored by Luigi Canina in the mid-1800s. An inscription bearing the names of those represented on the masonry disappeared in the period between 1978 and 1998. The sculptures are copies: the originals are now in the National Museum of the Baths of Diocletian.<br><br>The architecture of the tomb and the analysis of figures (particularly the hairstyle of the women) suggests the tomb was built at end of the Republican period and the beginning of the Imperial age (c. 30 BC).]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gx0hNDOu1PlWxPKhqx59r-5tsLBe_8oZFGhTb-idGmK_6vuNXDJEQFD82_qDsWPKRg8Z67XlATN0Eechwegv700JE_K2OBTi8ijFLTvDhM64qUWPJ_THk4ki2p7jAZRIbC2uc7guUP_BJhCCRNBEfm7Ybms7okZ_1FF2TbUcju3VyDRq-p4Xm7P3YUhGx-JYkgscm51pIjYaA?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
