      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Horatiorum et Curiatiorum (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzH74SoTsC1Ix81qNbovlDdfXf610S_d11WBX5kLy24dWm1srrlDX3PI-rvFbsvboaj_ENMrAMzxXpclzL8Rg3E_pGka_MOLWF1YmcicUc4s3YkzbpeatjVMpRhpGno7751kkhOrTIs1JNOthYhsDQ53H8MsjIOr_t9D6_MuksrRphLeau6dj7TmIJmmThviB8_bwRx5vi2xQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of the Horatii and Curiatii, Rome.<br><br>The mausoleum is situated on the southern boundary of the residential area, on the edge of Appian Way and is a unique example for its architecture reminding us of the Etruscan cinerary urns in Volterra.<br><br>Local history associates the mausoleum to the warriors Horatii and Curiatii, but historically speaking is more likely to be a reconstruction of the tomb of Arunte (an Etruscan leader) member of the ancient Arruntia family who had properties in the area. In fact, it was built in the first half of the 1st c. BC approximately six centuries after the famous battle of the Horatii and Curiatii.<br><br>It was built in huge peperino blocks; on a square base there are four truncated cones on the corners (only two remained intact) and probably a bigger fifth one stood on the drum-shaped central base containing the burial chamber of which only ruins can be found.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_GzH74SoTsC1Ix81qNbovlDdfXf610S_d11WBX5kLy24dWm1srrlDX3PI-rvFbsvboaj_ENMrAMzxXpclzL8Rg3E_pGka_MOLWF1YmcicUc4s3YkzbpeatjVMpRhpGno7751kkhOrTIs1JNOthYhsDQ53H8MsjIOr_t9D6_MuksrRphLeau6dj7TmIJmmThviB8_bwRx5vi2xQ?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
          </Data>
        </ExtendedData>
        <Point>
