      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum M. Vipsanii Agrippae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of M. Vipsanius Agrippa, Rome<br><br>Tomb M. Vipsanius Agrippa built for himself in the Campus Martius before his ashes were interred in the Mausoleum of Augustus.<br><br> Recently the tomb, which must have functioned as a cenotaph, has been tentatively associated with the remains of 5 parallel walls of peperino, each pierced by an opening 1.9 m wide, that were found in the 1880s under Via Vittorio Emanuele in the area of the Vallicella.<br><br> A large fragment (L. 2.8 m) of a monumental altar pulvinus (bolster) was found within the trench. Recent reexamination of the building materials and techniques suggests a late-Republican or early-Augustan date for the structure, while the sculptural decoration of the pulvinus can be ascribed to a relatively narrow chronological period early in the Augustan principate. However, the pulvinus was not necessarily found in its original location; as no other altar remains were found, it may have been taken to the site as spolia, since several late-Imperial workshops where marble fragments were re-carved are known in the W Campus Martius. Given the uncertainties surrounding the pulvinus, the unusual building remains, and the absence of corroborative testimony to support that of Dio Cassius, it is ot certain that this is his tomb.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-673AB7</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.467746,41.898163,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Octaviae M. f. (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[Tomb of Octavia, Rome.<br><br>Marble tomb belonging to an Octavia, daughter of a Marcus Octavius (perhaps M. Octavius Cn. f.), and probably wife to an Appius, dating to the mid-1st c. BC.<br><br> The tomb was discovered in 1616 near the intersection of Via di Porta Pinciana and Via Sistina; after it was drawn and recorded by Grimaldi, the tomb was dismantled for spolia.<br><br> Grimaldi sketched a flat-roofed, rectangular façade preceded by a shallow, distyle, Ionic porch whose architrave bore the epitaph. The tomb probably had a rectangular plan with the inscription and entrance on opposite sides; Grimaldi did not include dimensions, so the tomb’s depth has been estimated (c. 5 m) based on comparison with similar tombs.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>
            12.486231,41.904647,0
          </coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
      <Placemark>
        <name>Sepulcrum Priscillae (Roma, Italia)</name>
        <description><![CDATA[<img src="https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gxf17ZsGNhjO4Kpw7LrrBH6ZsHg9y_6eIzMqSgjvmgUK6BMP-LG3xFJ92tJcVsqlbtrmgx9WAaNazQrBz7oMNywdjurqsjM_iJiDjkBFFutoOykm35j-w2yVPCyTLo5B3tPAz8BylkZ-R-D7Q9C3_ECzU3riAxsbHQi5g5eX-bDipSPUyF2Ml8miZmmu13FWyAWmZm2yEi0pw?authuser=0&fife=s16383" height="200" width="auto" /><br><br>Tomb of Priscilla, Rome.<br><br>The Tomb of Priscilla is a monumental tomb erected in the 1st c. AD on the Appian Way, situated opposite the Church of Domine Quo Vadis.<br><br>The Tomb belonged to Priscilla, wife of Titus Flavius Abascanto, a freedman of the emperor Domitian.<br><br>On a quadrangular base, covered with travertine blocks (opus quadratum), there were in the past two superimposed cylindrical towers, built in opus mixtum and opus reticulatum, the upper one with 13 niches designed to house statues of the dead.<br><br>The funerary cell covered by a barrel vault is entered via a corridor, currently accessible from the basement of one of the houses that are adjacent to the monument. The cell was coated within by blocks of travertine and included three niches designed to house sarcophagi.<br><br>The tomb was used in the 11th century and later as a fortress. It belonged to the counts of Tusculum, and later the Caetani.]]></description>
        <styleUrl>#icon-1556-A52714</styleUrl>
        <ExtendedData>
          <Data name="gx_media_links">
            <value><![CDATA[https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3AE5a_Gxf17ZsGNhjO4Kpw7LrrBH6ZsHg9y_6eIzMqSgjvmgUK6BMP-LG3xFJ92tJcVsqlbtrmgx9WAaNazQrBz7oMNywdjurqsjM_iJiDjkBFFutoOykm35j-w2yVPCyTLo5B3tPAz8BylkZ-R-D7Q9C3_ECzU3riAxsbHQi5g5eX-bDipSPUyF2Ml8miZmmu13FWyAWmZm2yEi0pw?authuser=0&fife=s16383]]></value>
